@@ websocket
value: A WebSocket, a Server-Sent Events (SSE) stream and a long-polling request are three ways to let a server tell a client something the moment it happens. Plain HTTP cannot do this: the client asks, the server answers, and the conversation ends. If a chat message arrives for you at 10:00:01, the server has no way to send it until your browser asks again. These three techniques fix that, each with a different trade between simplicity, cost and capability.
Long polling is the oldest. The client sends a normal request, and the server does not answer until it has something to say (or until a timeout of roughly 30 to 60 seconds). The client then immediately sends the next request. SSE keeps one HTTP response open forever and writes small text events into it. WebSocket is different in kind: after one HTTP handshake the connection stops being HTTP and becomes a full-duplex channel (both sides can send at any time) carrying small framed messages, text or binary, with 2 to 14 bytes of header per message instead of hundreds of bytes of HTTP headers.
For the layer above, these are the transport of everything "live": chat, presence, collaborative editing, dashboards, notifications, multiplayer games, and the token-by-token streaming of AI answers (which mostly uses SSE). The layers below make it possible. TCP gives an ordered, reliable byte stream that stays open. HTTP gives the handshake and the proxy-friendly framing that lets the upgrade pass through the web's infrastructure. Sockets and I/O models (epoll, io_uring) let one server process hold hundreds of thousands of idle connections. Queueing theory (Little's law) tells you how many connections you will hold and why.
terms: Full-duplex — both sides can send at the same time, without waiting for a turn.
Upgrade handshake — an HTTP request with the header `Upgrade: websocket`; the server answers `101 Switching Protocols` and the same TCP connection then speaks the WebSocket protocol.
Frame — the unit of the WebSocket protocol: a small header (opcode, length, optional mask) followed by the payload.
Opcode — a 4-bit field in the frame header that says what the frame is: text, binary, close, ping, pong or continuation.
Masking — the client XORs every payload with a random 4-byte key before sending; it exists to protect proxies, not to give secrecy.
Ping/pong — control frames used as a heartbeat to find out whether the other side is still alive.
Server-Sent Events (SSE) — a one-way text stream from server to browser over one long-lived HTTP response with the media type `text/event-stream`.
Long polling — a request that the server holds open until data is ready, after which the client immediately asks again.
Idle timeout — the time after which a proxy or load balancer closes a connection that carries no bytes.
history: Before 2005, a web page could only learn about new data by asking. Around 2006 developers began to hold requests open to simulate push. Alex Russell named the family "Comet" in 2006, and servers such as Jetty and the Bayeux protocol (CometD) packaged it. It worked everywhere but was wasteful: every message cost a full set of HTTP headers, and the client had to re-establish the request after each event.
In 2008 Ian Hickson and others began a "TCPConnection" idea in the HTML5 draft, which became WebSocket. The goal was a raw-socket-like channel for browsers that could still pass through HTTP proxies and firewalls. Early drafts had security flaws, and in 2010 researchers showed that a malicious script could trick some caching proxies into storing forged content. That is why the final protocol forces clients to mask their frames. The IETF published it as RFC 6455 in December 2011, and the W3C defined the browser API. In parallel, the WHATWG defined Server-Sent Events and the `EventSource` API, with automatic reconnection built into the browser.
Later work fixed deployment problems. RFC 7692 (2015) added the permessage-deflate compression extension. HTTP/2 multiplexes many streams over one connection and has no Upgrade mechanism, so RFC 8441 (2018) defined WebSocket over a single HTTP/2 stream using extended CONNECT, and RFC 9220 (2022) did the same for HTTP/3. Libraries such as Socket.IO (2010) wrapped WebSocket with automatic fallback to long polling. Since about 2023, SSE has had a second life as the standard transport for streaming large language model output.
how: Start with long polling, because it shows the problem. The client sends `GET /updates?cursor=57`. The server checks whether there are events after cursor 57. If yes, it answers at once. If not, it parks the request (it keeps the socket and a small state object) and answers when an event arrives or after about 30 seconds with an empty response. The client reads the answer and immediately sends the next request with the new cursor. Every event costs a full HTTP request and response with headers (often 500 to 1,000 bytes), and between a response and the next request there is a short gap where an event could be missed unless the server keeps a log and the cursor lets the client catch up. That cursor is the essential trick: long polling is only correct if events are stored and numbered.
SSE improves on this by never closing the response. The server replies once with `Content-Type: text/event-stream` and then writes text blocks such as `id: 58`, `event: price`, `data: {"p": 101.5}` followed by a blank line. The browser's `EventSource` parses them and fires callbacks. If the connection drops, the browser reconnects by itself after the `retry:` interval and sends the header `Last-Event-ID: 58`, so the server can resume from the right place. This is long polling's cursor, built into the standard. SSE is plain HTTP, so it passes through proxies, works with ordinary cookies and authentication, and over HTTP/2 shares one TCP connection with other requests. Its limits: it only goes server to client, it carries only UTF-8 text, and over HTTP/1.1 browsers allow only about 6 connections per origin, so several tabs can exhaust the budget.
WebSocket starts as HTTP. The client sends a `GET` with `Upgrade: websocket`, `Connection: Upgrade`, `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13` and `Sec-WebSocket-Key`, which is 16 random bytes in base64. The server computes `base64(SHA-1(key + "258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11"))` and returns it in `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` with status `101 Switching Protocols`. The fixed GUID and the hash prove that the server really understands WebSocket and is not a confused HTTP server echoing headers. This is not authentication and not encryption; it is a protocol check. After the 101 response, the bytes on the TCP connection are WebSocket frames, and HTTP is no longer involved. This is the contribution of HTTP: it provides a handshake that every proxy and firewall already understands. TCP then provides the reliable, ordered byte stream underneath, so WebSocket does not need its own retransmission or ordering.
A frame starts with 2 bytes. The first byte holds the FIN bit (this is the last fragment of the message), three reserved bits (used by extensions such as compression), and a 4-bit opcode: `0x1` text, `0x2` binary, `0x8` close, `0x9` ping, `0xA` pong, `0x0` continuation. The second byte holds the MASK bit and a 7-bit length. Lengths up to 125 fit there; the value 126 means a 16-bit length follows, and 127 means a 64-bit length follows. Frames from client to server must carry a 4-byte masking key and the payload is XORed with it; frames from server to client are not masked. So the header is 2 bytes for a small server message and 6 bytes for a small client message, against hundreds of bytes for HTTP. A message can be split over several frames, and control frames (ping, pong, close) may be inserted between them. Closing is a two-step exchange: one side sends a close frame with a status code (1000 normal, 1001 going away, 1011 server error), the other replies with a close frame, and then TCP is closed.
Holding thousands of such connections is the job of sockets and I/O models. A thread per connection costs memory (a thread stack is commonly 1 MB of address space, and the scheduler suffers with tens of thousands of threads). Servers instead use readiness-based I/O: the kernel's `epoll` (Linux) tells one event-loop thread which of its 100,000 sockets have data or free buffer space, and the thread handles only those. An idle connection then costs only the kernel socket buffers and a small user-space object, often 10 to 50 KB in total. In 2012 WhatsApp reported more than 2 million connections on one Erlang server. This is the contribution of the I/O layer: it makes "mostly idle" connections cheap.
Queueing theory gives the sizing rule. Little's law says the average number of connections in the system is \( L = \lambda W \): the arrival rate of connections times the average time each stays open. If 10,000 users per minute (about 167 per second) join a live page and each stays 30 minutes (1,800 seconds), the server holds about 300,000 connections at steady state. With long polling, the same users create a continuous stream of requests instead: with a 30-second hold, 300,000 users generate about 10,000 requests per second even when nothing happens. This is why a WebSocket or SSE server is sized by connection count and memory, and a polling server by request rate and CPU.
Finally, the connection must stay alive. NATs, load balancers and proxies delete state for silent connections: an AWS Application Load Balancer closes idle connections after 60 seconds by default, and NGINX closes a proxied connection after 60 seconds without data (`proxy_read_timeout`). So both sides send heartbeats, ping frames in WebSocket or comment lines (`: keepalive`) in SSE, typically every 20 to 30 seconds. A missing pong tells the sender that the TCP connection is half-open, which TCP itself may not notice for minutes or hours.
diagram:
  LONG POLLING            SSE                    WEBSOCKET
  client    server        client    server       client      server
    |--GET--->|             |--GET--->|            |--GET Upgrade->|
    |  (wait) |             |<--200---|            |<--101---------|
    |<--data--|             |<-event--|            |==== frames ===|
    |--GET--->|             |<-event--|            |--msg--------->|
    |  (wait) |             |<-event--|            |<-msg----------|
    |<--data--|             |   ...   |            |<-msg----------|
    |--GET--->|            one response,           |--ping-------->|
  one request per          server -> client        |<-pong---------|
  event, headers each      only, text, auto        both directions,
  time                     reconnect + id          text or binary

  WebSocket frame header (client -> server, payload < 126 bytes)
  byte 0:  [FIN][RSV1][RSV2][RSV3][ opcode: 4 bits ]
  byte 1:  [MASK=1][ payload length: 7 bits ]
  bytes 2-5: masking key (4 random bytes)
  bytes 6..: payload XOR key
example: Let us size a live-score page and compare the three transports. Assume 200,000 viewers at the same time, and the score changes on average once every 10 seconds, with each update about 100 bytes of useful data.
Long polling. Each update is delivered by a response, and then each client sends a new request. A request plus response with headers is about 800 bytes, so one update costs about 800 bytes per client. Across 200,000 clients, one update means 200,000 responses and 200,000 new requests, about 160 MB of traffic, and each new request goes through the load balancer, authentication and routing again. If the server also times out every 30 seconds, an idle page still sends 200,000 / 30 ≈ 6,700 requests per second.
SSE. The event is written as `data: {...}` plus a blank line, about 110 bytes. One update to 200,000 clients is about 22 MB. There are no new requests and no repeated headers. Idle traffic is just a comment line of about 15 bytes every 25 seconds, so roughly 200,000 / 25 = 8,000 tiny writes per second in total, with no request parsing.
WebSocket. A server text frame has a 2-byte header, so the update is about 102 bytes, about 20 MB in total. Idle traffic is a ping (2 bytes) every 25 seconds and a pong reply (6 bytes). The big difference from SSE is not the bytes here but what the clients can send back: if this were a chat, clients could submit messages on the same connection without a new request.
Now memory. At 30 KB per connection, 200,000 connections need about 6 GB across the fleet. If one server instance comfortably holds 50,000 connections, you need at least 4 instances, and you add a margin so that when one dies, the other three can absorb its 50,000 clients without exceeding their own limit. Here is the part that causes outages: when one instance restarts, 50,000 clients reconnect within seconds. Each new connection costs a TLS handshake (roughly 1 to 2 ms of CPU) plus an authentication check, so 50,000 reconnects is 50 to 100 CPU-seconds of work. With 4 cores that is 12 to 25 seconds of saturation. Clients must therefore wait a random time before reconnecting, for example `delay = random(0, min(30 s, 1 s × 2^attempt))`. This is exponential backoff with full jitter, and it spreads the 50,000 reconnects over about 10 to 30 seconds.
intuition: Long polling is knocking on a door repeatedly with the question already in your hand; SSE is a radio the server keeps tuned to you; WebSocket is a telephone call.
Each step removes a repeated cost. Polling repeats the whole question for each event. SSE keeps the channel open but allows one direction only, which is all that many pages need, and so it keeps all the simplicity and tooling of HTTP. WebSocket removes the HTTP request/response shape entirely and keeps just a framed pipe, at the price of owning everything HTTP used to give you: caching, status codes, per-request authentication, simple load balancing and easy retries.
The design is natural because TCP already provides what a persistent channel needs: an ordered reliable stream that stays open as long as nobody closes it. The web only lacked a way to use it that proxies would accept. The Upgrade handshake is a polite request that every HTTP intermediary can read, and after it succeeds the intermediary sees an opaque stream. Masking exists because early intermediaries did not understand that and could be fooled.
mistakes: People often think WebSocket is "faster" than HTTP because it uses a different network. In fact it runs on the same TCP connection type, with the same latency and the same congestion control. It saves header bytes, handshake round trips for later messages, and the delay of waiting for the next request. For one large download or a single request, it is no faster at all.
People often think the masking of client frames is a form of encryption. In fact the key is sent in the same frame, so anyone can unmask it. Masking only makes the bytes on the wire unpredictable to scripts that try to forge HTTP traffic through a proxy. Confidentiality comes from using `wss://`, which is WebSocket over TLS.
People often think a connection that is open is a connection that works. In fact a TCP connection can be dead for minutes without either side knowing, for example after a phone switches from Wi-Fi to mobile data or a NAT table entry expires. Only application-level heartbeats detect this, and `onclose` may never fire.
People often think WebSocket is always the right choice for real-time features. In fact if data flows only from server to client (feeds, notifications, progress bars, LLM token streams), SSE is simpler: it reconnects by itself, resumes with `Last-Event-ID`, works with ordinary HTTP authentication and caching rules, and needs no special proxy support.
People often think the browser WebSocket API handles slow receivers. In fact the classic API has no backpressure: `send()` just queues data in memory, and you must watch `bufferedAmount` yourself. A fast server can fill a slow client's memory; a slow client can also make the server buffer without limit unless the server enforces a cap.
friction: State is the main pain. A stateless HTTP service can be scaled by adding instances behind any load balancer. A WebSocket server holds per-connection state, so a message for user U must reach the one instance that holds U's connection. You need either a routing layer (a table of user to instance, or consistent hashing of users) or a publish/subscribe bus (for example Redis or Kafka) so that every instance hears every event and delivers it to its own local connections. Deployments are also harder: restarting an instance disconnects everyone on it, so you drain gradually and rely on client reconnection with backoff and jitter.
Delivery guarantees are weaker than they look. A message written to a socket may be lost if the connection dies before the client reads it. WebSocket itself has no acknowledgement, no replay and no ordering beyond the single connection. Reliable systems add sequence numbers per stream and a resume protocol: the client reconnects with the last number it saw, and the server replays from a log. This is exactly what SSE's `Last-Event-ID` standardises, and what you must rebuild on top of WebSocket.
Security needs care. Browsers do not apply the same-origin policy to WebSocket handshakes, so a malicious page can open a socket to your server using the victim's cookies (Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking). The server must check the `Origin` header or use a token that the attacker cannot guess. The browser API also cannot set custom headers such as `Authorization`, so tokens usually travel in the first message or in the query string, where they may be logged. Every long-lived socket also needs limits: maximum message size, maximum messages per second per connection, and a limit on total connections per user, otherwise a single client can consume memory.
Infrastructure adds limits of its own. Some corporate proxies still block or cut upgraded connections. Load balancers have idle timeouts and sometimes maximum connection lifetimes. Mobile networks silently drop connections when radios sleep, and frequent keepalives cost battery. And a very large fleet pays for connection memory even when nothing is happening, which is why the cost of an idle connection (a few KB to tens of KB) and the number of connections per instance are the two numbers to track.
tradeoffs: Choose SSE when data flows mostly from server to client, messages are text (JSON), and you want the simplest operations: it is plain HTTP, reconnects and resumes itself, and works with ordinary authentication and observability. Choose WebSocket when the client also sends frequent small messages (chat, games, collaborative cursors, trading orders), when you need binary payloads, or when per-message overhead must be minimal. You give up HTTP semantics and tooling with WebSocket; you give up the reverse channel with SSE (the client can still send ordinary POST requests, which is often enough).
Choose long polling when you must work behind hostile networks or very old infrastructure, when updates are rare (once a minute or less), or when you want a fallback path. Its cost is request rate and header overhead, and a cursor-based log on the server. For rare events, a request per event is cheaper than keeping a million connections open.
Choose plain periodic polling (a request every 30 to 60 seconds) when freshness of a minute is acceptable and responses can be cached at a CDN. A million clients polling a cacheable URL costs the CDN almost nothing, while a million open WebSockets cost real memory on your own servers. Choose a push connection when latency of seconds matters or when polling would mostly return "no change".
Choose a managed push service (mobile push notifications, or a managed WebSocket gateway) when clients are mobile and often offline, or when you do not want to run a stateful fleet. You give up control of delivery details and pay per message or per connection minute; you gain not having to operate reconnect storms and connection draining yourself.
practice: Put a numbered event log behind any push channel, and let clients resume from the last number they saw. Then the transport can fail and reconnect freely without losing events, whichever one you use. Send heartbeats every 20 to 30 seconds (below the smallest idle timeout in the path), and close a connection after two missed pongs.
Always reconnect with exponential backoff and jitter, for example a base of 1 second, a cap of 30 to 60 seconds, and a random delay between 0 and the current cap. Add a server-side connection rate limit and a "go away, retry in N seconds" signal for overload. Before a deployment, drain: stop accepting new connections, tell clients to reconnect elsewhere, and spread the closes over a minute or more.
Measure per-connection memory in your own server under load, and set operating limits from it. On Linux, raise the file descriptor limit (`ulimit -n`) and watch `epoll`-based server settings; in NGINX, set `proxy_http_version 1.1`, pass the `Upgrade` and `Connection` headers, and raise `proxy_read_timeout` above your heartbeat interval. Check the `Origin` header on every handshake, authenticate with a short-lived token, and cap message size and rate.
For fan-out, keep each instance's local connections in a map from user or topic to connection, and use a message bus to deliver each event to the instances that care. Compress only if payloads are large and repetitive: permessage-deflate keeps a compression context per connection (tens of KB), which costs memory at a million connections.
quiz: What are the main steps of the WebSocket handshake, and what does `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` prove? || The client sends an HTTP GET with `Upgrade: websocket` and a random `Sec-WebSocket-Key`; the server replies `101 Switching Protocols` with `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`, the base64 SHA-1 of the key plus a fixed GUID. It proves the server understands WebSocket, not that the client is authenticated.
Why must WebSocket clients mask their frames, even though masking gives no secrecy? || Early attacks used scripts to make proxies cache forged responses by sending crafted bytes that looked like HTTP. A fresh random mask per frame means the script cannot control the bytes on the wire, so the proxy cannot be tricked. The key travels in the frame, so it is not encryption.
Why does SSE resume correctly after a dropped connection but a bare WebSocket does not? || SSE events carry an `id`, and the browser reconnects with `Last-Event-ID`, so the server can replay what was missed. WebSocket has no built-in sequence numbers or replay; you must add them.
10,000 users per minute join a page and stay 30 minutes on average. How many connections does the server hold in steady state? || By Little's law, L = λW = (10,000 / 60 per second) × 1,800 seconds = 300,000 connections.
A team wants to stream LLM tokens to a browser chat box and send the user's prompt in a normal POST. Which transport and why? || SSE. Data flows server to client, it is text, the prompt is a single request, and SSE gives automatic reconnection, plain HTTP authentication and simple proxying. WebSocket adds a stateful protocol without a benefit.
interview: How would you scale a WebSocket chat service from one server to a fleet serving 5 million concurrent users? || Each instance holds about 50,000 to 200,000 connections, so you need dozens to a hundred instances. A user's connection is on one instance, so deliver messages through a publish/subscribe bus or a user-to-instance routing table. Keep connection state small, store messages in a durable log with sequence numbers, and let clients resume. Plan for draining and reconnect storms.
What happens to your fleet when a load balancer or one third of the instances restart, and how do you prevent a reconnect storm? || All affected clients reconnect at once, each costing a TLS handshake and an auth check, which can saturate CPU and cause a cascade. Prevent it with client-side exponential backoff with full jitter, server-side connection rate limiting with a retry-after signal, gradual draining during deploys, and enough spare capacity to absorb one failure domain.
How do you detect and handle a half-open connection? || TCP may not report a dead peer for a long time. Send application heartbeats (ping every 20 to 30 seconds) and close after a missed pong or two. Also set TCP keepalive as a backstop. The client should treat a missing pong as a disconnect and reconnect with its last sequence number.
Why not use WebSocket for everything, including normal request/response APIs? || You lose HTTP caching, status codes, standard authentication per request, easy retries, and simple load balancing and observability, and you must build request correlation, backpressure and error handling yourself. Request/response over HTTP is better supported by all tooling. Use WebSocket for the push or bidirectional part only.
How does WebSocket work through HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and why does that matter? || HTTP/2 has no Upgrade mechanism, so RFC 8441 uses extended CONNECT with a `:protocol` pseudo-header to run a WebSocket on one HTTP/2 stream (RFC 9220 does it for HTTP/3). Many sockets then share one TCP or QUIC connection, which reduces connection count, but a lost packet can stall all streams on TCP (head-of-line blocking).
ref: spec | RFC 6455: The WebSocket Protocol | I. Fette & A. Melnikov, IETF, 2011 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455
ref: spec | RFC 8441: Bootstrapping WebSockets with HTTP/2 | P. McManus, IETF, 2018 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8441
ref: spec | RFC 6202: Known Issues and Best Practices for the Use of Long Polling and Streaming in Bidirectional HTTP | S. Loreto et al., IETF, 2011 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6202
ref: spec | HTML Living Standard: Server-sent events | WHATWG | https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html
ref: docs | WebSockets API | MDN Web Docs | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API
ref: blog | 1 million is so 2011 (WhatsApp connections per server) | WhatsApp Engineering blog, 2012 |
ref: book | High Performance Browser Networking, ch. 16-17 (Server-Sent Events, WebSocket) | Ilya Grigorik, 2013 | https://hpbn.co
@@ callstack
value: The call stack is the region of memory where a running program keeps the bookkeeping of its function calls. Every time a function is called, a block of memory called a frame is placed on the stack. It holds the address to return to, some saved register values, and the function's local variables. When the function returns, its frame is removed and execution continues where the caller left off. Because frames are added and removed in last-in, first-out order, one register (the stack pointer) and a few instructions are enough to manage the whole thing, at a cost of about one or two CPU cycles per push or pop.
A calling convention (part of the ABI, the Application Binary Interface) is the agreement that makes this work between pieces of code that were compiled separately, by different compilers, or even in different languages. It fixes which registers carry the arguments, which register carries the result, which registers a function must preserve, how the stack is aligned, and who cleans up. Without it, your Python interpreter (compiled in C) could not call a C library, Rust could not call C, and the operating system could not call your program's `main`.
For the layer above, this is what makes recursion, modular code, shared libraries and system calls possible. Interpreters and virtual machines build their own frame stacks on the same idea. Coroutines and closures exist because the plain stack discipline is too strict for them: a frame must sometimes outlive its call. Security work (stack smashing, canaries, shadow stacks, control-flow integrity) is about protecting the one dangerous fact of this design, which is that the return address sits in ordinary writable memory. The layers below provide the pieces: the instruction set (L3) supplies `call`, `ret`, `push`, `pop` and the stack-pointer register; the stored-program computer (L3) explains why a return address is just a number in memory; and induction (L4) is the proof method that explains why recursion with one frame per call is correct and terminates.
terms: Stack frame — the block of stack memory belonging to one active function call: return address, saved registers, locals and sometimes spilled arguments.
Stack pointer (SP) — the register that holds the address of the top of the stack; on x86-64 it is `rsp`, on ARM64 it is `sp`.
Frame pointer (FP) — an optional register (`rbp` on x86-64, `x29` on ARM64) that points to a fixed place in the current frame so that locals have constant offsets and debuggers can walk the stack.
Return address — the address of the instruction after the call; `ret` jumps to it.
Caller-saved and callee-saved registers — registers a callee may freely overwrite (caller-saved) and registers it must restore before returning (callee-saved).
ABI — Application Binary Interface; the binary-level contract of data layout, calling convention and system-call interface for one platform.
Prologue and epilogue — the first and last instructions of a function that create and tear down its frame.
Stack overflow — the stack grows past its limit, usually hitting an unmapped guard page and crashing the process.
Tail call — a call that is the last action of a function, which can reuse the current frame instead of adding a new one.
history: In the earliest machines, a subroutine was a block of code that ended by jumping back to a fixed place. In 1949, David Wheeler's EDSAC solved the return problem by having the caller store the return address into the subroutine's last instruction, using self-modifying code (the "Wheeler jump"). This allowed subroutine libraries, but not recursion: a function that calls itself would overwrite its own stored return address.
The stack idea arrived in the late 1950s. Friedrich Bauer and Klaus Samelson described the "stack principle" (Kellerprinzip) for evaluating expressions in 1957. The ALGOL 60 report (1960) allowed recursive procedures, and compiler writers such as Dijkstra implemented them with a run-time stack, one frame per activation. Burroughs machines such as the B5000 (1961) put the stack into hardware. By the 1970s, the push/pop/call/return pattern was a standard part of instruction sets (the PDP-11, then the x86 family).
Standardised conventions came later. On 32-bit x86, many conventions competed (cdecl, stdcall, fastcall), and mixing them was a common source of crashes. With 64-bit processors, platforms chose one rule each: the System V AMD64 ABI for Linux, BSD and macOS, a different Microsoft x64 convention for Windows, and the Arm Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS64) for ARM. Security research changed the picture too: Aleph One's "Smashing the Stack for Fun and Profit" (1996) explained how to overwrite return addresses, which led to stack canaries (StackGuard, 1998), non-executable stacks, ASLR, and in the 2020s hardware shadow stacks (Intel CET). WebAssembly (2017) went further and hid its call stack entirely from the program.
how: When a function `f` calls `g`, four things happen, and the calling convention decides each. First, the caller puts the arguments where the callee expects them. In the System V AMD64 ABI the first six integer or pointer arguments go in `rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9` and floating-point arguments in `xmm0` to `xmm7`; further arguments go on the stack. ARM64 uses `x0` to `x7`. Using registers instead of memory makes calls fast, since most functions have few arguments. Second, the caller executes `call g`. This one instruction pushes the address of the next instruction onto the stack (decreasing `rsp` by 8) and sets the program counter to `g`. This is where the stored-program idea matters: the return address is only a 64-bit number written into memory, in the same memory as data, and the CPU trusts whatever number `ret` later pops. ARM64 instead puts the return address in the link register `x30`, and the callee stores it on the stack only if it will make calls of its own.
Third, the callee builds its frame. The classic prologue is `push rbp; mov rbp, rsp; sub rsp, N`: save the caller's frame pointer, make `rbp` point at the new frame, and reserve N bytes for locals and spills. Optimising compilers often skip the frame pointer and address locals relative to `rsp`. The callee must preserve the callee-saved registers (`rbx, rbp, r12` to `r15` on System V): if it wants to use one, it saves the old value in its frame and restores it before returning. Any caller-saved register (`rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8` to `r11`) may be destroyed by the call, so the caller saves what it still needs. This split is a design compromise: if every register were callee-saved, tiny functions would waste time saving registers they never touch; if every register were caller-saved, a loop around a call would reload everything each time.
Fourth, the return. The result goes in `rax` (and `rdx` for a second word; floating-point results in `xmm0`). The epilogue `leave; ret` sets `rsp = rbp`, pops the saved `rbp`, and then `ret` pops the return address into the program counter. The caller continues at the next instruction. The stack pointer is back where it was before the call, which is the invariant that makes everything work: each call leaves the stack exactly as it found it. The convention also demands alignment: on x86-64 System V, `rsp` must be a multiple of 16 just before the `call` instruction, because SSE instructions on stack data need aligned addresses. The ABI even allows leaf functions (functions that call nothing) to use the 128 bytes below `rsp` (the "red zone") without moving the pointer.
Recursion works because every activation has its own frame. Here is where induction enters. To prove that `fact(n)` returns n!, we assume the call `fact(n-1)` returns (n−1)! (the induction hypothesis), and the frame of `fact(n)` still holds its own `n` unchanged, since the inner call used a different frame. So the result is n × (n−1)! = n!. The base case `n <= 1` stops the descent. Termination is also an induction argument: the argument shrinks on every call, so after n calls the base case is reached. The same reasoning also tells you the memory cost: depth n means n frames, so space is proportional to n.
Each thread has its own stack, and the operating system gives it a fixed maximum size: 8 MB for the main thread on typical Linux (`ulimit -s`), often 1 MB for Java threads (`-Xss`) and for Windows threads. Just below the limit is a guard page that is not mapped. Running into it raises a fault, which the program sees as a stack overflow (a segmentation fault in C, a `StackOverflowError` in Java). Runtimes with huge numbers of tasks make a different choice: Go starts each goroutine with a small stack of a few kilobytes and grows it by copying it to a larger block when it fills, which is possible because the runtime knows exactly where the pointers are.
Debuggers, profilers and exception handlers need to walk the stack: to find the caller's frame from the current one. With frame pointers, this is a simple linked list: each saved `rbp` points to the previous one. Without frame pointers, the compiler records the layout of each function in tables (DWARF call frame information, in `.eh_frame`), and the unwinder reads them. Profilers that sample stacks thousands of times a second are fast with frame pointers and slow with DWARF unwinding, which is why some Linux distributions (Fedora 38 and Ubuntu 24.04) turned frame pointers back on by default for system libraries.
diagram:
  high addresses
  +--------------------------+
  | caller's frame (main)    |
  +--------------------------+
  | arg 7+ (if any)          |  pushed by the caller
  | return address           |  pushed by CALL        <- 8 bytes
  | saved rbp                |  pushed by prologue    <- rbp points here
  | local variables, spills  |
  | saved callee-saved regs  |  <- rsp points here (top)
  +--------------------------+
  low addresses   (stack grows downward)

  call f(a, b):          callee g, after "push rbp; mov rbp, rsp":
    mov rdi, a             [rbp+8]  return address
    mov rsi, b             [rbp]    caller's saved rbp
    call f                 [rbp-8]  first local
    ; result in rax
example: Take a recursive factorial in C and follow the stack with numbers.
```c
long fact(long n) { return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); }
```
An unoptimised x86-64 compiler produces roughly this code (Intel syntax):
```text
fact:
  push rbp            ; save caller's frame pointer
  mov  rbp, rsp       ; new frame pointer
  sub  rsp, 16        ; room for locals
  mov  [rbp-8], rdi   ; spill argument n
  cmp  qword [rbp-8], 1
  jg   .recurse
  mov  eax, 1         ; base case: return 1
  jmp  .done
.recurse:
  mov  rax, [rbp-8]
  sub  rax, 1
  mov  rdi, rax       ; argument n-1 in rdi
  call fact           ; pushes return address
  imul rax, [rbp-8]   ; n * result
.done:
  leave               ; rsp = rbp; pop rbp
  ret                 ; pop return address into the PC
```
Now call `fact(3)` from `main`. The argument 3 is placed in `rdi`, and `call` pushes 8 bytes. The prologue pushes 8 more bytes (saved `rbp`) and subtracts 16 for locals: this frame costs 8 + 8 + 16 = 32 bytes. It spills n = 3 and, because 3 > 1, it computes 2, puts it in `rdi` and calls `fact` again. Now there are two frames (64 bytes). The call with n = 2 does the same and creates a third frame for n = 1 (96 bytes). With n = 1 the comparison `1 > 1` is false, so `eax = 1`, `leave` and `ret` remove the frame of n = 1 and return 1. The frame of n = 2 resumes after its `call`, computes 2 × 1 = 2 and returns. The frame of n = 3 computes 3 × 2 = 6. At the deepest point three frames existed, and each kept its own copy of n, which is why the multiplication used the right value.
Now ask how deep we can go. With an 8 MB stack and 32 bytes per frame, the limit is 8 × 1,048,576 / 32 = 262,144 calls. So `fact(1000000)` crashes with a stack overflow in this unoptimised build, even though the computation is trivial. An optimising compiler would use smaller frames (often 16 bytes or none), but the principle is the same: depth times frame size must fit in the stack. The fix for deep recursion is a loop, an explicit heap-allocated stack, or a tail-recursive form that the compiler turns into a jump (`gcc -O2` does this for tail calls in many cases, but the C standard does not promise it).
intuition: A stack of plates in a canteen: you only ever put a plate on top and take a plate from the top, and so the plate you take is always the one you last put down.
Function calls nest in exactly the same way: the function that was called last is the first one that must finish. A last-in, first-out structure is therefore the natural fit, and it is cheap, because the only moving part is one pointer. Giving every call a private frame gives each activation its own copy of its variables without any bookkeeping about names or lifetimes. The calling convention is then a small contract on top: it says where to put things so that independent teams, compilers and languages can write code that fits together.
The weakness is also visible in the picture. The return address sits in the same memory as the data of the function, a few bytes away from an array that may be overflowed. This is the stored-program idea in action, where code addresses are ordinary values, and it is the origin of an entire class of attacks.
mistakes: People often think variables live in the function's code or in a fixed place. In fact local variables of each call live in that call's frame, so two active calls of the same function have different copies. That is what makes recursion and threads work.
People often think `return` hands the value back through the stack. In fact the result usually travels in a register (`rax`, or `x0` on ARM64). Only large structures are returned through memory, via a hidden pointer that the caller passes as the first argument.
People often think a stack overflow means the machine ran out of memory. In fact the stack has a fixed limit (8 MB on typical Linux for the main thread) that is much smaller than the RAM. You can run out of stack with gigabytes of free memory. Deep recursion on user input (a nested JSON document, a long linked list) is a classic way to crash a service, or to be attacked.
People often think all calling conventions are the same, or that the language defines them. In fact the ABI is defined per platform and operating system, not per language. Linux x86-64 uses `rdi` first, Windows x64 uses `rcx` and requires 32 bytes of "shadow space", and 32-bit x86 passes arguments on the stack. Calling a function with the wrong convention does not fail to compile in foreign-function interfaces; it silently reads wrong registers.
People often think tail-call optimisation is automatic everywhere. In fact it is guaranteed in Scheme and some functional languages, optional in C and C++ compilers, and absent in Java, Python and (in general) Rust. Do not rely on it for unbounded recursion unless your language promises it.
friction: The main problem is security. A buffer overflow of a local array can overwrite the saved return address, and then `ret` jumps wherever the attacker wants (control hijacking). Mitigations each have a cost: stack canaries add a check to each protected function; non-executable stacks stop injected code but not return-oriented programming, which reuses existing code; shadow stacks keep a second, protected copy of return addresses in hardware, which needs OS and compiler support and does not cover every unusual control flow; pointer authentication on ARM signs return addresses.
Fixed stack size is the second pain. A thread's stack must be reserved in advance, so many threads with large stacks waste address space (10,000 threads × 1 MB = 10 GB of reserved addresses), and small stacks overflow on deep recursion. This is the reason why languages with huge numbers of tasks (Go, Erlang, Rust async, Kotlin coroutines) avoid one contiguous native stack per task. Growing stacks by copying requires exact pointer maps; segmented stacks (used by early Go) suffered "hot split" slowdowns at segment boundaries.
The register conventions create waste too. Saving and restoring callee-saved registers, spilling arguments and keeping 16-byte alignment cost cycles in small functions, which is why inlining is one of the most valuable optimisations: it removes the call entirely. At the same time, the ABI freezes decisions for decades: changing the layout of a standard type or the register assignment breaks every compiled library. This is the reason C++ standard library implementations have kept some inefficient layouts for years.
Finally, stack traces are fragile. Without frame pointers or unwind tables, a profiler cannot see the callers. Optimisations (inlining, tail calls) delete frames that you expected to see in the trace, so a stack trace may not match the source structure.
tradeoffs: Choose to keep frame pointers (compile with `-fno-omit-frame-pointer`) when you run production profilers and tracing tools that sample stacks often: the cost is one register (about 1 to 2 percent speed on x86-64 in typical measurements) and the gain is cheap, reliable stack walking. Choose to omit them when registers are scarce and the code is a tight compute kernel, and rely on DWARF unwinding for the rare debugging need.
Choose recursion when the problem is naturally recursive (trees, divide and conquer) and the depth is bounded by a small number such as log n. Choose an explicit stack on the heap, or a loop, when depth can depend on untrusted input or reach hundreds of thousands: you give up some code clarity and gain control over memory and a clean error instead of a crash.
Choose one big native stack per thread when you have tens or hundreds of threads and want the simplest, fastest calls. Choose small growable stacks (goroutines) or heap-allocated state machines (async/await) when you need hundreds of thousands of tasks: you pay for stack-copy complexity, or for the "coloured function" rewrite of the code, in exchange for memory of a few KB per task.
Choose security hardening (canaries, shadow stacks, CFI) by default for code that parses untrusted input; it costs low single-digit percent in time. For a closed numeric kernel with no untrusted input, you may reasonably leave it out.
practice: Know your stack limits. Check `ulimit -s` for the main thread, and set explicit sizes for threads you create (`-Xss` in Java, `pthread_attr_setstacksize` in C). In a service, bound the depth of any recursion that follows user data (parsers, JSON/Protobuf decoders, regular expressions with nesting, expression evaluators) with a depth counter, and reject deeper input.
When you read a crash or a profile, read the stack from the top: the first frame is where the program was, the next is its caller, and so on. Build production binaries with frame pointers or with symbols and `.eh_frame` available, and keep debug symbols for offline symbolication. In `perf`, `--call-graph fp` uses frame pointers and `--call-graph dwarf` uses DWARF at a much higher cost.
To see conventions directly, compile small C functions with `gcc -O1 -S -masm=intel` or use the Compiler Explorer (godbolt.org) and watch which registers are used. Try a function with seven arguments, a function that returns a 24-byte struct, and a function that calls another, and note where each value goes.
When you write foreign-function code (JNI, Python ctypes, Rust `extern "C"`, cgo), declare the exact types and calling convention, because the compiler cannot check them across the boundary. Never return pointers to locals, which die with the frame: the classic dangling pointer. And in languages with closures or async, remember that anything that must outlive the call has to move to the heap.
quiz: What does the `call` instruction do, and what does `ret` do? || `call` pushes the address of the next instruction onto the stack and jumps to the target; `ret` pops that address into the program counter. The matching pair returns control to the caller.
Why do calling conventions split registers into caller-saved and callee-saved sets? || It balances cost. If everything were callee-saved, small functions would save registers they never touch. If everything were caller-saved, loops around calls would reload all values every time. The split lets each side pay only for what it uses.
Why can a recursive function with a correct base case still crash on large input? || Each call adds a frame, so memory use grows with depth. The stack has a fixed limit (8 MB typical for a Linux main thread), so a depth beyond limit divided by frame size overflows, however much RAM is free.
A frame is 48 bytes and the thread stack is 1 MB. About how deep can recursion go? || 1,048,576 / 48 is about 21,800 calls. In practice slightly less, because the runtime and callers also use stack.
When would you replace recursion with an explicit stack on the heap, and what do you give up? || When depth depends on untrusted or very large input (deep JSON, long chains). You give up simple code and gain a depth limited by heap memory and the ability to fail cleanly instead of crashing.
interview: Walk through what happens on the stack when `main` calls `f(1, 2)` and `f` calls `g(3)`. Which registers and memory change? || `main` puts 1 and 2 in `rdi` and `rsi` and runs `call f`, pushing a return address. `f` saves `rbp`, sets up its frame, reserves locals, and for the call to `g` puts 3 in `rdi` and calls, pushing another return address. `g` returns its result in `rax`, `ret` pops back into `f`, and `f`'s epilogue restores `rbp` and `rsp` and returns to `main`. Callee-saved registers have the same values as before `f` ran.
How does a stack buffer overflow lead to arbitrary code execution, and which mitigations address which step? || A write past a local array reaches the saved return address, so `ret` jumps to an attacker-chosen address. Canaries detect the overwrite before `ret`. Non-executable stacks block injected code. ASLR makes target addresses unpredictable. CFI and shadow stacks check that returns go to legitimate places, which also stops return-oriented programming.
Why can a goroutine start with a stack of a few kilobytes while a pthread typically reserves megabytes? || A pthread's stack is a fixed region reserved up front; it cannot move because C code may hold raw pointers into it. The Go runtime knows where every pointer lives, so it can copy the stack to a larger block and fix the pointers when it fills. Memory is committed on demand either way, but the reserved address space and guard pages make thousands of pthreads costly.
What is the cost of the ABI being fixed, and give an example where it constrains design. || Changing register assignment or data layout breaks all existing compiled code, so ABIs stay for decades. For example, C++ standard library types like `std::string` or `std::unordered_map` keep layouts that newer designs would improve, because changing them would break binary compatibility across libraries.
How do exceptions or a debugger find the caller's frame when frame pointers are omitted? || The compiler emits unwind tables (DWARF CFI in `.eh_frame`) that describe, for each instruction address, how to compute the caller's stack pointer and which registers were saved where. The unwinder looks up the current program counter in the table and applies the rules, frame by frame. It is slower than following a frame-pointer chain.
ref: spec | System V Application Binary Interface, AMD64 Architecture Processor Supplement | H.J. Lu, M. Matz, M. Girkar, J. Hubicka, A. Jaeger, M. Mitchell | https://gitlab.com/x86-psABIs/x86-64-ABI
ref: spec | Procedure Call Standard for the Arm 64-bit Architecture (AAPCS64) | Arm | https://github.com/ARM-software/abi-aa
ref: paper | Smashing the Stack for Fun and Profit | Aleph One, Phrack 49, 1996 |
ref: book | Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective, ch. 3 (Machine-level programs) | Bryant & O'Hallaron, 3rd ed. 2015 |
ref: docs | Compiler Explorer | Matt Godbolt | https://godbolt.org
ref: book | Engineering a Compiler, ch. 6 (The procedure abstraction) | Cooper & Torczon, 3rd ed. 2022 |
ref: paper | StackGuard: Automatic Adaptive Detection and Prevention of Buffer-Overflow Attacks | Cowan et al., USENIX Security 1998 |
@@ vonneumann
value: A stored-program computer keeps its instructions in the same memory as its data. A register called the program counter (PC) holds the address of the next instruction. The processor repeats one cycle without end: fetch the instruction at the PC, decode it to see what it asks for, execute it, and move the PC forward (or, for a jump, to a new address). Almost every general-purpose computer built since 1949, from a phone to a server CPU, works this way.
The idea solves a specific problem. The first electronic computers, such as ENIAC (1945), were programmed by plugging cables and setting switches, which could take days for each new problem. If the program is only a list of numbers in memory, then changing the program means writing different numbers, which takes seconds. It also means that programs can be produced, loaded and changed by other programs. Compilers, linkers, operating systems, loaders, JIT compilers and debuggers all depend on this fact: a program is data that another program can create.
For the layer above, this is the abstract machine that every instruction set describes and every compiler targets. The instruction set (ISA), the pipeline, the call stack, interrupts and the memory hierarchy are all refinements of this single loop. The layers below supply the ingredients. The Turing machine (L3) shows that one fixed machine can run any program given as data, which is the theory of universality; the stored-program computer is its practical form. Digital logic (L3) provides the registers, counters, multiplexers and decoders from which the loop is built. Boolean algebra (L4) is the language in which the control logic (which wires to enable for which opcode) is designed and simplified.
terms: Program counter (PC) — the register that holds the address of the next instruction to fetch.
Instruction register (IR) — the register that holds the instruction being decoded and executed.
Opcode — the part of an instruction word that says which operation to perform.
Operand — the data or address an instruction works on: a register number, a constant or a memory address.
Fetch–decode–execute cycle — the repeating sequence: read the instruction at the PC, work out what it means, do it, update the PC.
Register — a very small, very fast storage cell inside the CPU, built from flip-flops.
Von Neumann architecture — one memory, holding both code and data, reached through one path from the CPU.
Harvard architecture — separate memories (and paths) for code and data.
Von Neumann bottleneck — the limit on speed set by the single path between CPU and memory.
history: In 1936 Alan Turing described a universal machine that reads a description of another machine from its tape and imitates it, so a program as data was already a theoretical idea. The engineering step came in the mid-1940s. The ENIAC team at the University of Pennsylvania, led by J. Presper Eckert and John Mauchly, found that rewiring the machine for each problem was the main cost. Their design for the successor, EDVAC, stored instructions in an internal memory (a mercury delay line). In June 1945, John von Neumann, who had joined the discussions, wrote "First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC", a clear description of the logical design. It circulated under his name alone, which is why the design carries it, though Eckert, Mauchly and others contributed ideas.
The first working stored-program computers followed in Britain. The Manchester "Baby" (Small-Scale Experimental Machine) ran its first program on 21 June 1948, using Williams-Kilburn tubes (cathode-ray tubes) as memory. EDSAC, built by Maurice Wilkes at Cambridge, ran its first programs on 6 May 1949 and was the first to be used regularly for real computation. EDVAC itself ran later, in 1951 to 1952. Commercial machines such as UNIVAC I (1951) and the IBM 701 (1952) made the model standard.
A competing layout was Harvard, named after the Harvard Mark I (1944), which read instructions from a paper tape separately from data. Modern chips use both: main memory is one space for code and data, but the L1 caches are split into an instruction cache and a data cache. In 1977 John Backus, in his Turing Award lecture, named the "von Neumann bottleneck" and criticised the word-at-a-time style of programming it encourages. Since then, much of computer architecture (caches, prefetching, out-of-order execution, wider memory buses) has been work to hide that bottleneck.
how: Take the smallest useful machine. It has a memory of numbered words, a program counter PC, an instruction register IR, an accumulator ACC for arithmetic, and a control unit. An instruction word is split into fields: for example the top 4 bits are the opcode (`LOAD`, `ADD`, `STORE`, `JUMP`, `HALT`) and the remaining bits are an address or a constant.
Fetch. The control unit puts the value of the PC on the address bus and asks memory to read. The word that comes back on the data bus is copied into the IR. The PC is then incremented to point at the next word (in a real instruction set the increment is the instruction size, 4 bytes for RISC-V or ARM64, 1 to 15 bytes for x86). Decode. The opcode bits of the IR go into a decoder, a block of logic gates that turns the 4-bit number into one active control line out of 16. This is Boolean logic: each control signal is a Boolean function of the opcode bits, for example "write the ACC" = `LOAD OR ADD`. Execute. The active control lines open the right paths: for `ADD x`, the address field goes to memory, the word read comes back, the ALU (arithmetic logic unit, built of gates such as full adders) adds it to the ACC, and the sum is stored in the ACC. For `JUMP x`, the address field is written into the PC, so the next fetch comes from `x`. A conditional jump writes the PC only if a flag is set; that single conditional is what gives the machine its power to loop and to choose, and it is the step from a calculator to a general computer.
Everything in the loop is made from digital logic: the PC is a register (a row of flip-flops that copy their input on each clock edge) with an adder next to it; memory is an array of such cells with address decoders; the buses are wires with multiplexers choosing which register drives them. A clock signal sequences the steps. In a simple design one instruction takes several clock cycles (fetch, decode, execute, write back). A modern core overlaps these stages in a pipeline and runs several instructions per cycle.
Because the instruction is just a word in memory, the machine can do things a fixed-function device cannot. A loader copies a program file into memory and sets the PC to its entry point: the operating system starting a process is a pure data copy plus one jump. A compiler writes instruction words to a file; a JIT compiler writes them directly into memory and jumps there. The same fact also explains the call stack: the return address is a number in memory that `ret` copies into the PC, so a bug that overwrites it redirects the program. Security measures such as "write XOR execute" (W^X) mark each page of memory as either writable or executable, never both, to restore some separation between code and data.
The cost of the shared path is the bottleneck. Think of the whole loop as a budget of memory accesses: a simple instruction such as `ADD` with a memory operand needs two (one to fetch the instruction, one to read the operand), and a `STORE` also needs two. Every extra access competes for the same bus. Each instruction needs a fetch from memory, and many need a data read or write on the same path. A modern core can start 4 to 6 instructions per cycle at about 3 to 5 GHz, so it can demand tens of billions of instructions per second, but main memory takes about 80 to 100 nanoseconds (hundreds of cycles) to respond to a miss. Hardware hides this with caches: the L1 instruction cache serves a fetch in about 4 cycles, and a separate L1 data cache serves loads, so at the CPU the design is effectively Harvard (a "modified Harvard" architecture), while programmers still see one memory. Branch prediction keeps the fetch going past unresolved jumps, because waiting for the jump to execute would stall the pipeline for 15 or more cycles.
diagram:
        +-------------------- MEMORY ---------------------+
        | addr  content                                   |
        |   0   LOAD  10     <- instructions (words)      |
        |   1   ADD   11                                  |
        |   2   STORE 12                                  |
        |   3   HALT                                      |
        |  10   7            <- data (words)              |
        |  11   5                                         |
        |  12   0                                         |
        +--------------^--------------------+-------------+
                       | address / data bus |
        +--------------+--------------------+-------------+
        | CPU                                             |
        |   PC --> [fetch]--> IR --> [decoder]            |
        |    ^                          |  control lines  |
        |    +---- PC+1 / jump target   v                 |
        |                       ACC <-> ALU <-> flags     |
        +-------------------------------------------------+
        loop:  fetch M[PC] -> IR;  PC <- PC+1;  decode;  execute
example: Let us run the tiny machine from the diagram by hand. Memory holds: address 0 `LOAD 10`, address 1 `ADD 11`, address 2 `STORE 12`, address 3 `HALT`; and data: address 10 holds 7, address 11 holds 5, address 12 holds 0. The PC starts at 0.
Cycle 1. Fetch: IR = M[0] = `LOAD 10`, PC becomes 1. Decode: opcode LOAD. Execute: ACC = M[10] = 7.
Cycle 2. Fetch: IR = M[1] = `ADD 11`, PC becomes 2. Execute: ACC = 7 + M[11] = 12.
Cycle 3. Fetch: IR = M[2] = `STORE 12`, PC becomes 3. Execute: M[12] = 12.
Cycle 4. Fetch: IR = M[3] = `HALT`, PC becomes 4. Execute: stop. Memory address 12 now holds 12. Four fetches, four decodes, four executions: this is 7 + 5 computed by data movement and control only.
Now see why "code is data" matters. Suppose the program is changed so that the instruction at address 2 is `STORE 3` instead of `STORE 12`. In cycle 3, the machine writes 12 into address 3, replacing `HALT` with the number 12. In cycle 4 the machine fetches M[3] and decodes the number 12 as an instruction. If the encoding gives opcode bits meaning `ADD` to this number, the machine does something nobody wrote in the source program. This is self-modifying code, and it also describes what a buffer overflow or an injected-code attack does: data is written, and then it is executed. It is also how a JIT compiler legitimately works: write instruction words as data, then jump to them.
Now a loop, to see the conditional jump. Add a counter at address 20 holding 3, and use `LOAD 20`, `SUB 21` (address 21 holds 1), `STORE 20`, `JNZ 0`, `HALT`. Each pass reduces M[20] by one, and `JNZ 0` writes 0 into the PC while the ACC is not zero. After passes with values 2, 1 and 0, the third pass has ACC = 0, the jump is not taken, the PC moves on and the machine halts. A program of 5 instructions executed 13 instructions in total (three passes of four, then HALT), because the PC can be set to an earlier address.
Finally the cost: a loop iteration here has 4 instruction fetches and 4 data accesses (loads and a store) on one path. A real core would keep the counter in a register, which removes the memory traffic, and the loop would run in a few cycles per iteration.
intuition: A cook who reads the next line of a recipe, does it, then moves a finger down the page; and the recipe is written on the same paper as the ingredients list, so a cook can also write new recipe lines.
The idea is natural once you ask how to make one machine do many jobs. Hard-wiring each job needs new hardware. Putting the job description into the same memory the machine already has costs nothing extra in hardware and lets any job be loaded in seconds. A counter that steps through memory is the simplest way to say "what next"; and a way to overwrite that counter gives choice and repetition. That is all that general computation needs, which is what Turing's theory had predicted. Everything beyond that (pipelines, caches, virtual memory) is a speed optimisation of the same loop.
mistakes: People often think "von Neumann architecture" means any computer with a CPU and memory. In fact its defining feature is that instructions and data share one memory and one access path. Many microcontrollers (some AVR and PIC chips) are strict Harvard machines, and modern CPUs are a mixture: unified main memory, split L1 caches.
People often think the CPU executes one instruction at a time, strictly in order. In fact that is only the programmer's view. Inside, pipelining, superscalar issue and out-of-order execution run many instructions in flight and may complete them in a different order, but the hardware makes the result look as if the simple loop had been followed (precise exceptions, in-order retirement).
People often think the idea was invented by John von Neumann alone. In fact the EDVAC design came from the ENIAC group, with Eckert and Mauchly as central contributors, and Turing had the theoretical idea in 1936 and proposed his own design (ACE) in 1945 and 1946. Von Neumann's report spread the clear description; credit is shared.
People often think code and data are separate in memory, so a data bug cannot affect code. In fact at the hardware level both are only bits in the same address space. Separation is imposed by software and by page permissions (W^X, DEP), and it can be bypassed, for example by return-oriented programming, which reuses existing code with attacker-chosen return addresses.
People often think the bottleneck is about the CPU being too slow. In fact the CPU is fast; the memory is far away and the path is narrow. Most of the effort in modern processors goes to avoiding memory accesses, so the speed of a program often depends on how well its access pattern fits the caches.
friction: The central limit is the memory wall. Processor speed grew much faster than DRAM latency improved: a core can do hundreds of operations in the time one cache miss takes (about 80 to 100 ns). Caches, prefetchers and out-of-order execution hide this for predictable access patterns but not for pointer chasing through a linked list or a random hash table. Memory bandwidth is the second limit: a server socket has maybe 100 to 400 GB/s of memory bandwidth, while its cores can consume far more, so data-heavy code (scans, joins, machine learning) is often bound by bandwidth, not by arithmetic.
Energy matters as much as time. Moving data off chip costs far more energy than computing with it, by a factor of tens to hundreds. This is why accelerators (GPUs, TPUs) keep data close to arithmetic units in large on-chip buffers, and why "near-memory" and "in-memory" computing are active research areas.
Security is the third cost. Shared memory for code and data lets attackers turn data into control. Mitigations (non-executable memory, ASLR, control-flow integrity, memory tagging) are all patches that restore separation in software or in extra hardware. Speculative execution, which exists to keep the fetch loop busy, created its own class of leaks (Spectre and Meltdown, disclosed in 2018).
Finally, the sequential model is hard to scale. Clock speeds stopped rising near 4 to 5 GHz around 2005 because of power, so performance now comes from many cores, vector units and accelerators. Then the single program counter is no longer the whole story: threads each have a PC, and shared memory needs a memory model and synchronisation. The von Neumann loop remains the model for each core, but the machine as a whole is not one loop.
tradeoffs: Choose a unified memory (von Neumann style) when flexibility matters: general operating systems, dynamic loading, JIT compilers and scripting languages need to create code at run time. Choose a Harvard design when the code is fixed and small, as in many microcontrollers and DSPs: you can fetch an instruction and read data in the same cycle, code can live in flash, and the data cannot be run as code by mistake. You give up self-modifying code and easy code loading.
Choose a general-purpose CPU when the workload is branchy and varied; choose a GPU or TPU when it is regular and parallel (dense matrix work). The accelerator removes much of the fetch-decode overhead per operation (one instruction drives many arithmetic units) and keeps data near the units, but it is poor at irregular control flow and needs the CPU to feed it.
Choose a RISC-style fixed-width instruction encoding (ARM64, RISC-V) when you want simple, parallel decoding and wide issue; choose a variable-length encoding (x86) when code density and compatibility with decades of software matter. x86 chips pay with a more complex decoder and often a decoded-micro-op cache to hide it.
Choose to enforce W^X on all memory unless you must generate code (JIT). For JIT, map pages writable, write the code, then flip them to executable, which costs a system call per switch but keeps the window of exposure small.
practice: To understand the loop in practice, write a tiny interpreter: an array of words, a PC, a `while` loop with a `switch` on the opcode. A dozen opcodes are enough to run a loop that sums numbers. This is exactly how bytecode virtual machines (the JVM interpreter, CPython) work: they are software von Neumann machines with their own PC, called the instruction pointer, and often their own stack.
When you profile, remember the model. Instructions per cycle (IPC) near 1 on a core that can do 4 means that it is waiting, usually for memory. Use `perf stat` to read IPC, cache misses and branch misses. Lay out data contiguously (arrays of structs or columns), process it in order so that the prefetcher works, and avoid pointer chasing in hot loops. Keep hot code small enough to stay in the instruction cache (32 KB L1I on many cores), because large unrolled or heavily inlined code can lose more in instruction cache misses than it gains.
For security, build with the standard hardening flags, keep W^X, and treat any feature that turns data into code (eval, dynamic code loading, deserialization that instantiates classes) as a boundary that needs validation. Read an annotated disassembly of a small function (`objdump -d`, Compiler Explorer) until you can say what each instruction does to PC, registers and memory. For deeper study, read the first chapters of Patterson and Hennessy, or build the simple CPU in the nand2tetris course, which goes from NAND gates to a working fetch-execute machine.
quiz: What are the three steps of the cycle, and what changes the program counter other than the normal increment? || Fetch the instruction at the PC, decode it, execute it. A jump, branch, call, return, interrupt or exception writes a new value into the PC.
Why does storing the program in ordinary memory make a compiler or an operating system loader possible? || Because instructions are only numbers, another program can create them as data (a compiler writes a file, a loader copies it into memory) and then start them by setting the PC. No rewiring is needed.
What is the von Neumann bottleneck, and what do modern CPUs do about it? || Every instruction and data word crosses one path to memory, which is far slower than the core. CPUs use caches (with split L1 instruction and data caches), prefetching, branch prediction and out-of-order execution to keep the core busy.
A core runs at 4 GHz and can start 4 instructions per cycle, but a program measures an IPC of 0.5. What does this suggest and what is the achieved rate? || The rate is 0.5 × 4 billion = 2 billion instructions per second, only one eighth of the peak. It points to stalls, most often cache misses or branch mispredictions, so look at memory access patterns first.
When would you choose a Harvard-style design over a unified memory? || For small fixed-program devices (microcontrollers, DSPs) where code is in flash, you want a fetch and a data access in the same cycle, and you do not need to load code at run time. You give up flexibility.
interview: Why does a return address on the stack make the stored-program model a security problem, and what hardware or software defences exist? || The saved return address is data in writable memory that `ret` loads into the PC, so overwriting it redirects execution. Defences: canaries, non-executable stacks (W^X), ASLR, control-flow integrity, hardware shadow stacks (Intel CET) and pointer authentication (ARM).
How can a processor with a split L1 instruction and data cache still run self-modifying code or a JIT correctly? || After code is written as data, the new bytes may sit in the data cache while the instruction cache holds stale code. Software must clean the data cache and invalidate the instruction cache for that range (explicit on ARM; x86 keeps them coherent for most cases but still needs a serializing step), and flip the page from writable to executable.
Explain how a pipeline preserves the illusion of the simple fetch-decode-execute loop when it runs instructions out of order. || Instructions may execute in any order that respects data dependencies, but results are committed in program order through a reorder buffer. If an earlier instruction faults or a branch was mispredicted, later speculative results are discarded, so the architectural state looks as if instructions ran one at a time.
Why is a CPU with branch prediction faster, and what does it cost when it is wrong? || Fetch can continue down the predicted path without waiting for the branch to resolve. A misprediction flushes the pipeline, costing about 15 to 20 cycles on modern cores, and speculative side effects can leak through caches (Spectre).
Where does the Turing machine come in; is a real computer a Turing machine? || A stored-program computer is a practical universal machine: it runs any program given as data. It differs from the ideal because memory is finite, so strictly it is a very large finite-state machine, but for practical purposes it is as capable as a Turing machine.
ref: paper | First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC | John von Neumann, 1945 |
ref: paper | Can Programming Be Liberated from the von Neumann Style? | John Backus, CACM 1978 (Turing Award lecture 1977) |
ref: book | Computer Organization and Design: The Hardware/Software Interface (RISC-V ed.) | Patterson & Hennessy, 2020 |
ref: course | The Elements of Computing Systems / nand2tetris | Nisan & Schocken | https://www.nand2tetris.org
ref: book | The Annotated Turing | Charles Petzold, 2008 |
ref: paper | On Computable Numbers, with an Application to the Entscheidungsproblem | Alan M. Turing, 1936 |
ref: book | Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach, 6th ed. | Hennessy & Patterson, 2017 |
@@ sat
value: A SAT solver takes a Boolean formula and answers one question: is there an assignment of true or false to its variables that makes the whole formula true? If yes, it returns such an assignment (a model). If no, it says "unsatisfiable", and modern solvers can also produce a checkable proof. SAT is the textbook NP-complete problem, so no algorithm is known that is fast on every input. Yet industrial solvers routinely decide formulas with millions of variables and tens of millions of clauses, because real problems have structure that clever search can exploit.
That makes SAT a general-purpose engine for hard combinatorial questions. You describe your problem as constraints, translate it to Boolean clauses, and let the solver search. Package managers use it to find a set of library versions that satisfy every dependency. Chip designers use it to check that a circuit matches its specification. Cloud providers use SMT solvers to prove that a security policy never allows public access to a bucket (AWS Zelkova does this for IAM and S3 policies). Compilers, test generators and program verifiers use it to ask "is there an input that reaches this bug?".
SMT (Satisfiability Modulo Theories) extends SAT by letting the variables stand for numbers, arrays or bit-vectors, so you can write `x + y > 5` or `a[i] = v` directly instead of encoding them in bits. For the layers above, a solver is a black box that discharges proof obligations in formal verification and Hoare-logic tools, turns planning and constraint-satisfaction problems into a search, and checks configurations. The layers below make it work. Propositional logic (L4) defines formulas and what "entails" means, and its resolution rule is how learned clauses are derived. Boolean algebra (L4) supplies the normal form: any formula can be turned into conjunctive normal form (an AND of ORs). P vs NP (L3) explains both why SAT is hard in the worst case (Cook–Levin) and why a good SAT solver is still a very valuable tool: many other hard problems reduce to it.
terms: Literal — a variable or its negation, such as `x` or `¬x`.
Clause — an OR of literals, such as `(¬a ∨ b ∨ c)`; it is satisfied when at least one literal is true.
CNF (conjunctive normal form) — a formula written as an AND of clauses; the standard input format of SAT solvers (DIMACS).
Unit propagation — if all but one literal of a clause are false, the last literal must be true; apply this repeatedly (also called Boolean constraint propagation).
Decision and decision level — a guess of a variable's value made by the solver; the level counts how many guesses are currently active.
Conflict — a clause in which every literal has become false under the current assignment.
Learned clause — a new clause, implied by the formula, derived by analysing a conflict so that the same mistake is never repeated.
CDCL — conflict-driven clause learning; the search algorithm of almost every modern solver.
SMT — satisfiability modulo theories; SAT combined with decision procedures for arithmetic, arrays, bit-vectors and so on.
history: The first algorithm is the Davis–Putnam procedure (1960), using resolution to eliminate variables, followed by the DPLL algorithm of Davis, Logemann and Loveland (1962), which replaced it with backtracking search plus unit propagation, because resolution used too much memory. In 1971 Stephen Cook proved that SAT is NP-complete (Leonid Levin independently, in the Soviet Union, shortly after), and Karp's 1972 list of 21 NP-complete problems made SAT the root of the theory. For decades, SAT was seen as a theoretical symbol of intractability, not a tool.
Then solvers improved by orders of magnitude. GRASP (Marques-Silva and Sakallah, 1996) introduced conflict analysis and clause learning, turning DPLL into CDCL. Chaff (Moskewicz, Madigan, Zhao, Zhang and Malik, 2001) added the two-watched-literal scheme, which made propagation fast, and the VSIDS heuristic for choosing variables. MiniSat (Eén and Sörensson, 2003) packaged these ideas in a few hundred lines of C++ and became the base of many research solvers. Biere's CaDiCaL and Kissat, and Glucose, refine it with better restarts, clause deletion and preprocessing. The yearly SAT Competition (since 2002) tracks progress. On the application side, bounded model checking (Biere et al., 1999) showed that SAT could verify hardware faster than the BDD methods then used. SMT solvers followed: Z3 (de Moura and Bjørner, Microsoft Research, 2008) and cvc5 (successor of CVC4) became standard, and the DPLL(T) architecture links a SAT core to theory solvers.
Results grew spectacular. In 2016, Heule, Kullmann and Marek settled the Boolean Pythagorean triples problem with a SAT solver and a proof of roughly 200 terabytes (before compression), checked by an independent verified checker. Package managers adopted the technology too: libsolv (used by openSUSE and DNF) is a SAT-based resolver, and Dart's pub introduced PubGrub (2018), a CDCL-style resolver that also produces human-readable explanations.
how: Everything starts with CNF. Most problems are not given as an AND of ORs, so a front end converts them. The Tseitin transformation (1968) does this in linear size: give each sub-expression a fresh variable and add clauses saying the variable equals the operation. For example `g = a ∧ b` becomes `(¬g ∨ a) ∧ (¬g ∨ b) ∧ (g ∨ ¬a ∨ ¬b)`. The new formula is satisfiable exactly when the original is, which is what Boolean algebra's laws (De Morgan, distribution) guarantee, and the naive distribution would blow up exponentially, which Tseitin avoids by adding variables.
DPLL then works like this. Pick a variable and guess a value (a decision). Apply unit propagation: any clause with all but one literal false forces the last literal, which may force more. If a clause becomes entirely false, there is a conflict, so undo the latest decision and try the other value. If all variables are assigned with no conflict, the formula is satisfiable. Plain DPLL is complete but forgets: it can hit the same dead end repeatedly in different parts of the search tree.
CDCL fixes this with learning. When a conflict happens, the solver examines the implication graph: which decisions and which clauses forced which assignments. Starting from the conflicting clause, it repeatedly applies resolution with the clause that caused the most recent assigned literal, until exactly one literal of the current decision level remains (the first unique implication point, or 1-UIP). The result is a learned clause: a new fact implied by the formula that says "this combination of decisions fails". It is added to the formula. The solver then backjumps, not to the previous decision, but to the second-highest decision level among the learned clause's literals, where the clause becomes a unit and flips a literal. Non-chronological backjumping can skip many irrelevant decisions at once. If a conflict happens at level zero, the formula is unsatisfiable, and the sequence of learned clauses is a resolution proof.
Speed comes from engineering. Two watched literals: each clause watches two of its literals that are not false. A clause only needs attention when one watched literal becomes false, so most assignments touch very few clauses, and backtracking costs nothing because watches need no update. VSIDS (variable state independent decaying sum) gives each variable an activity score that is bumped when it takes part in a conflict and decays over time, and the solver decides on the highest-activity variable, which focuses the search on the hard core of the problem. Restarts (often following the Luby sequence or glucose-style rules) abandon the current assignment while keeping learned clauses, which avoids getting stuck in an unlucky part of the tree. Phase saving remembers the last value of each variable, so the solver returns to a good partial solution after a restart. Clause deletion removes learned clauses that look useless (using measures such as literal block distance), because otherwise they would fill memory and slow propagation. Preprocessing (variable elimination, subsumption) shrinks the formula before search.
SMT adds theories on top by the DPLL(T) scheme. The formula is abstracted: each atom such as `x + y > 5` becomes a Boolean variable. The SAT core assigns Booleans. Whenever it has a (partial) assignment, the theory solver checks whether the corresponding conjunction of theory facts is consistent: for linear arithmetic a simplex-style procedure, for equality with uninterpreted functions the congruence closure algorithm, for bit-vectors usually bit-blasting into SAT, for arrays axioms about read and write. If the theory solver finds an inconsistency, it returns a small explanation (a subset of the atoms that cannot all hold), and the SAT core adds its negation as a learned clause. So the Boolean engine handles the combinatorial choices and the theory solver handles the meaning of the numbers.
From the P vs NP side, remember the cost: worst-case time is exponential, and no one knows whether that can be avoided. Random 3-SAT formulas get hardest when the ratio of clauses to variables is about 4.26, where they flip from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable. Structured industrial formulas are far easier than random ones of the same size, because they have small "backdoor" sets of influential variables and local structure that learning captures.
diagram:
  formula (CNF) --> [ preprocess ] --> +---------------------------+
                                       |        CDCL loop          |
        +----------------------------->|  pick var by VSIDS        |
        |                              |  decide  (level += 1)     |
        |                              +-------------+-------------+
        |                                            v
        |                              +---------------------------+
        |                              | unit propagation          |
        |                              | (two watched literals)    |
        |                              +------+------------+-------+
        |                         no conflict |            | conflict
        |                                     v            v
        |                         all assigned? -> SAT   analyse (1-UIP)
        |                                                  |
        |                                  learned clause + backjump
        +--------------------------------------------------+
                          conflict at level 0  ->  UNSAT (+ proof)

  SMT:  SAT core  <--- Boolean atoms --->  theory solver (simplex,
                  <--- explanation  ---    congruence closure, ...)
example: Take seven clauses over variables a to g:
C1 = (¬a ∨ b), C2 = (¬a ∨ c), C3 = (¬b ∨ ¬c ∨ d), C4 = (¬b ∨ ¬c ∨ ¬d), C5 = (a ∨ e), C6 = (¬e ∨ f), C7 = (¬f ∨ g).
Step 1: decide a = true (level 1). Unit propagation: C1 has ¬a false, so b must be true (reason C1). C2 forces c = true. C3 is now (false ∨ false ∨ d), so d = true (reason C3). Now C4 is (¬b ∨ ¬c ∨ ¬d) with all three literals false: a conflict.
Step 2: analyse. Start with the conflict clause C4 = (¬b ∨ ¬c ∨ ¬d). Resolve it with C3 on d: the result is (¬b ∨ ¬c). Resolve with C2 on c: (¬b ∨ ¬a). Resolve with C1 on b: (¬a). Now only one literal from the current level remains, so the learned clause is (¬a). It says: a can never be true.
Step 3: backjump to level 0 and assert a = false by the learned clause. Propagate: C5 = (a ∨ e) forces e = true; C6 forces f = true; C7 forces g = true. Every clause is satisfied, so the formula is satisfiable. The remaining variables b, c, d are free; setting them to false satisfies C3 and C4 too. One conflict and one learned clause settled what plain trial and error might have repeated in many branches.
Now an SMT query. We ask whether integers x and y can satisfy x + y > 5, x < 2 and y < 3.
```python
from z3 import Ints, Solver
x, y = Ints("x y")
s = Solver()
s.add(x + y > 5, x < 2, y < 3)
print(s.check())      # unsat
s2 = Solver()
s2.add(x + y > 5, x < 5, y < 3)
print(s2.check())     # sat
print(s2.model())     # for example [x = 4, y = 2]
```
The first query is unsatisfiable: over the integers, x < 2 means x ≤ 1 and y < 3 means y ≤ 2, so x + y ≤ 3, which cannot exceed 5. The theory solver finds this with linear arithmetic and returns a three-atom explanation, which the SAT core stores as a clause. In the second query, loosening x to x < 5 makes room: x = 4 and y = 2 give a sum of 6, which is greater than 5, so the answer is `sat` and the model is a concrete witness.
intuition: A detective who, on every dead end, writes down the exact combination of assumptions that made the dead end, and never walks into that combination again.
Plain search is brute force with good manners. The insight of CDCL is that a failure is information: the conflict says that a few specific decisions cannot hold together, and a short clause can say so for the rest of the run. Propagation makes each guess pay off, since one decision often forces dozens of others. Together the solver learns a compressed model of why the problem is hard. SMT follows the same split in a more modest way: let a specialist (the theory solver) say what is possible, and let the Boolean engine decide the combinatorial structure.
mistakes: People often think "NP-complete" means SAT is unsolvable in practice. In fact NP-completeness is a statement about worst cases. Real inputs from hardware, software and planning problems have structure, and solvers handle millions of variables. The theory says that no algorithm is fast on every input, not that none is fast on the inputs you have.
People often think a SAT solver just tries all assignments. In fact exhaustive enumeration of 100 variables would need 2^100 tries. CDCL prunes by propagation and by learned clauses that cut away entire regions of the space at once.
People often think a solver result of "unsat" is a claim you have to trust. In fact modern solvers can emit a proof in the DRAT format, and an independent checker (some formally verified) can validate it. A "sat" answer is easy to check by evaluating the formula with the returned assignment.
People often think SMT means a bigger, slower SAT. In fact it uses a different division of labour: theory solvers reason directly about arithmetic or arrays with algorithms such as simplex, which would be very costly after encoding to bits. Encoding is still sometimes best (bit-vectors), but it is a choice.
People often think encoding a problem to SAT is mechanical and always fine. In fact the encoding determines performance. Two correct encodings of the same constraint (for example "at most one of n variables is true", with n²/2 pairwise clauses versus a linear-size ladder encoding) can differ by orders of magnitude in solving time.
friction: Worst-case hardness remains: some small formulas (pigeonhole-like) are very hard for CDCL, because resolution proofs of them are provably exponential, and that limit applies to any solver based on resolution. Solvers are sensitive to small changes: renaming variables or reordering clauses can change run time from seconds to hours, which makes performance unpredictable and benchmarks noisy. Many production uses therefore set a time limit and treat "timeout" as a third answer.
Encodings are the main engineering cost. Cardinality constraints, arithmetic, and quantifiers need care; SMT with quantifiers is undecidable in general, and solvers use heuristics (instantiation) that may return "unknown". Nonlinear integer arithmetic is undecidable, so Z3 may say `unknown` for harmless-looking constraints. Floating-point and string theories exist but are expensive.
Memory also matters: learned clauses and proofs can be huge (the Pythagorean triples proof was 200 TB before compression), and long proofs are slow to verify. Incremental solving (adding and removing assumptions between calls) is supported but has subtleties.
Finally, usability: a bare "unsat" tells a user little. Good tools extract an unsatisfiable core (a small subset of the constraints that already conflicts), as package managers do to explain "package A needs B 2.x but C needs B 1.x". Building that explanation layer is often more work than building the encoding.
tradeoffs: Choose SAT with a hand-made encoding when the problem is purely Boolean or finite (scheduling slots, circuit equivalence, puzzle-like constraints) and you want the fastest solver: modern CDCL on a tight encoding often beats a general SMT call by a large factor. Choose SMT when the problem has arithmetic, arrays, or bit-vectors and the natural statement is clearer than the encoding: you pay some speed for correctness and readability.
Choose a SAT/SMT solver over a mixed-integer programming solver when the problem is mostly logical (many Boolean choices with implications) and you need a yes/no answer or a proof. Choose integer linear programming when there is a numeric objective to optimise and mostly linear constraints, since the LP relaxation gives strong bounds. MaxSAT and optimising SMT (Z3 `Optimize`) sit in between.
Choose a SAT solver over a model checker with explicit state exploration when the state space is huge but the property is local or bounded (bounded model checking). Choose explicit-state or BDD methods when the state space is small, and you need to prove properties over unbounded executions with fixed-point reasoning.
Choose a proof-producing configuration (DRAT output) when the result will be relied on, such as in certification, and accept the slowdown and disk cost; choose the default mode for exploration and testing.
practice: Start from the use you have. For dependency resolution, use an existing resolver (libsolv, PubGrub-based tools, `uv`) instead of writing one. For checking configurations or policies, write them as constraints in Z3's Python API and ask for a counterexample: "is there a request that the policy allows but should not?". A `sat` answer is a concrete bug with a witness; `unsat` is a proof.
Learn the encoding patterns: use Tseitin for arbitrary formulas, use sequential-counter or totalizer encodings for "at most k", use bit-vectors (not unbounded integers) when modelling machine arithmetic, and give constraints names with `assert_and_track` or assumptions so that you can ask for an unsat core. Set a timeout (`s.set("timeout", 10000)` in milliseconds) and handle `unknown`. Try several seeds or solvers on hard instances: a portfolio of solvers is a cheap way to cut the variance.
Keep models small. Reduce the domain (bounded integers, fewer array indices) first, scale up later. Solve incrementally when the constraints differ by a few clauses, using push/pop or assumptions. For big formulas, read the DIMACS statistics (variables, clauses, conflicts per second): if conflicts per second collapse, memory or clause database size is the problem.
To go deeper, read the Handbook of Satisfiability, play with the "Z3 Guide" tutorial, and look at how Dafny, Verus, Kani and AWS Zelkova hand obligations to SMT solvers. Writing a 200-line DPLL solver in Python is a good exercise, and adding clause learning teaches the whole idea.
quiz: What are the four main ingredients of a modern CDCL solver? || Unit propagation (with watched literals), decisions guided by a heuristic such as VSIDS, conflict analysis that learns a clause, and non-chronological backjumping, plus restarts and clause deletion for robustness.
Why does unit propagation make search much cheaper than blind guessing? || One decision often forces other variables through clauses with a single unforced literal, so many variables are set without branching, and a wrong guess is detected as soon as a clause becomes fully false.
How does a learned clause help later in the search? || It is a fact implied by the formula that rules out a combination of assignments that led to a conflict. Propagation then blocks that combination anywhere in the search tree, and the clause often triggers a unit implication right after the backjump.
A formula has 300 variables and 1,278 clauses of 3 literals each, chosen at random. Is it likely to be hard? || Yes. The ratio 1,278 / 300 = 4.26 is the phase transition for random 3-SAT, where instances are hardest, about half are satisfiable, and solvers need the longest time.
When would you prefer an SMT solver over encoding to SAT yourself? || When the constraints are naturally arithmetic or about arrays and data structures: the theory solver handles them directly and your model stays readable. Prefer a tight hand encoding to SAT when everything is Boolean or small finite and speed is the priority.
interview: Explain how CDCL differs from DPLL and why that matters in practice. || DPLL backtracks chronologically and forgets why it failed. CDCL analyses each conflict, learns a clause with the 1-UIP scheme, and jumps back non-chronologically, so the same failure is blocked everywhere in later search. With watched literals, VSIDS and restarts it solves industrial instances with millions of variables that DPLL cannot.
Why can SAT solvers handle huge industrial formulas if SAT is NP-complete? || NP-completeness describes worst-case inputs. Industrial formulas have structure: small sets of influential variables, local interactions and modularity that learning and VSIDS exploit. Random or crafted formulas (pigeonhole, random 3-SAT at ratio 4.26) can still be very hard.
How would you use a solver to verify that a cloud access policy never allows public read? || Encode the policy as logical constraints over request attributes (principal, action, resource, conditions, including string and IP-range theories), add the negated property ("request is public and allowed"), and ask for satisfiability. `unsat` is a proof that no public access exists; `sat` returns a concrete request as a counterexample. AWS Zelkova does this with SMT.
What are the limits of using SMT for program verification? || Quantifiers and non-linear arithmetic make queries undecidable or lead to `unknown` and timeouts. Loops need invariants supplied by people or inferred by other tools. Results depend on the model of the language (overflow, aliasing). Solver run-time is unpredictable, so verifiers add time limits and require stable proofs.
How do you debug an "unsat" result when you expected "sat"? || Ask for an unsatisfiable core by tracking each assertion with a label, then reduce it to a minimal subset of conflicting constraints. Inspect those constraints; often an encoding mistake (off-by-one, wrong sign) or an over-constrained input is the cause.
ref: paper | The Complexity of Theorem-Proving Procedures | Stephen A. Cook, STOC 1971 |
ref: paper | Chaff: Engineering an Efficient SAT Solver | Moskewicz, Madigan, Zhao, Zhang & Malik, DAC 2001 |
ref: paper | An Extensible SAT-solver (MiniSat) | Niklas Eén & Niklas Sörensson, SAT 2003 |
ref: book | Handbook of Satisfiability, 2nd ed. | Biere, Heule, van Maaren & Walsh (eds.), IOS Press, 2021 |
ref: paper | Z3: An Efficient SMT Solver | Leonardo de Moura & Nikolaj Bjørner, TACAS 2008 |
ref: docs | Z3 Guide and Python tutorial | Microsoft Research | https://microsoft.github.io/z3guide/
ref: paper | Solving and Verifying the Boolean Pythagorean Triples Problem via Cube-and-Conquer | Heule, Kullmann & Marek, SAT 2016 | https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.00723
ref: paper | Semantic-based Automated Reasoning for AWS Access Policies using SMT | Backes et al., FMCAD 2018 |
@@ modelcheck
value: Model checking is a way to find bugs in concurrent and distributed designs that testing almost never finds. You write the system as a state machine (a set of states and the transitions between them), you write the requirement as a formula in temporal logic ("a lock is never held by two processes", "every request is eventually answered"), and the model checker explores every reachable state, not a sample. If the requirement can fail, it prints a counterexample: the exact sequence of steps that leads to the failure, usually the shortest one.
This matters because the bugs that hurt distributed systems hide in rare interleavings: two messages arrive in an unlucky order, a node crashes between two writes, a timeout fires at the worst moment. A test suite runs a few thousand such schedules; a model checker covers all of them for a bounded model. Amazon Web Services reported in 2015 that TLA+ model checking found subtle bugs in S3, DynamoDB, EBS and other core services before they reached production. One DynamoDB bug needed a trace of 35 high-level steps, which no test would reach by chance.
For the layer above, the TLA+ tool (the TLC checker), the P language, SPIN, Alloy and similar tools are the engines behind design reviews of consensus protocols such as Paxos and Raft, and deterministic simulation testing is a randomised cousin of the same idea. The layers below supply the machinery. Finite automata (L3) are the model of the system and of the property: a state machine with transitions, and Büchi automata for infinite runs. Propositional and first-order logic (L4) give the language for describing states and temporal operators. Graph algorithms (L3) do the work: checking is a reachability search in the state graph, and finding a violation of a liveness property is finding a cycle. Formal languages (L4) explain that an LTL property is a set of infinite words, so "the system satisfies the property" is a language containment question. Space complexity (L3) explains the cost: LTL model checking is PSPACE-complete, which is why the size of the state space is the main limit.
terms: State — one complete snapshot of the system: all variables, program counters and messages in flight.
Transition — a step that takes the system from one state to another; the model is the graph of states and transitions.
Kripke structure — the formal name for a state graph in which each state is labelled with the facts that are true there.
Safety property — "something bad never happens"; it can be violated by a finite trace, such as two processes in a critical section.
Liveness property — "something good eventually happens"; it can only be violated by an infinite run, such as a request that waits forever.
LTL (linear temporal logic) — a logic over single execution paths with operators G (always), F (eventually), X (next) and U (until).
CTL (computation tree logic) — a logic over the tree of all possible futures, with path quantifiers such as A (on all paths) and E (on some path).
Counterexample — a concrete execution that violates the property; it is the main output of a model checker.
State explosion — the number of states grows exponentially with the number of components.
history: Amir Pnueli proposed temporal logic as the right language for reasoning about concurrent programs in 1977. At that time, correctness was proved by hand with invariants, which was slow and needed a person for each system. In 1981, Edmund Clarke and Allen Emerson, and independently Jean-Pierre Queille and Joseph Sifakis (published 1982), had the key idea: for a finite-state system, do not prove anything, just search the state graph and check the formula directly. Clarke, Emerson and Sifakis received the Turing Award in 2007 for this. The first checkers handled thousands of states, which was enough for small protocols and not for hardware.
The next jump came from representation. Kenneth McMillan's symbolic model checking (1992) stored sets of states as binary decision diagrams (BDDs) instead of one by one, and checked circuits with more than 10^20 states. Meanwhile Gerard Holzmann built SPIN at Bell Labs (the tool received the ACM Software System Award in 2001) for protocol design, using on-the-fly search and partial-order reduction. The Pentium FDIV floating-point bug of 1994 cost Intel about 475 million dollars and pushed industry to adopt formal checking of hardware. In 1999 bounded model checking (Biere and others) replaced BDDs by SAT solvers for many problems, and counterexample-guided abstraction refinement (CEGAR, 2000) allowed checking of software by abstracting it.
For software and systems design, Leslie Lamport's TLA+ language (1990s) and the TLC checker (Yuan Yu, 1999) became the practical choice. Chris Newcombe and colleagues at Amazon described their experience in "How Amazon Web Services Uses Formal Methods" (CACM, 2015); later work at AWS used lightweight formal methods and the P language to validate storage nodes in S3. Diego Ongaro specified Raft in TLA+ in his dissertation. The trend is toward lightweight use: engineers write a one- to two-page spec of the hard protocol, run TLC on a small configuration, and fix the design before writing code.
how: Step 1: build the model. A system is described by variables and an initial condition, plus a next-state relation that says how variables may change in one step. Concurrency is modelled by interleaving: in each step, exactly one component (a process, a message delivery, a crash) takes a step, chosen non-deterministically. The set of all states reachable from the initial states under this relation is the state graph. Because every possible choice is taken, the model includes every interleaving, which is the source of its power. Finite automata are exactly this object: states, transitions, initial states.
Step 2: write the property. Temporal logic adds time to propositional logic. In LTL, G p means p holds in every state of the path, F p means p holds in some later state, X p means p holds in the next state, and p U q means p holds until q becomes true. Typical requirements are the safety property G ¬(crit1 ∧ crit2) ("never both in the critical section"), and the liveness property G (request → F grant) ("every request is eventually granted"). Safety properties are checked on finite prefixes: any violation is visible in some finite trace. Liveness properties are violated only by infinite behaviour, and need fairness assumptions ("a process that can always move eventually moves"), otherwise the scheduler may ignore a process forever and every liveness property fails trivially.
Step 3: explore. For a safety property, the simplest checker is a breadth-first or depth-first search. It keeps a hash set of visited states, a queue of states to expand, and for each new state it evaluates the invariant. If the invariant fails in some state, the checker follows parent pointers back to an initial state and prints that trace. Breadth-first search gives the shortest counterexample, which is why the traces are readable. This is plain graph reachability from the graph algorithms layer; the work is in making it fast, with compact state encoding (hashing states to 64-bit fingerprints, as TLC does, trading a tiny chance of missing a state for much smaller memory) and by running on many cores or machines.
Step 4: check liveness and full LTL. The standard method, from Vardi and Wolper (1986), is automata-theoretic. Translate the negation of the property into a Büchi automaton, an automaton that accepts infinite words by visiting an accepting state infinitely often. Build the product of the system graph and this automaton. The system violates the property exactly if the product has a reachable cycle that goes through an accepting state. That is found by a graph search (nested depth-first search, or strongly connected components). So the check is a language question from formal language theory: the set of system runs must be a subset of the set of runs the property allows, and the product tests whether the intersection with the complement is empty. For CTL, checking is simpler: label the states with the subformulas that hold, working bottom-up, which takes time linear in the model size times the formula size.
Step 5: fight the state explosion. A system with n components of c local states has up to c^n global states. Several techniques reduce this. Partial-order reduction notices that two independent steps (two processes changing unrelated variables) commute, so it explores only one of the two orders. Symmetry reduction treats states that differ only by renaming identical processes as one. Symbolic methods represent sets of states as BDDs or as SAT formulas. Abstraction replaces details by a smaller over-approximation and refines it when a counterexample turns out to be spurious. Bounded checking explores only up to depth k, or limits the model to a small number of nodes, messages and failures (the small-scope hypothesis: most design bugs show up in small instances, such as 3 nodes and 2 values).
The guarantee is precise and limited. If the checker explores the whole graph of the model and finds no violation, the property holds for that model. It says nothing about the real code, which may differ, or about instances larger than the model. This is why model checking is used on designs, and why it works best together with testing of the implementation.
diagram:
  system model                       property (negated)
  (state graph)                      LTL -> Buchi automaton
  +------------------+               +------------------+
  | s0 -> s1 -> s2   |               |  q0 --> q1(acc)  |
  |  \         /     |               +--------+---------+
  |   +-> s3 -+      |                        |
  +--------+---------+                        |
           |           product graph          |
           +----------->  (s, q) pairs  <-----+
                              |
              search: reachable cycle through an accepting q ?
                    |                              |
                   yes                            no
          counterexample trace              property holds
          s0 s1 s2 (s3 s1 s2)*              for this model

  safety only:  BFS from initial states, test invariant in each state
  BFS gives the shortest trace; fingerprints cost ~8 bytes per state
example: Take two processes that want to enter a critical section, with a simple protocol. Each process has three program locations: 0 (idle), 1 (has seen that the other process's flag is down) and 2 (critical, flag up). Process i does: at 0, if the other process is not at 2, move to 1; at 1, move to 2 (this raises its flag); at 2, move back to 0. The global state is a pair (pc1, pc2), so there are at most 3 × 3 = 9 states. The requirement is the safety property G ¬(pc1 = 2 ∧ pc2 = 2).
Start from (0,0). Breadth-first search expands level by level. Level 1: P1 moves to give (1,0), or P2 moves to give (0,1). Level 2 from (1,0): P1 moves to (2,0); P2 checks that P1 is not at 2 (it is at 1), so P2 moves to (1,1). From (0,1) we get (1,1) (already known) and (0,2). Level 3 from (1,1): P1 moves to (2,1); P2 moves to (1,2). Level 4 from (2,1): P2 moves to (2,2). The invariant fails there. Following parent pointers gives the shortest counterexample, four steps:
(0,0) → P1 checks → (1,0) → P2 checks → (1,1) → P1 enters → (2,1) → P2 enters → (2,2).
Read it as a story: both processes looked before either raised a flag, so both saw "free". This is a check-then-act race, the same pattern as a read-then-write on a database row without a lock. The usual bug hunt would need two threads to be paused at exactly the wrong instants; the checker tells you the schedule.
Now try the "obvious fix": each process raises its flag first, then checks that the other flag is down. Add locations: 0 idle, 1 flag up (waiting), 2 critical. At 0 → 1 always; at 1 → 2 only if the other is not at 1 or 2; at 2 → 0. The safety property now holds in all reachable states. But the liveness property G (pc1 = 1 → F pc1 = 2) fails: from (1,1), neither process can move, since each waits for the other's flag. That is a deadlock, shown by a reachable state with no outgoing transitions (or a self-loop). The checker reports the path (0,0) → (1,0) → (1,1) and stops. The classic fix (Peterson's algorithm) adds a turn variable, and the checker can then verify both properties.
Finally scale. If each of n processes has 3 local states, the full graph has 3^n states. For n = 20 that is about 3.5 billion states. At 8 bytes per fingerprint, the visited set needs about 28 GB, which is at the limit of one machine. This is why teams model 3 nodes, not 300, and why they use symmetry reduction to treat "process 1 is critical" and "process 2 is critical" as the same state shape.
intuition: A very patient inspector who walks through every room of a building you drew, including the rooms you forgot existed, and hands you the path that leads to a locked door.
Testing asks "did it fail on the runs I tried?". Model checking asks "is there any run at all that fails?" and answers by finite search. The reason this is possible is that a finite state machine has only finitely many states, so exhaustive exploration terminates. The reason it is useful is that concurrency bugs are about order, and order is exactly what the search enumerates for free. The reason the counterexample is readable is that breadth-first search finds the shortest one.
mistakes: People often think model checking proves the real code correct. In fact it checks a model. If the model omits a detail, such as a retry or a clock skew, the proof does not cover it. The value is in checking the design, and a refinement gap remains between the model and the implementation.
People often think "all states explored, no violation" is true for any size. In fact it is true for the configuration that was checked (for example 3 nodes, 2 values, at most 1 crash). Bugs that need 5 nodes can be missed. The small-scope hypothesis makes small models useful, but it is a hypothesis.
People often think a liveness property is just a safety property that takes longer. In fact they are different in kind. A safety violation is a finite trace; a liveness violation is an infinite behaviour (a cycle) and depends on fairness. Without fairness assumptions, "eventually" fails because the scheduler may starve a process forever.
People often think the state explosion makes the method useless. In fact it limits the size of what you can check, but for the core of a protocol (a few components, a few messages) the spaces are in the millions to billions and fit in memory or on a cluster. AWS applies it to precisely this core.
People often think model checking replaces testing, or the reverse. In fact the two find different bugs. Model checking finds design-level interleaving errors in small instances; tests find implementation errors, performance problems and integration mistakes at full size. Use both.
friction: The state-space explosion is the hard limit: adding one more node or one more message can multiply the states by 10 to 100. Models must be abstract to be checkable, and choosing the abstraction needs skill. Specifications can be wrong: if you encode the wrong property or an over-constrained model (so that bad states are unreachable by mistake), the checker reports success. A common safeguard is to check that interesting states are reachable (for example, that some request actually completes).
The complexity theory gives bounds that no tool can avoid. LTL model checking is PSPACE-complete in the formula size, and checking a system given as a product of many small components is PSPACE-hard in general. Real-time and probabilistic systems need extra machinery (timed automata in UPPAAL, probabilistic model checking in PRISM) with higher costs. Unbounded domains (integers, unbounded queues, unbounded numbers of nodes) make the problem undecidable in general, and one needs abstraction, bounds or proof assistants.
There is also the human cost. Engineers must learn a specification language and a way of thinking about the system, which takes weeks. The spec must be maintained as the design changes, or it rots. The tools give little help in connecting the model to code, although techniques such as trace validation (checking that logs of the real system are valid behaviours of the spec) and deterministic simulation try to close the gap.
tradeoffs: Choose explicit-state model checking (TLC, SPIN) when the system is concurrent or distributed, the interesting core fits in a few billion states, and you need a readable counterexample: you give up completeness for larger parameters. Choose symbolic or SAT-based bounded checking when states are numerous but have regular structure (hardware, bit-level logic) and you only need bugs up to a depth: you give up a full proof unless you also find an inductive invariant.
Choose a theorem prover or deductive verifier (TLAPS, Coq, Dafny with SMT) when you need a guarantee for all sizes, such as for any number of nodes: it costs far more effort, often person-months, but holds for the infinite family. Choose model checking first to find bugs cheaply, then a proof only for the few components where it is worth the cost.
Choose randomised simulation (deterministic simulation testing, TLC in simulation mode, fuzzing) when the model is too big to enumerate. You give up the guarantee of coverage and gain the ability to run the real code, with thousands of seeds, and reproduce any failure.
Choose a lightweight spec on a small configuration for a design review of a new protocol: one to two pages, checked in minutes. Choose a full formal specification of all behaviours only for safety-critical designs such as consensus, replication or storage formats.
practice: Start with the most dangerous piece: the protocol that coordinates several machines (leader election, replication, locking, a state machine for a payment). Write down its state variables, the actions, and two or three invariants in plain language, and then translate them into TLA+ or P. Model failures as actions (a node crashes, a message is lost, duplicated or delayed) because the bugs are there.
Begin with a tiny configuration (2 or 3 nodes, 1 or 2 values, a small limit on messages), and see it pass quickly; then grow it until the checker takes tens of minutes. Use symmetry sets for identical nodes. Add liveness only after safety, and state the fairness assumption explicitly. Always make sure a "good" scenario is reachable, so a vacuous model cannot pass.
Read counterexamples carefully: each is either a real design bug, a model bug, or a missing assumption. Keep the spec next to the code, and review it as part of the design review. For implementations, add assertions that mirror the invariants, and use the spec to generate test cases or to validate recorded traces.
For deeper study, work through Lamport's Specifying Systems or the TLA+ video course, read the AWS CACM paper, and try SPIN on a small protocol such as Peterson's mutual exclusion. Clarke, Grumberg and Peled's Model Checking is the textbook for the theory.
quiz: What is the difference between a safety and a liveness property, with an example of each? || Safety says something bad never happens and is violated by a finite trace (two processes in the critical section together). Liveness says something good eventually happens and is violated only by an infinite behaviour (a request never served).
Why does breadth-first search give a readable counterexample? || BFS visits states in order of distance from the initial state, so the first violating state it finds is reached by a shortest path. The path printed from parent pointers has the fewest steps.
Why do liveness checks need a cycle search and fairness assumptions? || A liveness violation is an infinite run, which in a finite graph means a reachable cycle in which the good event never occurs. Without fairness the cycle could be one where the scheduler never runs some process, which would make every liveness property fail trivially.
A system has 4 processes, each with 5 local states, and shared state with 6 values. How many global states at most? || 5^4 × 6 = 625 × 6 = 3,750 states. Adding a fifth process raises it to 5^5 × 6 = 18,750, a factor of 5 per process.
When would you choose a theorem prover instead of a model checker? || When you need the guarantee for any number of nodes or any size of data (an unbounded family), or when the state space is infinite. You pay in effort, often person-months, in return for a proof that does not depend on a small configuration.
interview: How would you use model checking on a new leader-election protocol, and what would you model? || Model the nodes' state (term, role, votes), the actions (timeout, request vote, receive vote, become leader), the network as a set of messages that may be lost, duplicated or reordered, and crashes. Check the safety invariant "at most one leader per term" and a liveness property under fairness (a leader is eventually elected if a majority is up). Start with 3 nodes and small bounds, and treat every counterexample as a design finding.
Your TLC run passes quickly with no violations. How do you decide whether to trust it? || Check for vacuity: confirm that interesting states are reachable (a request completes, a leader is elected), try deliberately breaking the protocol and see the checker fail, and check that the constraints on the model do not hide bad states. Then increase the configuration to see whether results hold at larger sizes.
How does the automata-theoretic approach check an LTL property, and what is its complexity? || Negate the property, translate it to a Büchi automaton, form the product with the system graph, and search for a reachable cycle through an accepting state; any such cycle is a counterexample. The complexity is linear in the system size and exponential in the formula size, and the problem is PSPACE-complete overall.
Why not just test with many random schedules instead? || Random schedules sample the space and rarely hit rare orderings; a model checker covers all schedules of a bounded model and returns a minimal trace. But testing runs the real implementation at full scale. Deterministic simulation testing combines them by randomly exploring schedules of real code with replayable seeds.
What does partial-order reduction do, and when is it unsound? || It explores only one ordering of independent transitions, since they lead to the same state, which cuts the state space by large factors. It is sound when the reduction preserves the property being checked (for safety properties, ample-set conditions must hold); with liveness properties one must also avoid ignoring an enabled transition forever (the cycle proviso).
ref: paper | Design and Synthesis of Synchronization Skeletons Using Branching Time Temporal Logic | E. M. Clarke & E. A. Emerson, 1981 |
ref: paper | The Temporal Logic of Programs | Amir Pnueli, FOCS 1977 |
ref: paper | An Automata-Theoretic Approach to Automatic Program Verification | Vardi & Wolper, LICS 1986 |
ref: paper | How Amazon Web Services Uses Formal Methods | Newcombe, Rath, Zhang, Munteanu, Brooker & Deardeuff, CACM 2015 | https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/formal-methods-amazon.pdf
ref: book | Handbook of Model Checking | Clarke, Henzinger, Veith & Bloem (eds.), Springer 2018 |
ref: book | Specifying Systems | Leslie Lamport, 2002 | https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/book.html
ref: book | The SPIN Model Checker: Primer and Reference Manual | Gerard J. Holzmann, 2003 |
ref: paper | Using Lightweight Formal Methods to Validate a Key-Value Storage Node in Amazon S3 | Bornholt et al., SOSP 2021 |
@@ prng
value: A computer is a deterministic machine, yet programs need random numbers all the time: to pick a sample, shuffle a deck, split traffic for an experiment, choose a retry delay, generate a session token or an encryption key. A pseudorandom number generator (PRNG) closes this gap. It keeps a small internal state, and each call applies a fixed function that updates the state and produces an output that looks random. Given the same starting value (the seed) it always produces the same sequence, which is a feature for simulations and tests and a disaster for secrets.
There are two quite different goals behind the same idea. A statistical PRNG (Mersenne Twister, PCG, xoshiro) must pass tests for uniformity and independence and be very fast, around a nanosecond per number. A cryptographically secure PRNG (CSPRNG) must in addition be unpredictable: even an attacker who has seen millions of outputs must not be able to guess the next bit with probability better than one half, or recover the seed. A CSPRNG is typically a stream cipher such as ChaCha20 or a block cipher in counter mode, seeded with real entropy from the operating system. On Linux it is behind `getrandom()` and `/dev/urandom`.
For the layer above, randomness is a quiet dependency everywhere: TLS and key generation, session identifiers and one-time codes (authentication), randomised algorithms and sampling, random identifiers (UUIDv4, ULID), fuzzing and property-based testing, and deterministic simulation testing, where one seed must replay a whole run. The layers below make it work. Probability (L4) defines what "uniform and independent" means and gives the statistical tests; number theory (L4) provides the modular recurrences behind classic generators; information theory (L4) tells us how many bits of real unpredictability (entropy) the seed must contain and why a generator can never create entropy it was not given; and symmetric encryption (L3) supplies the ciphers that turn a short secret key into a long stream that is computationally indistinguishable from random bits.
terms: Seed — the initial value of the generator's internal state; the same seed always gives the same sequence.
State — the internal memory of the generator, updated at each step; its size bounds the period.
Period — the number of outputs before the sequence repeats.
Entropy — a measure of unpredictability, in bits; a seed with 128 bits of entropy has 2^128 equally likely values from the attacker's point of view.
CSPRNG — a generator whose output no efficient algorithm can distinguish from true random bits, even given part of the output.
Next-bit test — the standard definition of unpredictability: no efficient algorithm predicts the next output bit with probability noticeably above one half.
Reseeding — mixing fresh entropy into the state, so that a leaked state stops being dangerous after a while.
Modulo bias — the skew produced by reducing a random number with `% n` when n does not divide the range evenly.
Linear congruential generator (LCG) — the simplest classic generator: x(next) = (a × x + c) mod m.
history: John von Neumann proposed the "middle-square" method in 1946: square the number and take the middle digits. It was fast on early machines and poor in quality: it often falls into short cycles or into zero. In 1949 Derrick Lehmer introduced the linear congruential generator, and for the next fifty years most libraries used a variation of it. The danger of weak parameters became famous with IBM's RANDU in the 1960s, whose successive triples of outputs lie on only 15 planes in space, which corrupted many scientific results. Donald Knuth's The Art of Computer Programming, volume 2 (1969), set out the theory and the tests.
Quality improved with larger states. Matsumoto and Nishimura's Mersenne Twister (1997 to 1998) has a period of 2^19937 − 1 and became the default in Python, Ruby, R and many other languages. Marsaglia's xorshift (2003) showed that a few shifts and XORs make a fast generator. O'Neill's PCG family (2014) added an output permutation to an LCG, and Blackman and Vigna's xoshiro256** (2018) is a popular modern choice. Test suites matured too: Marsaglia's Diehard, then TestU01 (L'Ecuyer and Simard, 2007) with its BigCrush battery, and PractRand.
Cryptographic needs developed on a separate track. Blum, Micali and Yao (1982 to 1984) defined the theory: a generator is secure if no efficient test can tell it from random, which is equivalent to unpredictability of the next bit. Practical designs followed: Yarrow and Fortuna (Ferguson and Schneier, 2003) for entropy pools, the NIST SP 800-90A deterministic generators (CTR_DRBG, HMAC_DRBG, Hash_DRBG), and ChaCha20-based designs such as OpenBSD's `arc4random` and the Linux generator, whose 2022 rewrite (Linux 5.17 and 5.18, mainly by Jason Donenfeld) uses ChaCha20 for output and BLAKE2s for mixing entropy.
Failures shaped practice. Netscape's SSL in 1995 seeded its generator with the time and process IDs, which Goldberg and Wagner broke in 1996. In 2006 a Debian change to OpenSSL left only about 32,000 possible keys, a flaw found in 2008. The NIST generator Dual_EC_DRBG (2006) was shown to allow a hidden backdoor, was deployed in some products, and was withdrawn in 2014 after the 2013 leaks. In 2012 researchers scanning the internet found thousands of devices that generated keys at boot with too little entropy, so that different devices shared prime factors.
how: Every PRNG has the same shape: a state s, an update function f that turns s into the next state, and an output function g that turns the state into a number. Starting from a seed s0, the n-th output is g(s_n) where s_(n+1) = f(s_n). Because the state is finite (say b bits), the sequence must eventually repeat, so the period is at most 2^b. Everything else is a choice of f and g.
Statistical generators choose f for speed and equidistribution. In an LCG the update is the modular recurrence x(next) = (a × x + c) mod m, straight from number theory. The Hull–Dobell theorem says when this has the full period m: c and m must be coprime, a − 1 must be divisible by every prime factor of m, and by 4 if m is divisible by 4. LCGs with a power-of-two modulus have a known weakness: the lowest bit alternates with period 2, the lowest k bits have period 2^k, so a good generator outputs only the high bits (PCG applies a permutation to hide this). Xorshift and xoshiro use linear operations on bits (shifts and XORs) over the field with two elements, which is fast and gives huge periods, and then scramble the output with a multiplication or rotation. Mersenne Twister keeps 624 words of 32 bits (about 2.5 KB) and produces a new block with a linear recurrence; its period is enormous, but it is slow to warm up from a poor seed and it fails some linear-complexity tests.
All these functions are linear or nearly linear, and that is exactly why they are not secure. From 624 consecutive outputs of a Mersenne Twister, an attacker can solve for the full state and predict every later number. A 48-bit LCG such as Java's `java.util.Random` can be predicted from two outputs by trying the missing bits. The probability layer tells us what the generator must achieve statistically: outputs uniform on the range, and any group of outputs independent. The statistical test batteries check this by applying many tests, for example chi-squared on counts or the birthday test on collisions, and a good generator passes them all. But passing tests does not mean unpredictability, because tests look for patterns that someone has thought of.
A CSPRNG chooses f and g so that predicting the output is as hard as breaking a cipher. The usual construction takes a stream cipher. ChaCha20 (Bernstein, 2008) takes a 256-bit key, a 64-bit or 96-bit nonce and a counter, and produces a 64-byte block per counter value through 20 rounds of additions, XORs and rotations. The key is the secret state. The symmetric encryption layer supplies the guarantee: if ChaCha20 is a secure cipher, its keystream is indistinguishable from random bits for anyone who does not know the key, so observing outputs reveals nothing about the key or the next output. The same holds for AES in counter mode. Modern designs add "fast key erasure": after producing a buffer of output, the generator takes the first 32 bytes of the keystream as the next key and overwrites the old one, so a later compromise of the memory does not reveal past outputs (backtracking resistance).
The seed is the weak point, and here information theory enters. A deterministic function cannot create entropy: if the seed has only 20 bits of entropy (for example the time in seconds mixed with a process ID), the output has at most 20 bits of unpredictability, whatever the cipher, because the attacker can enumerate 2^20 seeds. A CSPRNG therefore needs at least 128 bits of entropy from physical sources: interrupt timing, disk and network jitter, hardware instructions (RDRAND, RDSEED on x86, similar on ARM), and the boot loader's seed file. The kernel collects these into a pool, hashes them together, and seeds the generator once it estimates enough entropy. After that, the generator can produce unlimited output; there is no need to keep "using up" entropy, which is why `/dev/urandom` and `getrandom()` are fine for all purposes once initialised. Since Linux 5.6, `/dev/random` also blocks only until the generator is initialised at boot.
Practical use adds a few more pieces. To turn raw bits into a number in 0 to n−1, never use `x % n` for a large n: if the range of x (say 2^32) is not a multiple of n, some results are slightly more likely. Use rejection sampling: discard values from the incomplete top part of the range. Lemire's method does this with one multiplication and rarely a retry. To generate floating-point numbers in [0, 1), take the top 53 bits and multiply by 2^-53. To make many independent streams in parallel (for simulation threads), do not seed several generators with consecutive integers; use a splittable generator (SplitMix, counter-based generators such as Philox) or jump functions that skip far ahead in the sequence.
diagram:
  STATISTICAL PRNG (fast, predictable)

     seed --> [ state s ] --f--> [ state s' ] --f--> ...
                  |                  |
                  g                  g
                  v                  v
               output 1           output 2
     example f: s' = (a*s + c) mod m          period <= 2^(state bits)

  CSPRNG (stream cipher + entropy)

   boot jitter,  --> [ entropy pool ] --hash--> key K (256 bits)
   interrupts,                                    |
   RDRAND, seed                        ChaCha20(K, counter, nonce)
   file                                           |
                                      64-byte blocks of keystream
                                       |                  |
                         first 32 bytes -> new key K'    rest -> output
                                  (old key erased)

  rule: output bits are only as unpredictable as the entropy in K
example: Take a tiny LCG with modulus m = 16, multiplier a = 5, increment c = 3, and seed x0 = 7. The recurrence is x(next) = (5x + 3) mod 16. The sequence is 7, then (35 + 3) mod 16 = 6, then (30 + 3) mod 16 = 1, then (5 + 3) mod 16 = 8, then (40 + 3) mod 16 = 11, then 10, 5, 12, 15, 14, 9, 0, 3, 2, 13, 4, and then back to 7. All 16 values appear exactly once before the repeat, so the period is 16 (the Hull–Dobell conditions hold: 3 and 16 are coprime, and a − 1 = 4 is divisible by 2 and by 4). The generator looks fine in a histogram: every value appears equally often.
Now look at the low bit: 7, 6, 1, 8, 11, 10, 5, 12 gives 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0. It strictly alternates. If you used `x % 2` as a coin flip, you would get heads, tails, heads, tails. The lowest two bits have period 4, the lowest three have period 8. This is the reason real LCGs output only the high bits. Also notice that the whole state is the output: after seeing one value, say 11, an attacker can compute every following value, 10, 5, 12, and so on. A generator is only unpredictable if the output reveals little about the state, and for an LCG with a 16-bit state it reveals everything.
Next the entropy of seeding. Suppose a service seeds its generator with the current Unix time in seconds, and generates a 128-bit session token. The attacker knows the service started in some one-hour window. That is 3,600 possible seeds, so about 12 bits of entropy. The attacker generates the token sequence for each of the 3,600 seeds and checks them against one observed token. The 128-bit token has the entropy of the 12-bit seed. With a seed from the operating system (256 bits of entropy) the same search needs 2^256 attempts.
Last, modulo bias. A generator produces a byte in 0 to 255 and you want a die roll 0 to 5 with `b % 6`. Since 256 = 6 × 42 + 4, the values 0 to 3 appear 43 times and 4 to 5 appear 42 times: probabilities 43/256 = 16.80% against 42/256 = 16.41%. With rejection sampling you discard bytes 252 to 255 and draw again (a 4/256 = 1.6% chance of a retry), which makes all six outcomes exactly equal. With 32-bit draws the effect is tiny (2^32 mod 6 = 4, so a relative bias near one in a billion), but for modular reduction of large n, or for shuffles of large arrays, it can matter.
```python
import os, secrets, random

def die_roll_biased():
    return os.urandom(1)[0] % 6      # slight modulo bias

def die_roll_exact():
    while True:
        b = os.urandom(1)[0]
        if b < 252:                  # 252 = 6 * 42
            return b % 6

token = secrets.token_hex(16)    # 128-bit secret from the OS
rng = random.Random(42)          # repeatable, NOT secret
```
intuition: A very long, scrambled recipe book that you read from a starting page given by the seed: the book is fixed, but without knowing the page (and the scrambling) you cannot tell what comes next.
The insight is that "random-looking" is a property that can be defined by what an observer can do. A statistical generator only has to fool statistical tests. A secure generator has to fool every efficient algorithm, which is the same as a good cipher having to fool every efficient attacker. This is why the CSPRNG is just a stream cipher with a key from the world: the cipher stretches a few real random bits into as many as you want, and the difficulty of breaking the cipher is the difficulty of predicting the output.
It also explains the limit. A function cannot add information, so the unpredictability of the output equals the entropy of the seed plus nothing. The art of building a CSPRNG is collecting enough real entropy once and then never revealing the key.
mistakes: People often think a generator that passes statistical tests is secure. In fact the Mersenne Twister passes almost every statistical test and is still completely predictable from 624 outputs. Statistical quality and unpredictability are different properties.
People often think `random()` or `Math.random()` is fine for tokens, passwords or keys. In fact the default generators in most languages (Python `random`, Java `Random`, JavaScript `Math.random`, C `rand`) are not designed to be secret. Use `secrets`, `SecureRandom`, `crypto.getRandomValues` or `getrandom()`.
People often think `/dev/urandom` is weaker than `/dev/random` and that you should avoid it. In fact on a modern Linux, once the system has booted and the generator is initialised, they behave the same; the old idea that entropy is "used up" does not apply to a good CSPRNG. The real risk is early boot, before initialisation, and `getrandom()` handles it by blocking until the generator is ready.
People often think seeding with the time makes it random enough. In fact the time has little entropy, because an attacker knows roughly when the program started. The same applies to process IDs and counters. Seeds must come from the operating system.
People often think more output "uses up" randomness, or that you need to reseed after each use. In fact a good CSPRNG can produce a vast stream from one 256-bit seed (the original ChaCha20 with a 64-bit counter has a keystream of 2^70 bytes per key and nonce). Reseeding is for recovery after a possible state compromise, and for fork or VM-snapshot safety, not for refreshing the stream.
People often think a hash of a short secret is random. In fact hashing spreads the bits but adds no entropy; hash(small number) can be enumerated.
friction: The weak spots are at the edges, not in the maths. Early boot is the hardest case: embedded devices and virtual machines may have almost no entropy at first start, and generate long-lived keys right away. Cloned virtual machine images and container snapshots start with the same generator state and produce the same "random" values. A process that forks must make sure parent and child do not continue with the same state (OpenSSL had a long history of this problem, and modern kernels offer `MADV_WIPEONFORK` and a VM generation ID for it).
Trust in hardware sources is a problem. RDRAND is fast but is a black box, and the Linux kernel treats it as one input to be mixed, not the only source. Dual_EC_DRBG showed that a standard can contain a backdoor. Entropy estimation is hard: a source that looks random may be predictable, and there is no general test that proves a stream has high entropy. NIST SP 800-90B gives procedures for estimating it conservatively.
Performance and reproducibility pull in the other direction. A CSPRNG costs a few cycles per byte, which is fine for keys but noticeable for Monte Carlo simulations with billions of draws, where a fast statistical generator is right. Reproducibility across languages, versions and CPU counts is fragile: if the standard library changes its algorithm, a "seeded" experiment changes results; parallel code that shares one generator gives different results per run depending on thread timing.
Finally, nobody can prove that a CSPRNG is secure, because that would prove that P differs from NP or, more precisely, that one-way functions exist. Security rests on years of failed attacks on the underlying cipher.
tradeoffs: Choose the operating system CSPRNG (`getrandom()`, `/dev/urandom`, `SecureRandom`, `crypto.getRandomValues`) for anything secret: keys, tokens, nonces, salts, IDs that must not be guessed. It costs on the order of tens to hundreds of nanoseconds per call plus a system call in some libraries, and you give up reproducibility.
Choose a fast statistical generator (PCG, xoshiro256**, Philox) for simulation, sampling, shuffling and testing, where you want speed (under a nanosecond per number) and reproducibility from a seed. You give up any protection against a determined adversary, so never expose its outputs where guessing matters, for example as an ID that guards access.
Choose a counter-based or splittable generator when you run many parallel streams and need each to be independent and repeatable irrespective of the thread count. Choose a single shared generator behind a lock only for small, non-critical uses.
Choose a seeded generator in tests and in deterministic simulation, and log the seed, so every failure can be replayed. Choose a fresh OS-seeded generator in production for the same code. The deciding factor is who must not be able to predict the output.
Choose hash-based sampling (a hash of the ID compared to a threshold) instead of a random coin when the decision must be consistent for the same ID across services, as in trace sampling or A/B bucketing; choose a random draw when each event is independent.
practice: Use the right API by purpose. For secrets: Python `secrets`, Java `SecureRandom`, Go `crypto/rand`, Node `crypto.randomBytes`, Rust `getrandom` or the `rand` crate's `OsRng`. For simulation: NumPy's `Generator` with PCG64 (`numpy.random.default_rng(seed)`), not the legacy global state. Never invent your own generator, and never use a time-based seed for anything that matters.
For randomised tests and simulations, print the seed at the start of every run and accept it as a parameter, so a failing run can be reproduced exactly. In parallel code, give each worker its own stream derived from the master seed (for example `SeedSequence.spawn` in NumPy, or a `jump()` in xoshiro) instead of seeding with `seed + worker_id`.
For choosing a number in a range or shuffling, use the library's `randrange`, `shuffle`, `nextInt(bound)` which handle bias, and do not write `rand() % n` unless the bias is acceptable. For identifiers, UUIDv4 has 122 random bits; if you need sortable IDs, ULID or UUIDv7 combine a timestamp with random bits, and the random part still must come from a CSPRNG if guessability matters.
Operationally, in containers and VMs, check that the entropy source is available at boot (virtio-rng, a seed file) and that snapshots are re-seeded when restored. In security reviews, search the code for `rand()`, `Math.random`, `new Random(` and time-based seeds near tokens, passwords and keys.
quiz: What is the difference between a statistical PRNG and a CSPRNG? || A statistical PRNG only needs to pass statistical tests and be fast; its output can be predicted from its state or from a few outputs. A CSPRNG must be unpredictable to any efficient attacker even after seeing a lot of output, and needs a seed with at least about 128 bits of real entropy.
Why can't a generator with a 20-bit-entropy seed produce a secure 128-bit token? || A deterministic function adds no entropy. The attacker can try all 2^20 (about a million) seeds, generate each token, and compare, so the token is only as hard to guess as the seed.
Why does the low bit of an LCG with a power-of-two modulus alternate, and what is the practical fix? || With modulus 2^k, the lowest bit of a × x + c depends only on the lowest bit of x, so it flips with period 2 (period 2^j for the low j bits). Use the high bits as output, or add a scrambling output function as PCG does.
You need a uniform number from 0 to 99 from a random byte. How do you avoid modulo bias, and what is the retry probability? || Discard bytes 200 to 255 (the incomplete group, since 256 = 2 × 100 + 56), then use b % 100. The retry probability is 56/256, about 21.9%. The values 0 to 199 each map to every residue exactly twice.
When would you use a seeded Mersenne Twister or PCG in production code even though it is predictable? || For simulations, sampling and tests where reproducibility and speed matter and no adversary benefits from predicting the numbers. Never for secrets, access control or anything an attacker gains from guessing.
interview: A service creates password-reset tokens with `Random(System.currentTimeMillis())`. Explain the attack and the fix. || The seed has very little entropy, since the attacker knows roughly when the process started or the token was issued and can try each millisecond; `java.util.Random` is also an LCG whose state follows from two outputs. The attacker regenerates the tokens and takes over accounts. Fix: use `SecureRandom` (OS seeded) to produce at least 128 random bits and store only a hash of the token with an expiry.
Why is it a problem to snapshot a VM or fork a process that has a CSPRNG in memory? || Both copies continue with the same internal state, so they produce identical "random" values: duplicate nonces, keys or session IDs. Fixes: reseed after fork or restore (VM generation IDs, `MADV_WIPEONFORK`, `getrandom()` called after the event), and avoid caching random buffers across snapshots.
How does a Linux-style CSPRNG collect and use entropy, and why is there no need to block once it is initialised? || It mixes timing jitter, interrupts, hardware RNG output and a saved seed into a pool using a hash function, and seeds a ChaCha20-based generator once it has enough entropy (around 256 bits). A secure stream cipher expands that seed into practically unlimited output, and recovering the key would need breaking the cipher, so entropy is not consumed by output. Blocking is only needed before first initialisation.
How would you generate independent random streams for 1,000 parallel simulation workers, reproducibly? || Derive them from a master seed with a splittable construction: NumPy `SeedSequence.spawn`, a counter-based generator (Philox) with the worker ID as part of the key, or jump-ahead functions that give non-overlapping substreams. Avoid seeding with consecutive integers, which may give correlated streams in weak generators.
What would convince you that a generator is secure, and what cannot be proven? || Using a well-studied cipher or hash construction with an analysed reduction, vetted implementations, and entropy from the OS. Statistical tests can only detect failures, never prove security. Absolute proofs would require proving that one-way functions exist, which is an open problem.
ref: book | The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 2: Seminumerical Algorithms, ch. 3 (Random Numbers) | Donald E. Knuth, 3rd ed. 1997 |
ref: paper | Mersenne Twister: A 623-Dimensionally Equidistributed Uniform Pseudo-Random Number Generator | Matsumoto & Nishimura, ACM TOMACS 1998 |
ref: paper | PCG: A Family of Simple Fast Space-Efficient Statistically Good Algorithms for Random Number Generation | Melissa E. O'Neill, 2014 | https://www.pcg-random.org/paper.html
ref: paper | Scrambled Linear Pseudorandom Number Generators | David Blackman & Sebastiano Vigna, ACM TOMS 2021 | https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.01407
ref: paper | TestU01: A C Library for Empirical Testing of Random Number Generators | L'Ecuyer & Simard, ACM TOMS 2007 |
ref: book | Cryptography Engineering (ch. 9, Generating Randomness: Fortuna) | Ferguson, Schneier & Kohno, 2010 |
ref: paper | Mining Your Ps and Qs: Detection of Widespread Weak Keys in Network Devices | Heninger, Durumeric, Wustrow & Halderman, USENIX Security 2012 |
ref: docs | getrandom(2) manual page | Linux man-pages project | https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/getrandom.2.html
@@ approxalgo
value: Many practical optimisation problems are NP-hard: packing pods onto nodes, scheduling jobs on machines, choosing which servers cover all data, planning a delivery route. No known algorithm solves them exactly in polynomial time, and most experts believe none exists. An approximation algorithm gives up exact optimality and in return gives two promises: it runs in polynomial time, and its answer is provably within a fixed factor of the best possible. A "2-approximation" for a minimisation problem never returns a solution more than twice as costly as the optimum, on every input, including adversarial ones.
That second promise is what separates an approximation algorithm from a heuristic. A heuristic usually works; an approximation algorithm comes with a proof. This is valuable in systems work because the worst case is the one that causes outages. If a bin-packing scheduler is known to use at most 11/9 of the optimal number of machines plus a constant, a capacity planner can budget for it, and a surprise cannot be larger than the proof allows.
For the layer above, schedulers and orchestrators (Borg, Kubernetes) pack tasks onto machines by approximate bin packing; DAG schedulers use list scheduling, a 2-approximation for finishing time; network and storage designers use greedy set cover and facility-location algorithms. The layers below make the idea work. P vs NP (L3) tells us why exactness is hopeless in general, and its hardness proofs also show how far approximation can go. Greedy and divide-and-conquer (L3) provide the simplest algorithms and the analysis habits behind them. Linear programming (L4) provides relaxations and duality: a cheap-to-solve version of the problem that gives a lower bound on the optimum. Integer programming (L4) defines the true optimum that the bounds are compared against and the branch-and-bound method for exact solutions when the instance is small enough.
terms: Approximation ratio — the worst-case ratio between the algorithm's value and the optimal value; for minimisation, ALG ≤ ρ × OPT with ρ ≥ 1.
OPT — the value of the best possible solution, which we usually cannot compute.
Lower bound — a number that is certainly at most OPT (for minimisation) and is easy to compute; analysis compares ALG with it.
LP relaxation — the integer program with the integrality constraints dropped (variables may be fractions), solvable in polynomial time.
Integrality gap — the worst-case ratio between the integer optimum and the LP optimum; it limits what any LP-based method can prove.
Rounding — turning a fractional LP solution into an integer solution while controlling the loss.
PTAS and FPTAS — a (fully) polynomial-time approximation scheme gives ratio 1 + ε for any ε you choose, with run time polynomial in the input (and, for an FPTAS, also in 1/ε).
APX-hard — as hard as the problems that cannot be approximated arbitrarily well unless P = NP.
Maximal matching — a set of edges, no two sharing a vertex, to which no further edge can be added.
history: The field started with a scheduling paper. In 1966 Ronald Graham analysed "list scheduling" on identical machines: put each job on the currently least-loaded machine. He proved the finishing time is at most 2 − 1/m times the optimum (m machines), the first worst-case guarantee for a polynomial heuristic. In 1969 he showed that sorting jobs longest first improves the factor to 4/3 − 1/(3m). In the early 1970s, after Cook and Karp (1971 to 1972) showed that hundreds of problems are NP-complete, the question became how well they can be solved approximately. David Johnson's 1974 paper "Approximation Algorithms for Combinatorial Problems" analysed bin packing, set cover, and others, and set the style of the field. Johnson and others proved that First Fit Decreasing bin packing uses at most 11/9 × OPT + 6/9 bins. Christofides (1976) gave a 3/2-approximation for metric travelling salesman, which stood as the best known for 45 years until Karlin, Klein and Oveis Gharan (2021) improved it by a tiny constant.
Two developments in the 1990s changed the picture. The PCP theorem (Arora, Lund, Motwani, Sudan and Szegedy, 1992) showed that for many problems even approximating is NP-hard, giving a theory of lower bounds. For example Feige (1998) proved that set cover cannot be approximated better than about ln n, which the greedy algorithm already achieves. On the algorithm side, linear and semidefinite programming became tools: Goemans and Williamson (1995) rounded a semidefinite relaxation to get a 0.878-approximation for max-cut, and primal–dual methods gave simple proofs. Vazirani's book (2001) and Williamson and Shmoys's (2011) made the field teachable.
how: The setup. A minimisation problem has an optimal value OPT that is NP-hard to compute. We want an algorithm with ALG ≤ ρ × OPT. We cannot compare with OPT directly, so every proof uses a stand-in, a quantity LB that is easy to compute and satisfies LB ≤ OPT. Then the proof has two steps: show ALG ≤ ρ × LB, and conclude ALG ≤ ρ × OPT. Finding a good lower bound is the creative part. Where it comes from is the main classification of techniques.
Greedy with a combinatorial bound. Vertex cover asks for the smallest set of vertices touching every edge. Greedy algorithm: find a maximal matching M (scan the edges, take an edge if neither endpoint is used yet), and output all endpoints, 2|M| vertices. This is a cover, because if some edge were uncovered, both its endpoints would be free and the edge could have been added to M, contradicting maximality. The lower bound: the matching edges share no vertices, so any cover needs a different vertex for each, so OPT ≥ |M|. Hence ALG = 2|M| ≤ 2 OPT. The algorithm is simple and the proof is two lines. The greedy layer provides the idea (take what is locally forced) and the analysis habit (charge cost to a bound).
Greedy for set cover. We must cover n elements using the fewest sets. Repeatedly pick the set that covers the most uncovered elements. Analysis: charge each newly covered element 1/(number of elements newly covered by that pick). Over time, the optimal solution's k sets cover all remaining elements, so some set covers at least a 1/k fraction of what remains; the number of uncovered elements shrinks by a factor (1 − 1/k) each round, giving about k ln n rounds. So the ratio is H_n = 1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n ≈ ln n + 1. This is the best possible up to lower-order terms, unless P = NP (Feige 1998; Dinur and Steurer 2014).
LP relaxation and rounding. Write vertex cover as an integer program: a variable x_v ∈ {0, 1} for each vertex, minimise the sum of x_v subject to x_u + x_v ≥ 1 for each edge. Relax to 0 ≤ x_v ≤ 1, which is a linear program that can be solved in polynomial time. Its optimum is at most OPT because every integer solution is also a fractional one, so LP ≤ OPT is our lower bound. Rounding: put v in the cover if x_v ≥ 1/2. Every edge has x_u + x_v ≥ 1, so one endpoint is at least 1/2: the result is a cover. Each chosen vertex contributes x_v ≥ 1/2 to the LP value, so the cover has size at most 2 × LP ≤ 2 × OPT. The integrality gap shows the limit of this method: on the complete graph with n vertices, the LP can set every x_v = 1/2 for value n/2, while OPT = n − 1 (all but one vertex), a gap approaching 2. No LP-based argument can beat 2 for this relaxation. Duality gives a third view: the dual of the LP is a fractional matching, and the maximal-matching bound above is a feasible dual solution; primal–dual algorithms build primal and dual solutions together and compare them.
Local search and scheduling. List scheduling: assign each job to the machine with the least load. Let job j be the one that finishes last, at time C. When j was placed, its machine had the least load, at most (total work)/m. So C ≤ total/m + (1 − 1/m) p_j. Both terms are bounded by OPT: total/m ≤ OPT (average load), and p_j ≤ OPT (the job must run somewhere). So C ≤ (2 − 1/m) OPT. Sorting jobs longest first (LPT) shrinks the last job's size relative to OPT and improves the ratio to 4/3 − 1/(3m).
Approximation schemes. For some problems ratio 1 + ε is possible for any ε. Knapsack has an FPTAS: scale and round the profits so they become small integers, then run exact dynamic programming over profit values. Rounding loses at most an ε fraction of the optimum, and the table size is polynomial in n and 1/ε. This is divide and conquer's cousin, dynamic programming with controlled rounding.
Limits. The same hardness machinery that gives NP-hardness also gives hardness of approximation. General travelling salesman has no constant-factor approximation unless P = NP (otherwise you could decide Hamiltonian cycle), but metric TSP does. Bin packing cannot be approximated better than 3/2 in the absolute sense (it would solve the partition problem), yet it has an asymptotic scheme where the error is ε × OPT plus a constant. For vertex cover, going below 1.36 is NP-hard, and below 2 is hard under the Unique Games Conjecture. Knowing these boundaries tells you when to stop looking for a better algorithm.
diagram:
  minimisation: values on a line (smaller is better)

   LB         OPT                         ALG            2*LB
    |----------|---------------------------|---------------|
    ^          ^                           ^
    easy to    true optimum,               algorithm's answer
    compute    NP-hard to find
               proof:  ALG <= rho * LB <= rho * OPT

  Where does LB come from?
  +-----------------+-------------------------+----------------------+
  | technique       | lower bound LB          | example              |
  +-----------------+-------------------------+----------------------+
  | greedy          | combinatorial witness   | matching <= vertex   |
  |                 | (disjoint edges)        | cover  (ratio 2)     |
  | LP rounding     | LP optimum <= OPT       | x_v >= 1/2 -> pick   |
  | primal-dual     | any feasible dual value | set cover, Steiner   |
  | average / max   | total/m and max job     | list scheduling      |
  +-----------------+-------------------------+----------------------+
example: Part 1, vertex cover on a path a-b-c-d-e-f with edges ab, bc, cd, de, ef. The optimum is 3: the edges ab, cd, ef share no vertex, so any cover needs at least 3 vertices, and {b, d, e} is a cover of size 3. Run the matching algorithm. If it scans the edges in the order bc, de, then ab (blocked: b used), cd (blocked), ef (blocked), the matching is {bc, de}. The cover is {b, c, d, e}: size 4, ratio 4/3. If it scans in the order ab, cd, ef, the matching has 3 edges and the cover has all 6 vertices: ratio 6/3 = 2, exactly the guarantee. The bound is tight, and the order of the scan decides whether you are lucky. The lower bound is visible: the matching {ab, cd, ef} proves OPT ≥ 3.
Part 2, scheduling five jobs of lengths 3, 3, 2, 2, 2 on m = 2 machines. The optimum is 6: machine 1 runs 3 + 3 and machine 2 runs 2 + 2 + 2. Longest-first list scheduling does this: job 3 goes to machine 1 (load 3), the second 3 goes to machine 2 (load 3), the first 2 goes to machine 1 (load 5), the second 2 goes to machine 2 (load 5), the last 2 goes to machine 1 (load 7). The result is 7, a ratio 7/6 ≈ 1.167. Graham's bound for m = 2 is 4/3 − 1/6 = 7/6, so this input is exactly the worst case. Plain list scheduling in the order 2, 2, 2, 3, 3 also gives 7: 2 to M1, 2 to M2, 2 to M1 (load 4), 3 to M2 (5), 3 to M1 (7). Its guarantee is weaker (2 − 1/2 = 3/2), so on other inputs it can be further from the optimum.
Part 3, the cost of exactness. A cluster scheduler must place 1,000 pods on nodes. An exact bin-packing solver (integer program with branch and bound) may take seconds on 50 pods and hours on 1,000, with no time guarantee. First Fit Decreasing sorts pods by size and puts each in the first node where it fits: O(n log n) time, under a millisecond for 1,000 pods, with at most 11/9 × OPT + 6/9 nodes. If the optimum is 90 nodes, FFD uses at most 11/9 × 90 + 0.67 = 110.67, so at most 110 nodes. In practice it is usually within a few percent, because real instances are easier than the worst case. The guarantee is what lets the planner reserve 22% headroom with confidence.
```python
def vertex_cover_2approx(edges):
    cover, used = set(), set()
    for u, v in edges:            # greedy maximal matching
        if u not in used and v not in used:
            used.update((u, v))
            cover.update((u, v))  # take both endpoints
    return cover

path = [("a","b"), ("c","d"), ("e","f"), ("b","c"), ("d","e")]
print(sorted(vertex_cover_2approx(path)))  # all six vertices
```
intuition: Measuring a field you cannot walk by pacing a fence you can: you do not know the exact area, but a cheap bound sandwiches it, and the algorithm's answer is proved to sit near the bound.
The central idea is that proving closeness does not require knowing the optimum. It only requires something computable that cannot exceed the optimum, and an argument that your answer cannot exceed that something by more than a factor. Hard problems usually have easy relaxations: a matching ignores the structure of the cover, an LP ignores integrality, an average ignores indivisibility. The approximation ratio measures how much that ignorance can cost.
mistakes: People often think an approximation algorithm is "a heuristic with a nice name". In fact the difference is a worst-case proof. A heuristic can fail badly on some input and give no warning; an approximation algorithm cannot exceed its ratio on any input.
People often think the ratio tells you the typical quality. In fact it is the worst case, usually reached only by contrived inputs. First Fit Decreasing has a worst-case ratio of 11/9, yet on real workloads it is often within 1 to 3 percent of optimal. A good ratio is a safety guarantee, and a poor ratio does not mean poor behaviour in practice.
People often think any NP-hard problem can be approximated well if you try harder. In fact some cannot be approximated within any constant factor unless P = NP (general travelling salesman, clique within n^(1-ε)), and others have sharp thresholds (set cover at ln n, vertex cover at about 1.36 unconditionally). The hardness theory tells you when to stop.
People often think that rounding an LP solution always gives an integer solution that is nearly optimal. In fact the integrality gap bounds what is possible: for vertex cover, the LP can be a factor 2 below the true optimum, so no rounding of that LP can prove better than 2. A stronger relaxation (semidefinite programming, lift-and-project) is needed to go further, if it is possible at all.
People often think "polynomial time" implies "fast enough". In fact some approximation schemes (PTAS) run in time like n^(1/ε), which is useless at ε = 0.01. Practical algorithms are usually simple greedy or local-search methods with moderate ratios.
friction: The guarantees can be weak or pessimistic. A factor of 2 on cluster cost is large money, and the worst case rarely shows up, so engineers often prefer an exact or near-exact method on real data. Conversely, a heuristic without a guarantee may beat the approximation algorithm on every instance you see and still fail on the one that matters. The gap between theory and practice is the central tension.
Real problems add constraints that break the clean models. Pods have CPU, memory, GPU, disk and network needs (vector bin packing, where the best known ratios are worse and the problem is harder to approximate), plus affinity, anti-affinity, priorities and moving costs. Online settings, where items arrive without knowledge of the future, have their own ratios (First Fit is 1.7-competitive, and no online algorithm beats about 1.54). Objectives are often multi-dimensional, which a single ratio does not capture.
There is also a modelling risk. The approximation guarantee is relative to the model's optimum. If the objective is a poor proxy for what you want (cost ignoring fragmentation and migration), a 1.1-approximation of the wrong thing is still wrong. And proofs of ratios often depend on exact arithmetic and on assumptions (metric distances, triangle inequality) that real data can violate.
Finally, the theory has open problems: whether vertex cover can be approximated below 2 (it cannot if the Unique Games Conjecture is true), whether metric TSP has a 4/3-approximation (widely conjectured), and whether bin packing can always be solved within OPT + 1 bins. These affect how much effort is worth spending.
tradeoffs: Choose a simple provable approximation (greedy, list scheduling, FFD) when you need predictable run time (milliseconds), an answer on every input, and a worst-case bound for planning. Choose an exact solver (integer programming with branch and bound, or SAT/SMT) when the instance is small (dozens to a few hundred decision variables, depending on structure), the cost of suboptimality is large, and you can afford unpredictable run time. You give up either speed and predictability, or optimality.
Choose LP rounding or a primal–dual method when you also want the lower bound: solving the LP tells you how far from optimal any answer can be, which is useful to report ("within 8% of the best possible") even if the final answer comes from a heuristic. You pay for solving a large LP, which is slower than greedy.
Choose local search or metaheuristics (simulated annealing, genetic algorithms) when the real objective is messy, constraints are many, and you can accept no guarantee; run them from a greedy start so the result is no worse than the proven one. You give up the proof and gain flexibility.
Choose an online algorithm with a competitive ratio when decisions must be made as items arrive (scheduling pods as they are submitted). Choose offline sorting (FFD, LPT) when you can batch the decisions: sorting alone improves the ratio, here from 1.7 to 11/9 for bin packing and from 2 to 4/3 for scheduling.
practice: When you meet an optimisation problem, first check whether it is a known NP-hard problem in disguise (bin packing, set cover, scheduling, knapsack, travelling salesman, graph partitioning). Searching for the name saves months, and the literature lists the best ratios and the standard algorithm. If the instance is small, try an exact solver (OR-Tools, HiGHS, Gurobi, a SAT solver) first; the answer and its proven gap show how much room a heuristic has.
Report both a solution and a bound. Run the greedy algorithm and also compute an easy lower bound (total demand divided by node capacity for packing; the LP value for covering). If the two are within a few percent, stop optimising. In scheduler design, put the proven algorithm (sort by size, best fit) in as the baseline and measure improvements against it.
Test the worst case on purpose. Generate adversarial inputs (many items just over half the bin size, one very long job after many short ones) to see how far the algorithm can fall. Watch for the dimensions that matter in production: multi-resource packing, fragmentation, and the cost of moving things.
To study, read the first chapters of Williamson and Shmoys, The Design of Approximation Algorithms (free online), solve the vertex cover, set cover and scheduling proofs by hand, and use OR-Tools to compare a greedy result to the LP bound on random instances.
quiz: Why does the maximal-matching algorithm give a 2-approximation for vertex cover? || Its output has 2|M| vertices and covers every edge (otherwise the edge could extend M). Any cover needs one distinct vertex per matching edge, since the edges share no vertices, so OPT ≥ |M| and ALG = 2|M| ≤ 2 OPT.
What is an integrality gap and why does it matter? || It is the worst-case ratio between the integer optimum and the LP-relaxation optimum. Any proof that uses the LP value as lower bound cannot show a ratio better than the gap; for vertex cover it is about 2.
Why does sorting jobs longest-first improve list scheduling? || In the analysis, the last job to finish adds its length to a nearly-average load. When jobs are sorted, that last job is small, so the extra term shrinks relative to OPT, and the ratio drops from 2 − 1/m to 4/3 − 1/(3m).
Jobs of length 3, 3, 2, 2, 2 on 2 machines: what are OPT, the longest-first result and the ratio? || OPT = 6 (3 + 3 against 2 + 2 + 2). Longest-first gives 7. The ratio is 7/6, which equals Graham's bound 4/3 − 1/6.
When is a 2-approximation not good enough, and what would you do? || When the cost of a factor of two is large and the instance is small enough to solve better: use an exact integer-programming or SAT solver, or an LP-based method with local search, and report the gap to the lower bound.
interview: How would you pack pods onto nodes so that the number of nodes is close to minimum, and what can you guarantee? || This is vector bin packing. Sort by the dominant resource and use first fit or best fit decreasing, which has a provable ratio in one dimension (11/9 OPT + 6/9) and weaker ratios in several. Report a lower bound (total demand over node capacity per resource) and track the gap. Add constraints (affinity, spreading) as filters and accept loss of the guarantee when they bind.
Prove that list scheduling is a (2 − 1/m)-approximation for makespan. || Let job j finish last at time C. When it started, its machine had the least load, at most (total − p_j)/m. So C ≤ (total − p_j)/m + p_j = total/m + (1 − 1/m) p_j. Since total/m ≤ OPT and p_j ≤ OPT, C ≤ (2 − 1/m) OPT.
Why can't we approximate general travelling salesman within a constant factor, but we can for metric TSP? || With arbitrary weights, a constant-factor algorithm could be used to decide Hamiltonian cycle (give existing edges weight 1 and missing edges a huge weight), which is NP-complete. The triangle inequality in the metric case allows shortcuts: MST doubling gives 2, Christofides gives 3/2.
What is the difference between an approximation algorithm and an online algorithm's competitive ratio? || Approximation algorithms have the whole input but limited time; the loss comes from computational hardness. Online algorithms have unlimited computation but see the input piece by piece; the loss comes from lack of information. Both are worst-case ratios against OPT.
How would you use an LP relaxation in practice even if you never round it? || Solve the LP to get a lower bound for the minimum cost. Compare your heuristic's result with it to report a certified gap, to decide when to stop optimising, and to guide branch-and-bound or column generation.
ref: book | The Design of Approximation Algorithms | David P. Williamson & David B. Shmoys, Cambridge University Press, 2011 | https://www.designofapproxalgs.com
ref: book | Approximation Algorithms | Vijay V. Vazirani, Springer, 2001 |
ref: paper | Bounds for Certain Multiprocessing Anomalies | Ronald L. Graham, Bell System Technical Journal, 1966 |
ref: paper | Approximation Algorithms for Combinatorial Problems | David S. Johnson, JCSS 1974 |
ref: paper | A Threshold of ln n for Approximating Set Cover | Uriel Feige, JACM 1998 |
ref: paper | A (Slightly) Improved Approximation Algorithm for Metric TSP | Karlin, Klein & Oveis Gharan, STOC 2021 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.01409
ref: book | Algorithm Design (ch. 11, Approximation Algorithms) | Kleinberg & Tardos, 2005 |
ref: paper | Large-scale cluster management at Google with Borg | Verma et al., EuroSys 2015 |
@@ heuristicsearch
value: A* (pronounced "A star") finds a cheapest path from a start state to a goal state while exploring far fewer states than blind search. Dijkstra's algorithm expands states in order of their cost so far, which means it spreads out in every direction like a ripple, including directions that lead away from the goal. A* adds a guess of how far each state still is from the goal, called the heuristic, and expands the state with the lowest "cost so far plus guessed cost to go". The ripple is bent toward the goal. If the guess is never too high (never overestimates), A* still returns a path that is exactly optimal.
This turns an intractable search into a tractable one in many real settings. A road router cannot afford to explore a whole continent to find a route across a city, but with straight-line distance as the guess it explores a narrow band along the way. A game character finds a path across a map in microseconds. A puzzle solver finds the shortest solution of the 15-puzzle, whose state space has about 10^13 states, by expanding a tiny fraction of them. A planner or a query optimizer treats "which sequence of actions" or "which join order" as a path in a huge graph and uses cost estimates to avoid exploring hopeless parts.
For the layer above, A* and its relatives are the core of automated planning, motion planning for robots (grid and lattice planners), and the search over plans in query optimizers with cost-based pruning. The layers below supply the pieces. Graph algorithms (L3) provide the model (states as nodes, actions as weighted edges) and Dijkstra's algorithm, of which A* is a small modification. Trees, heaps and tries (L3) provide the priority queue (a binary heap with O(log n) insert and extract-min) that holds the frontier. Geometry and metric spaces (L4) provide the source of good heuristics: straight-line distance is a metric, it obeys the triangle inequality, and so it can never exceed the length of a real path.
terms: State space — the graph whose nodes are the possible situations (a board position, a map location) and whose edges are the allowed moves with their costs.
g(n) — the cost of the best path found so far from the start to node n.
h(n) — the heuristic: an estimate of the cost of the cheapest path from n to a goal.
f(n) — g(n) + h(n); A* always expands the open node with the smallest f.
Open list (frontier) — nodes that have been discovered but not yet expanded, kept in a priority queue ordered by f.
Closed list (explored set) — nodes already expanded; it prevents repeated work.
Admissible heuristic — one that never overestimates: h(n) ≤ h*(n), the true cost to the goal.
Consistent (monotone) heuristic — h(n) ≤ cost(n, m) + h(m) for every edge n to m; it is the triangle inequality for the heuristic.
Expanding a node — removing it from the open list, generating its successors, and updating their g values.
history: Dijkstra published his shortest-path algorithm in 1959. In the late 1960s, the Shakey robot project at the Stanford Research Institute needed a robot to plan routes through rooms, and Peter Hart, Nils Nilsson and Bertram Raphael worked out how to add domain knowledge to the search. Their 1968 paper, "A Formal Basis for the Heuristic Determination of Minimum Cost Paths", defined A*, proved that it finds an optimal path if the heuristic is admissible, and proved that no other algorithm using the same information expands fewer nodes (in a sense later made precise). A short correction in 1972 fixed a detail about re-expanding nodes when the heuristic is admissible but not consistent.
Artificial intelligence research then asked how to make good heuristics and how to cope with memory. A* needs to remember every generated node, which becomes the limit long before time does. Richard Korf's IDA* (1985) replaced the priority queue with repeated depth-first searches with a rising cost limit, using memory proportional to the path length. Judea Pearl's book Heuristics (1984) and Dechter and Pearl's 1985 proof of optimal efficiency gave the theory. Culberson and Schaeffer's pattern databases (1998) built heuristics by solving simplified versions of the problem in advance; with them Korf solved random instances of Rubik's Cube optimally in 1997 (the average solution has about 18 moves; the maximum needed is 20, shown by Rokicki and others in 2010).
In practice, A* spread to games (pathfinding on grids and navigation meshes, jump point search by Harabor and Grastien, 2011), to robotics (Hybrid A* for vehicles, 2007), to automated planning (Fast Downward's heuristics such as LM-cut), and to road networks. For continental road maps, plain A* is too slow, so engines use preprocessing: ALT (A* with landmarks and the triangle inequality, Goldberg and Harrelson 2005) and contraction hierarchies (Geisberger and others, 2008), which are what map services build on.
how: Start with Dijkstra's algorithm. It keeps a priority queue of nodes ordered by g(n), the cost from the start. It removes the smallest, relaxes its outgoing edges (if g(n) + cost(n, m) is less than the best known g(m), update m), and stops when the goal is removed. Its correctness argument: when a node is removed, no cheaper path to it can exist, because all cheaper candidates would already have been removed. The problem is that the removal order depends only on distance from the start, so the search grows a disk around the start and reaches the goal only when the disk is as large as the whole distance.
A* changes one thing: the queue is ordered by f(n) = g(n) + h(n). Everything else is the same. Insert the start with f = h(start). Repeat: remove the node n with the smallest f from the heap; if n is a goal, return the path (follow parent pointers). Otherwise, for each successor m with edge cost c, compute g' = g(n) + c. If g' is lower than the best g(m) known (or m is new), record it, set the parent to n, and insert or update m in the heap with f = g' + h(m). The heap is the priority queue from the tree layer: finding the best open node costs O(log n), and the number of heap operations is at most the number of expanded nodes times the branching factor.
Why is it optimal when h is admissible? Suppose the goal G is removed from the heap with f(G) = g(G) (since h(G) = 0), and suppose a cheaper path to G exists with cost C* < g(G). Along that path there must be some node n' that is in the open list (the first unexpanded node on the path); its g is already optimal, and f(n') = g(n') + h(n') ≤ g(n') + h*(n') = C* (admissibility). So f(n') ≤ C* < g(G) = f(G), and n' would have been removed before G. Contradiction. So the first goal removed has optimal cost. Admissibility is the exact condition needed: an overestimate could hide the good path behind a node that looks too expensive.
Consistency adds efficiency. If h(n) ≤ c(n, m) + h(m) for every edge, then f never decreases along a path, and the first time A* expands a node, its g is already the best possible. Then each node is expanded at most once, and the closed set is safe. There is a neat way to see this: define new edge weights c'(n, m) = c(n, m) − h(n) + h(m). Consistency is exactly c' ≥ 0. The cost of any path from start to goal changes by the constant h(goal) − h(start) = −h(start), so shortest paths are unchanged, and A* with heuristic h is just Dijkstra's algorithm on the graph with weights c'. The heuristic does not change which path is best; it re-weights edges so that edges leading toward the goal become cheaper (even zero) and edges leading away become more expensive. With a perfect heuristic h = h*, all edges on optimal paths get weight zero, and A* walks straight to the goal. With h = 0 it is Dijkstra.
Where do heuristics come from? The geometry layer gives the simplest: on a map, straight-line distance is a lower bound for any path, by the triangle inequality, and it is consistent. On a grid with 4-direction moves, Manhattan distance |dx| + |dy| works; with 8 directions use octile distance. The general recipe is relaxation: solve an easier version of the problem exactly and use its cost. In the sliding-tile puzzle, if tiles could move through each other, each tile would need its Manhattan distance, and the sum is admissible. Pattern databases precompute, for a subset of the tiles, the exact cost to place them (ignoring the rest) for every arrangement, store it in a table, and look it up as h. Landmark heuristics (ALT) precompute distances to a few landmark nodes and use the triangle inequality |d(L, goal) − d(L, n)| ≤ d(n, goal). Several admissible heuristics can be combined with max, which is still admissible and at least as informed as each.
The cost is governed by heuristic accuracy. The number of expanded nodes grows exponentially in the error of the heuristic, roughly b^(error) for branching factor b in simple models (Pohl, Korf). A heuristic that is off by 2 steps on a branching factor of 3 costs about 3² = 9 times more nodes than a perfect one. The more accurate heuristic needs more precomputation or per-node time, so there is a balance.
Weighted A* uses f = g + w × h with w > 1 (for example 1.5): it expands far fewer nodes and returns a path whose cost is at most w times the optimum. This is a bounded-suboptimal trade-off, widely used in robotics and games. Memory-bounded variants (IDA*, SMA*, RBFS) trade repeated work for space.
diagram:
  Dijkstra (h = 0)               A* (h = straight-line distance)

  expands everything within      expands a narrow band toward G
  distance d of S

        . . . . . . .                     . . . . . . .
      . . . o o o . . .                   . . . . . . .
      . . o o o o o . .                   . . . . . o o .
      . o o o S o o o .  ....G            . . S o o o G .
      . . o o o o o . .                   . . . . . o o .
      . . . o o o . . .                   . . . . . . .

  o = expanded node       queue key:  f(n) = g(n) + h(n)
                          g = cost from S,  h = estimate to G

  consistent h:  c'(n,m) = c(n,m) - h(n) + h(m) >= 0
  => A* is Dijkstra on re-weighted edges c'; path costs shift by
     a constant
example: A small graph. Edges (directed, with cost): S→A 2, S→B 3, A→C 4, B→C 1, B→D 6, C→G 5, D→G 2. Heuristic values: h(S) = 8, h(A) = 6, h(B) = 5, h(C) = 4, h(D) = 2, h(G) = 0. The true best cost from S is 9 (S→B→C→G = 3 + 1 + 5). Check admissibility: the true cost to the goal from S is 9 and h(S) = 8 ≤ 9; from B it is 6 and h(B) = 5; from C it is 5 and h(C) = 4; from D it is 2 and h(D) = 2; from A it is 9 and h(A) = 6. Check consistency on edge B→C: h(B) = 5 ≤ 1 + h(C) = 5. Good. All other edges also satisfy it.
Run A*. Open list starts as {S: g=0, f=8}. Remove S. Generate A with g = 2, f = 2 + 6 = 8, and B with g = 3, f = 3 + 5 = 8. Open: {A: 8, B: 8}. Break the tie in favour of the larger g: remove B. Generate C with g = 3 + 1 = 4, f = 4 + 4 = 8, and D with g = 9, f = 9 + 2 = 11. Open: {A: 8, C: 8, D: 11}. Remove C (g = 4 beats A's 2 in the tie). Generate G with g = 4 + 5 = 9, f = 9 + 0 = 9. Open: {A: 8, G: 9, D: 11}. Remove A (f = 8). Its successor C could be reached with g = 2 + 4 = 6, which is worse than the recorded 4, so nothing changes. Open: {G: 9, D: 11}. Remove G: it is the goal, f = 9. The path, through parent pointers, is S→B→C→G with cost 9, which is optimal. Expanded nodes: S, B, C, A, and D was never expanded. Dijkstra orders by g alone: S, A, B, C, and then D and G both have g = 9, so it may expand D as well.
On a real grid the difference is large. Take a 1000 × 1000 open grid and ask for the path from one corner to the opposite corner. Dijkstra expands every cell closer than the goal: about a million cells. A* with the Manhattan heuristic on an empty grid has f equal to the optimal cost for every cell inside the rectangle between start and goal, so the result depends on the tie-breaking: with a bad tie-break it still visits much of the rectangle, but with "prefer larger g" it walks nearly straight and expands about 2,000 cells (the path length). Add walls and the heuristic becomes less accurate, but A* still expands a small fraction of what Dijkstra does.
```python
import heapq

def astar(start, goal, neighbors, h):
    # neighbors(n) yields (m, cost); h(n) estimates cost to goal
    g = {start: 0}
    parent = {start: None}
    pq = [(h(start), 0, start)]        # (f, -g, node)
    done = set()
    while pq:
        f, _, n = heapq.heappop(pq)
        if n in done:
            continue
        if n == goal:
            path = []
            while n is not None:
                path.append(n); n = parent[n]
            return g[goal], path[::-1]
        done.add(n)
        for m, c in neighbors(n):
            g2 = g[n] + c
            if g2 < g.get(m, float("inf")):
                g[m] = g2
                parent[m] = n
                heapq.heappush(pq, (g2 + h(m), -g2, m))
    return None
```
intuition: Hiking toward a mountain hut with a compass: you prefer the trail that has cost you least so far and also points toward the hut, and as long as your estimate of the remaining distance is never longer than the truth, you will never wrongly give up on the best trail.
The design is natural once you see the two kinds of information. The cost already paid, g, is certain. The remaining cost, h, is a guess. If the guess never exceeds the truth, then f is an optimistic estimate of the best total cost through that node. The best strategy is to always follow the most promising optimistic estimate: if the goal is reached while all other estimates are no better, nothing could have been cheaper. A heuristic that is too optimistic costs you time (more nodes are explored), and one that is too pessimistic costs you correctness.
mistakes: People often think A* is a different algorithm from Dijkstra. In fact it is Dijkstra on a re-weighted graph when the heuristic is consistent. Setting h = 0 gives Dijkstra exactly, and a heuristic only changes the order in which nodes are expanded.
People often think a better-looking heuristic always gives a better result. In fact if h overestimates even once, the path may be suboptimal; admissibility is the condition for optimality. Weighted A* (inflating h on purpose) knowingly gives that up, in exchange for speed and a bounded error.
People often think A* is always faster than Dijkstra. In fact with a poor heuristic (or h = 0) the two expand the same nodes, and A* adds the cost of computing h. If the heuristic is expensive (a pattern database lookup, a large landmark table), the per-node cost matters. Also, when you need paths to many destinations, Dijkstra's single sweep can be better.
People often think a consistent heuristic and an admissible heuristic are the same. In fact consistency implies admissibility, but not the reverse. With an admissible but inconsistent heuristic, A* may find a cheaper path to an already-expanded node and must re-expand it, which can cost exponential time in bad cases.
People often think A* is only for maps. In fact it works on any graph with a goal test and edge costs: puzzles, planning problems, parsing (A* parsing for natural language), and the search for a cheap query plan.
friction: Memory is the first wall. A* stores every generated node: on a hard puzzle, billions of nodes at 16 to 32 bytes each exhaust RAM in minutes, long before the time budget ends. IDA* and memory-bounded A* variants save memory but repeat work, and they lose against A* on graphs with many duplicate states without a transposition table.
Good heuristics are hard to design. They must be admissible, cheap to compute and close to the true cost, and these goals conflict. Domain-specific work (pattern databases, landmark selection, LM-cut for planning) takes real effort, and the memory for precomputed tables can be large (a Rubik's cube pattern database is on the order of tens to hundreds of millions of entries). For problems with huge plateaus (many nodes with equal f), tie-breaking alone can change the number of expansions by a factor of 100.
The optimality guarantee is demanding: optimal search on large problems is often infeasible, which is why practical systems accept suboptimal answers (weighted A*, anytime variants like ARA*) or use hierarchical methods. Real road networks add time-dependent costs, turn restrictions and traffic, which break static preprocessing; contraction hierarchies must be updated or customised when weights change.
In dynamic environments, the world changes during the search. Replanning methods (D* Lite, used in robot navigation) reuse earlier search effort when edge costs change, which is more complex than plain A*.
tradeoffs: Choose A* with an admissible, consistent heuristic when you need an optimal path, the state space is large but a good estimate exists (geometry, a relaxed problem), and memory holds the explored set. You give up memory (every generated node) and the effort of designing the heuristic.
Choose weighted A* (w between 1.2 and 3) or greedy best-first search when speed matters more than exact optimality: in games and robot navigation a path within 10 to 50 percent of optimal found 10 to 100 times faster is usually the right trade. You give up optimality but keep a bound (w × optimal for weighted A*; none for pure greedy).
Choose IDA* or another memory-bounded method when the state space is huge and memory is the limit, and when there are few duplicate states (tree-like search). You give up time through repeated expansions.
Choose Dijkstra, or a one-to-all search, when there is no useful heuristic, when you need distances to many targets, or when preprocessing is feasible. Choose preprocessing methods (ALT, contraction hierarchies) when the graph is static, queries are numerous, and each query must take milliseconds on a continental road network: you pay with an hours-long build and extra memory, and you gain query times that are orders of magnitude lower than plain A*.
Choose bidirectional search when both ends are known and the branching factor is high, since two searches of depth d/2 explore far fewer nodes than one of depth d.
practice: Start by writing down the state, the moves, the costs and the goal test, and make sure the state is canonical (identical situations map to the same key) so that duplicates are detected. Begin with Dijkstra or breadth-first search on a small instance to have a correct baseline. Then add a heuristic and check on random instances that the A* cost always equals the baseline cost: if it ever differs, h is not admissible.
Build heuristics by relaxation: drop a constraint, solve the easier problem, use its cost. Take the max of several admissible heuristics. For grids use octile distance for 8-way movement and Manhattan for 4-way. Use integer costs where possible, since floating-point rounding can break tie rules and consistency checks, and use a tie-breaking rule that prefers larger g (or smaller h), which cuts expansions greatly on open terrain.
Use a binary heap with lazy deletion (push duplicates, skip stale entries, as the code does), or an indexed heap with decrease-key. For game maps, search on a coarse graph (navigation mesh, waypoints) rather than raw cells, and cache paths. For production routing, use an existing engine (OSRM, Valhalla, GraphHopper) that implements contraction hierarchies, not a hand-written A*.
To measure, count expanded nodes, generated nodes, and the peak size of the open list; these tell you whether the heuristic or the memory is the bottleneck. For query planners, the same idea appears as branch-and-bound: carry the best complete plan's cost as an upper bound and discard any partial plan whose cost plus an optimistic estimate exceeds it.
quiz: What is the formula A* uses to choose the next node, and what do its parts mean? || f(n) = g(n) + h(n), where g is the exact cost from the start to n, and h is the heuristic estimate of the remaining cost to the goal. A* expands the open node with the smallest f.
Why does an admissible heuristic guarantee an optimal answer? || If h never overestimates, any node on a truly optimal path has f at most the optimal cost C*. So such a node is always expanded before a goal of cost greater than C*, and the first goal removed from the queue is optimal.
Why is A* with a consistent heuristic the same as Dijkstra on modified edge weights? || With c'(n, m) = c(n, m) − h(n) + h(m), consistency makes every c' non-negative, and every start-to-goal path changes cost by the same constant, so shortest paths are unchanged. Dijkstra on c' expands nodes in order of f.
On a 1000 × 1000 open grid, about how many cells does Dijkstra expand for a corner-to-corner path, and what does A* with Manhattan distance and good tie-breaking expand? || Dijkstra expands about a million cells (all closer than the goal). A* with good tie-breaking expands roughly the path length, about 2,000 cells, because f equals the optimal cost along the whole shortest path.
You must route on a national road network with millions of queries per day. Plain A* or something else? || Something else: use a preprocessed method (contraction hierarchies or ALT), which has a build cost and extra memory but answers queries orders of magnitude faster than plain A*, since the static road graph can be preprocessed.
interview: A* returns a path that is 20 percent longer than Dijkstra's. What went wrong, and how do you find it? || The heuristic is not admissible (it overestimates for some state), or a weight w > 1 was used on purpose, or the closed set blocks needed re-expansion with an inconsistent heuristic. Find it by running both on many random instances and checking where h(n) exceeds the true remaining cost computed by backward Dijkstra from the goal.
How would you design a heuristic for a new puzzle or planning domain? || Relax the problem by removing constraints so that it becomes easy, solve the relaxed problem exactly, and use its cost, which is admissible by construction. Improve it by combining several relaxations with max, or by precomputing exact costs for abstractions (pattern databases). Measure node expansions on a benchmark set against the extra time per node.
What are the memory problems of A*, and what are the alternatives? || It stores all generated nodes, which grows exponentially. IDA* uses memory linear in depth by repeated depth-first searches with a rising f-limit, at the cost of re-expanding nodes. SMA* and RBFS use bounded memory. Weighted A* shrinks the search itself. Bidirectional search reduces depth.
Why do road routers not use plain A*, and what do they use? || Plain A* with straight-line distance still expands a large area on continental maps, and the heuristic is weak when roads wind. Routers preprocess the static graph: contraction hierarchies add shortcut edges so queries touch a few hundred nodes, and ALT uses landmark distances with the triangle inequality. Time-dependent traffic needs customisable variants.
How does cost-based pruning in a query optimizer resemble A*? || The optimizer searches a space of partial plans, each with a cost so far and an optimistic bound on the remaining work. It keeps the best complete plan's cost as an upper bound and discards partial plans whose cost plus bound exceeds it. This is branch and bound, the same idea of ordering by optimistic total cost, though Cascades-style memoisation shares sub-plans across the search.
ref: paper | A Formal Basis for the Heuristic Determination of Minimum Cost Paths | Hart, Nilsson & Raphael, IEEE Transactions on Systems Science and Cybernetics, 1968 |
ref: paper | Depth-First Iterative-Deepening: An Optimal Admissible Tree Search | Richard E. Korf, Artificial Intelligence 1985 |
ref: paper | On the Optimality of A* | Rina Dechter & Judea Pearl, Artificial Intelligence 1985 |
ref: paper | Finding Optimal Solutions to Rubik's Cube Using Pattern Databases | Richard E. Korf, AAAI 1997 |
ref: paper | Computing the Shortest Path: A* Search Meets Graph Theory | Goldberg & Harrelson, SODA 2005 |
ref: book | Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, ch. 3 (Solving Problems by Searching), 4th ed. | Russell & Norvig, 2020 | https://aima.cs.berkeley.edu
ref: docs | Introduction to A* (and implementation notes) | Amit Patel, Red Blob Games | https://www.redblobgames.com/pathfinding/a-star/introduction.html
ref: paper | Contraction Hierarchies: Faster and Simpler Hierarchical Routing in Road Networks | Geisberger, Sanders, Schultes & Delling, WEA 2008 |
@@ anycast
value: Anycast and GeoDNS are the two main ways to send each user of a global service to a nearby, working copy of it. The problem is physical. Light in fibre travels at about 200,000 km per second, so a round trip to a server 10,000 km away costs at least 100 ms before any software runs, and a web page needs several round trips. A service with servers in one city is slow for the rest of the world and fails completely if that city has an outage. The fix is to run the service in many places and to steer each user to a good one.
With anycast, many sites announce the same IP address range to the internet using BGP, the protocol that routers use to tell each other which addresses they can reach. Each router on the internet then simply forwards packets along the best route it knows to that address, and because the routes lead to different sites from different parts of the world, each user's packets arrive at a site that is near them in network terms. Nobody decides; the routing system does. With GeoDNS, the decision is made in the naming system instead. The authoritative DNS server looks at who is asking (the IP address of the resolver, or the client subnet it passes along) and answers with the IP address of a nearby, healthy region. Different users get different answers for the same name.
For the layer above, these are the first step of global load balancing: CDNs and edge networks (Cloudflare serves every product from one anycast network), multi-region failover and disaster recovery, and the public DNS services (the root servers, 8.8.8.8 and 1.1.1.1) are anycast. The layers below do the real work. BGP and IP routing (L3) deliver packets hop by hop to wherever the best route leads, with no idea that several sites share the address. DNS (L3) supplies the naming, caching and time-to-live mechanics that GeoDNS rides on. Graph theory (L4) describes routing as a shortest-path problem, though BGP's notion of "shortest" is a policy-weighted path of networks, not of milliseconds. The speed of light (L5) sets the floor for latency and is the reason the steering matters at all.
terms: Anycast — one IP address announced from many places; the network delivers each packet to one of them, usually the closest by routing.
BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) — the protocol by which autonomous systems (networks) announce which IP prefixes they can reach and choose among paths.
Prefix — a block of IP addresses written in CIDR form, such as 203.0.113.0/24 (256 addresses).
AS path — the list of autonomous systems a BGP route has passed through; shorter is usually preferred.
Catchment — the set of users whose traffic is delivered to a given anycast site.
Withdrawal — a BGP message saying a prefix is no longer reachable here; it is how a failed site drains itself.
GeoDNS — a DNS server that gives different answers to different askers based on their location or measured latency.
EDNS Client Subnet (ECS) — a DNS extension (RFC 7871) in which a resolver passes a truncated client address (for example a /24) to the authoritative server so it can give a good answer.
TTL — time to live; how long a resolver may cache a DNS answer.
ECMP — equal-cost multipath; spreading packets of different flows over several equal routes, using a hash of the flow.
history: Anycast was described in RFC 1546, "Host Anycasting Service", in 1993 by Partridge, Mendez and Milliken, as a way for a client to reach "any one" of several servers sharing an address. It stayed an idea for years because the exact use was unclear. The important early application was DNS. The root name servers, which every resolver depends on, could not be placed in all the locations where queries came from, and a single address had to be listed for each of the 13 root server identities. In 2002 the F root server operator (ISC) began announcing its address from multiple sites using BGP, and others followed. Today the 13 root identities run on well over a thousand anycast instances worldwide. RFC 4786 (2006, BCP 126) documented how to operate such services.
GeoDNS grew in parallel with content delivery networks. Akamai (founded 1998) steered users by answering DNS queries with the address of a nearby cache server chosen from live measurements of the network. Cloud providers later offered the same as a product, for example latency-based and geolocation routing in Amazon Route 53 (2012 and later). Both approaches suffered from the same weakness, which is that DNS sees the resolver and not the user. Public resolvers such as Google's 8.8.8.8 (2009) made this worse, since a user in Mumbai may use a resolver run in another city, which led to EDNS Client Subnet (2016, RFC 7871).
The 2010s brought anycast to web traffic. Cloudflare (2010) built its whole network on it. Google Cloud's global external load balancer, Amazon's Global Accelerator (2018) and many others offer a small set of static anycast addresses that enter the provider's backbone at the nearest edge. Research (for example by Li and others, SIGCOMM 2018) measured how often BGP's choice is not the lowest-latency site, which drove techniques such as regional anycast, careful peering and hybrid designs where DNS and anycast work together.
how: Anycast, step by step. An operator owns a prefix, for example a /24, and an autonomous system number (ASN). It deploys the same service on servers in several sites, say Mumbai, Frankfurt and Virginia. At each site, a router (or the server itself, running a BGP daemon) announces the prefix to the local upstream networks and peers. These neighbours pass the announcement on, adding their own AS number to the path. Within minutes, every network on the internet has received several routes to the same prefix, one per direction.
Each router on the internet picks one best route per prefix using the BGP decision process: first the highest local preference (set by business policy: customers over peers over transit providers), then the shortest AS path, then lower origin type, then lower MED, then prefer routes learned from external peers, then the lowest interior (IGP) cost to the exit point, and finally tie-breakers. A user's packet to the anycast address is then forwarded hop by hop. Every router looks up the destination with longest-prefix match and sends the packet toward the next hop of its best route. No router knows or cares that several sites share the address. The packet arrives at whichever site is the origin of the path chosen by the routers along the way. This is the contribution of IP routing: forwarding is purely local, and the destination address names a place in the routing system, not a machine.
The map of who reaches which site is the catchment. The graph layer shows what BGP optimises: it is a path-vector protocol on the graph of networks, close to shortest paths in AS hops after policy has been applied. AS hops are a poor proxy for distance. A user whose provider buys transit from a network that is closer to the Frankfurt site, by AS-path length, may be sent to Frankfurt although Mumbai is 10 times closer. Operators therefore shape the catchments: announce a prefix only from selected sites, prepend their own AS number several times to make a path look longer, tag announcements with BGP communities that ask upstream providers to lower their local preference, and add peering at exchange points where many local networks connect.
Failover is built into the mechanism. Each site runs a health check, and if the service fails, the site withdraws its announcement (or the BGP session drops because the machine died). Neighbours remove the route, and routers that were using it select the next best, which leads to another site. Convergence takes from a few seconds to a couple of minutes, since BGP updates propagate with timers and route-flap damping may delay re-announcements. In-flight connections to the failed site are lost; new connections go elsewhere.
A weakness of anycast is that routes can change in the middle of a connection. If the best path from a user's network moves to a different site, the next packets of a TCP connection arrive at a server that has never seen it and the server resets it. This is rare in practice because routes on the internet are stable over seconds to minutes, which is longer than most web requests, and operators avoid it for long-lived flows by terminating TCP at the edge and carrying only short requests, by using QUIC connection IDs, or by sharing state between sites. Inside each site, ECMP spreads flows across many servers. The router hashes the flow's 5-tuple, but when a server is added or removed, plain hashing remaps many flows, so sites use consistent hashing in software load balancers (such as Google's Maglev and Meta's Katran) to keep most flows in place.
GeoDNS, step by step. The service has a name, such as `api.example.com`, whose authoritative DNS servers hold several A records, one per region. A user's device asks its resolver (at the ISP or a public service). The resolver asks the authoritative server. The authoritative server examines the source address of the query, maps it with a geolocation database or latency measurements to the closest healthy region, and returns that region's address with a short TTL (often 20 to 60 seconds). The resolver caches the answer for the TTL and gives it to all users behind it. If ECS is supported, the resolver sends a truncated client subnet, and the answer can be based on the actual user rather than on the resolver. The DNS layer contributes the mechanism of cached, delegated answers with a TTL; that same caching is the weak point. After a region fails, the health check needs to detect it (for Route 53 the default is a probe every 30 seconds with a failure threshold of 3, so up to about 90 seconds, or about 30 seconds with fast checks), the authoritative server then stops returning the address, and clients keep the old answer until the TTL expires. Some resolvers, applications and operating systems cache longer than the TTL.
Many systems combine them. A common design uses GeoDNS or latency-based DNS to choose a region and uses anycast IPs for the edge in front of it, or gives a handful of anycast addresses per region for resilience. DNS is also used for traffic shifting (send 10 percent of a country to a new region), which anycast cannot do precisely.
diagram:
  ANYCAST (one address, many origins)

   user (Chennai) --> ISP A --> IXP --> [ site MUM ] 203.0.113.0/24
   user (Berlin)  --> ISP B --> IXP --> [ site FRA ] 203.0.113.0/24
   user (Boston)  --> ISP C --> IXP --> [ site IAD ] 203.0.113.0/24
     every router: best BGP route to 203.0.113.0/24, then forward

   site FRA fails -> withdraws prefix -> neighbours drop route ->
   Berlin converges to the next best origin (seconds to minutes)

  GEODNS (one name, many addresses)

   user -> resolver -> authoritative DNS (health-checked)
                        |  asks: where is this resolver / client subnet?
                        v
          answer: A 198.51.100.7  (nearest healthy region, TTL 30 s)
   user connects directly to that regional address

  anycast: network picks the site | GeoDNS: you pick, cached for TTL
example: Setup: a service is announced as 203.0.113.0/24 from Mumbai, Frankfurt and Virginia. A user in Chennai is served by an ISP that peers at an exchange in Mumbai. The ISP receives three routes for the prefix: from Mumbai with AS path length 2 (ISP, origin), from Frankfurt with length 3 via a transit provider, and from Virginia with length 4. Local preference is equal, so the shortest AS path wins: Mumbai. The great-circle distance Chennai to Mumbai is about 1,030 km. At about 10 microseconds of round-trip fibre delay per kilometre of straight distance, that is a floor of about 10 ms; the actual path is longer, so perhaps 20 to 25 ms. If the traffic had gone to Frankfurt (about 7,700 km), the floor would be 77 ms, in practice 130 to 180 ms.
Now Mumbai fails. The site's health check notices within 2 seconds and withdraws the route (or the BGP session drops). The Mumbai ISP sees the route disappear and promotes the Frankfurt route. Suppose convergence takes 20 seconds. In that time, requests to Chennai users fail or time out; after that, new connections reach Frankfurt, with 130 to 180 ms round trip, and no client or DNS change was needed. TCP connections that were open to Mumbai are lost and must be re-established by the client.
Compare with GeoDNS for the same failure. The authoritative server answers with TTL 30 seconds. Health checks run every 10 seconds (fast checks) with a threshold of 3, so detection takes up to 30 seconds. After this, the server answers with Frankfurt's address. Resolvers that cached the old answer keep using it until it expires, up to another 30 seconds, so the worst case is about 60 seconds of failure for well-behaved resolvers, and longer for those that ignore TTLs. With default health checks (30 seconds, threshold 3) and a 60-second TTL, the worst case is 90 + 60 = 150 seconds. The upside: you choose exactly who goes where, so you can drain 20 percent of Indian traffic to a new region, or serve each country from a region that keeps data inside it.
Last, a capacity check. Suppose the service handles 300,000 requests per second globally, with each of the 3 sites sized for 150,000 per second (50 percent headroom). In normal times the catchments send 100,000 each. If Mumbai fails, its catchment moves wholesale to the next site on each path, maybe all to Frankfurt: 200,000 per second, above that site's capacity of 150,000. Anycast does not know capacity. The fix is to size each site for the loss of its worst neighbour, to shape catchments in advance (announce from Singapore too), and to withdraw routes gradually under overload.
```python
import time

def run(site, prefix, healthy, announce, withdraw):
    state = "withdrawn"
    ok_since = None
    while True:
        if healthy(site):
            ok_since = ok_since or time.time()
            # hold-down: announce only after 30 s of good health
            if state == "withdrawn" and time.time() - ok_since > 30:
                announce(prefix); state = "announced"
        else:
            ok_since = None
            if state == "announced":
                withdraw(prefix); state = "withdrawn"
        time.sleep(2)
```
intuition: Anycast is like a national emergency number printed on every phone: you dial the same digits wherever you are, and the phone network, not you, connects you to the closest dispatch centre. GeoDNS is like asking a receptionist, who looks at where you are calling from and gives you the direct number of a nearby office.
Both approaches use a layer that already exists and that every client already uses. BGP already moves packets toward destinations, so announcing one prefix from many places converts "nearest in the routing sense" into a free load-balancing decision, with no client change and no extra round trip. DNS already turns names into addresses before every connection, so answering differently per asker adds a decision point at no extra cost. The two differ in who holds the pen: the routing system, which is fast and automatic but not aware of your capacity or latency, or your authoritative server, which is precise but cached.
mistakes: People often think anycast sends users to the geographically nearest site. In fact BGP picks by policy and AS-path length, and a path that is shorter in networks may be much longer in kilometres. Studies of anycast networks show a fraction of users reaching a site that is not the lowest-latency one. Operators must test and tune with peering, prepending and regional prefixes.
People often think anycast breaks TCP. In fact it works well for most traffic because routes are stable for the duration of typical connections, and DNS over UDP is naturally safe. The risk is for long-lived connections when routes change; designs reduce it by terminating connections at the edge, using QUIC, or keeping flows short.
People often think GeoDNS knows where the user is. In fact it sees the resolver's address, which can be far from the user (public resolvers, corporate resolvers), unless ECS is available, and the geolocation database itself can be wrong. The answer is also cached, so one decision serves thousands of users.
People often think a short DNS TTL guarantees fast failover. In fact some resolvers, operating systems, browsers and applications hold answers longer or pin connections, and an application that keeps a connection open never asks again. A TTL of 30 seconds gives a typical failover of a minute or two with a tail beyond that.
People often think anycast gives load balancing. In fact it spreads users by network topology, not by load. A big ISP behind one site can overload it while others idle. Load control needs capacity-aware measures: more sites, split prefixes, withdrawals under overload, or DNS-based shifting on top.
friction: Control is limited. With anycast you steer by influencing other networks' decisions, using prepending and communities, which are advisory and may be ignored; a change in some distant network's policy can move a large part of your traffic overnight. Debugging "why does this user reach that site" needs looking glasses, traceroutes and measurement platforms. A single bad BGP announcement, or a route leak or hijack elsewhere, can pull traffic to the wrong place, as in several well-known outages; the countermeasures are RPKI route-origin validation and monitoring.
Anycast needs infrastructure: your own AS number, address space (the smallest prefix accepted globally is typically a /24 for IPv4 and a /48 for IPv6), BGP-capable routers or hosts at each site, and relationships with upstream networks at each location. This is why most companies buy it as a service (a CDN, a cloud global load balancer). Convergence is slow compared with a server failure, and route-flap damping can delay recovery.
Stateful traffic is the hard case. Sticky sessions and long connections suffer when catchments change, and anycast across the whole internet for TCP at scale demands careful engineering (consistent hashing inside sites, state sharing, edge termination). DDoS defence is a plus (attack traffic spreads across sites, and each site absorbs a share), yet one site with a large local attack source can still be saturated.
GeoDNS has its own limits. Geolocation data is imperfect; resolvers hide users; caching delays changes; and a DNS answer commits a client to a single address for the TTL, so it cannot rebalance in-flight traffic. Measurement-based steering (latency maps) is only as good as the freshness of its measurements. Both systems also need ongoing work to keep sites healthy and capacity matched to catchments.
tradeoffs: Choose anycast when you run your own edge, need failover in seconds without client cooperation, and the traffic is short-lived (HTTP requests, DNS, QUIC): the network gives you proximity and failure handling for free, and you give up precise control of who goes where. Choose GeoDNS when you need explicit control (country-level data residency, gradual shifting, weighted rollout, capacity-aware routing), have long-lived connections that must not move, or cannot run BGP: you give up fast failover and accept resolver and caching errors.
Choose both for a global service: GeoDNS or latency-based routing to pick a region and anycast addresses at each edge for resilience and DDoS absorption. This costs more operational complexity but reduces the weaknesses of each.
Choose a managed anycast service (a CDN, AWS Global Accelerator, Google's global load balancer) unless you operate at a scale where owning prefixes and peering pays off: running your own is a large fixed cost (ASN, address space, routers, peering agreements, 24-hour operations), with savings only at large traffic volumes.
Choose a small number of sites with large capacity if your traffic is small and failures are rare; choose many sites if latency to the long tail of users matters. Each added site shortens distances but adds capacity planning, deployment and catchment-management work.
Choose short TTLs (20 to 60 seconds) for names that need failover, at the cost of more DNS queries; choose long TTLs (minutes to hours) for stable names, which reduce load and lookup latency.
practice: Decide what you actually need to steer: latency, failover, capacity or compliance. If you are not a network operator, use the provider's tools: latency-based or geolocation routing in your DNS service with health checks, or a global anycast front end. Set TTLs deliberately (30 to 60 seconds for failover-sensitive names), turn on health checks that test the real service path and not only the port, and test failover by actually failing a region during a drill.
If you run anycast, announce from every site with a health check that withdraws on failure and a hold-down timer that stops flapping. Keep the advertised prefix at the minimum globally routable size, deploy RPKI route origin authorisation records so others can reject hijacks, and monitor from many vantage points (RIPE Atlas, ThousandEyes) to see catchments and latency. Plan capacity for the loss of the largest site with its traffic landing on its neighbours, and keep a way to shift traffic manually (prepend, withdraw, community tags).
For stateful protocols, terminate at the edge and forward requests to the backend over a stable path, keep sessions in a shared store, or use QUIC with connection IDs routed by the load balancer. Measure the real distribution of round-trip times per country or network, and compare it with the best site per user; the gap is the value of tuning.
To learn the mechanics, read RFC 4786, look up your own anycast catchment with `dig +short CH TXT id.server @1.1.1.1` (many anycast DNS services return the identity of the answering site), and trace the route to a public anycast address with `traceroute` from different networks.
quiz: How does anycast send a user to a nearby site without the user or the application doing anything? || Several sites announce the same prefix via BGP. Each router chooses its best route to that prefix and forwards packets along it, so packets end up at whichever site the user's network prefers, usually the closest by routing policy.
Why can an anycast site that is geographically far still win for some users? || BGP prefers routes by local preference and AS-path length, not by distance or latency. A path through fewer networks can be physically longer. Operators correct this with peering, prepending and regional announcements.
What limits the failover speed of GeoDNS, and how does the time add up? || Health-check detection time plus the TTL held by resolvers (and longer for resolvers that ignore it). For example, 3 checks at 10 s = 30 s, plus a 30 s TTL gives about 60 s worst case for well-behaved resolvers.
A user in Frankfurt is wrongly sent to Mumbai, 6,600 km away by great-circle distance. What is the minimum round-trip time added by distance? || About 6,600 km × 10 µs per km = 66 ms as a floor from light in fibre (2/3 of c); real paths are longer, so 100 ms or more is common.
When would you pick GeoDNS over anycast for a service? || When you need explicit, capacity-aware or compliance-driven control (data residency, weighted shifts), have long-lived stateful connections that must not move mid-session, or cannot run BGP. You accept slower failover.
interview: How would you design global traffic steering for a service with three regions and a 99.99 percent availability target? || Use anycast addresses at the edge for fast failover and DDoS absorption, with edge proxies that terminate connections and forward over a private backbone to the nearest healthy region. Add DNS or application-level steering for capacity-aware shifting and drills. Size each region to absorb its neighbour's traffic, health-check the real service path, and test region failure regularly.
What can go wrong when a route changes mid-connection on an anycast service, and how do large operators handle it? || Packets of an existing TCP flow arrive at another site without state and get reset. Operators terminate TCP at the edge so only short requests cross, use QUIC connection IDs or consistent hashing with state sharing, make routes stable (avoid flapping and unnecessary changes), and monitor resets by site.
Explain why BGP's choice may not minimise latency, and what you would do about it. || BGP applies business policy first and AS-path length second; it has no latency information. Fixes: add peering near users, announce prefixes only from sites that make sense (regional anycast), prepend or use communities to adjust, and measure real latency per network to find bad catchments. Hybrid DNS steering can override for specific networks.
How does EDNS Client Subnet improve GeoDNS, and what does it cost? || The resolver passes a truncated client prefix so the authoritative server can geolocate the user and not the resolver, which helps with public resolvers. The costs are privacy exposure of part of the client address, lower cache hit rates for the resolver (answers are cached per subnet), and more load on the authoritative servers.
A BGP leak sends your anycast prefix's traffic through a distant network. How do you detect and reduce this risk? || Detect via monitoring from many vantage points (latency and path changes, BGP monitoring services). Reduce with RPKI origin validation and ROAs with a correct maximum length, announcing more-specific prefixes during an incident, strict filtering with peers, and an on-call procedure to contact the offending network.
