ref: paper | Maglev: A Fast and Reliable Software Network Load Balancer | Eisenbud et al., NSDI 2016 |
ref: blog | What is Anycast? / How Cloudflare's network works | Cloudflare Learning Center | https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/cdn/glossary/anycast-network/
ref: docs | Amazon Route 53: Choosing a routing policy | AWS | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy.html
@@ httpcaching
value: HTTP caching lets a response be reused instead of fetched again. The fastest request is the one never sent, and the cheapest response is a tiny "nothing changed". Two small pieces of metadata make this work. A freshness lifetime (`Cache-Control: max-age=3600`) tells every cache how long it may reuse the stored copy without asking. A validator (`ETag: "3f9a1c"`) lets a cache, once the copy is stale, ask the server "do you still have version 3f9a1c?". If yes, the server sends `304 Not Modified` with no body, and the cache keeps using its copy.
Caches exist at every step of the path: the browser, a corporate proxy, a CDN edge server, a reverse proxy in front of your application (NGINX `proxy_cache`, Varnish). A well-cached site serves most requests from the nearest cache. With a CDN hit ratio of 95 percent, an origin that would see 10,000 requests per second sees 500. Users get a page in milliseconds, and the origin needs a fraction of the servers. A poorly cached site pays for the same bytes again and again.
For the layer above, this is what makes the browser fast, what a CDN is made of, and what lets NGINX and Cloudflare absorb traffic. Object stores such as S3 return ETags and answer conditional requests for the same reason. The layers below provide the mechanisms. HTTP (L3) provides the request and response model, the header fields, status codes such as 304 and the idea that a URL names a resource. Cryptographic hash functions (L3) provide the usual way to make a validator: an ETag is often a hash of the content, so that equal bytes always have equal tags. Hardware clocks and timers (L3) matter because freshness is about time: a cache must measure how old a copy is, and clocks on different machines disagree, which is why HTTP prefers relative lifetimes to absolute dates.
terms: Fresh and stale — a stored response is fresh while its age is below its freshness lifetime; once older, it is stale and must be revalidated before reuse (unless a rule allows stale use).
Freshness lifetime — how long a response may be reused without contacting the origin; set by `max-age`, `s-maxage` or `Expires`.
Validator — a value that identifies a version of a resource: `ETag` (an opaque tag) or `Last-Modified` (a date).
Conditional request — a request carrying `If-None-Match` (with an ETag) or `If-Modified-Since` (with a date); the server answers 304 if the version is unchanged.
Revalidation — asking the origin whether a stale copy is still valid.
Private and shared cache — a private cache serves one user (the browser); a shared cache serves many (proxy, CDN).
Cache key — what identifies an entry: the method and URL, plus the request headers named in `Vary`.
Vary — a response header listing request headers (for example `Accept-Encoding`) that change the response and so must be part of the cache key.
Cache busting — changing the URL (usually by putting a content hash in the file name) so that a new version is fetched even though the old one is cached for a year.
history: Early HTTP (1991 to 1994) had no caching rules. HTTP/1.0 (RFC 1945, 1996) added `Expires` and `Last-Modified` with `If-Modified-Since`, and proxy caches such as Squid (from 1996) started to save expensive transatlantic bandwidth. The rules were incomplete and caches guessed. HTTP/1.1 (RFC 2068 in 1997, RFC 2616 in 1999) introduced `Cache-Control` with directives such as `max-age` and `no-cache`, and entity tags (`ETag`, `If-None-Match`), which fixed the one-second resolution of dates and the trouble of files that change without a new time stamp.
Later documents refined the details. RFC 5861 (2010) added `stale-while-revalidate` and `stale-if-error`, which let a cache serve an old copy while it fetches a new one in the background or when the origin is down. RFC 7234 (2014) rewrote the caching rules as a separate document, and RFC 8246 (2017) added `immutable`, which tells browsers not to revalidate when the user reloads a fingerprinted file. RFC 9111 (June 2022) is the current HTTP Caching specification, part of the revised HTTP standards (RFC 9110 and others), and replaces RFC 7234.
Practice moved with the tools. Build tools such as webpack and Vite put a hash of the content into file names (`app.3f9a1c.js`) so that assets can be cached for a year. CDNs (Akamai from 1998, Cloudflare, Fastly) turned caches into a global service and added their own controls (`s-maxage`, `Surrogate-Control`, purge APIs, `Cache-Tag`). Privacy concerns changed browsers: since about 2020 (Chrome 86), the HTTP cache is partitioned by the top-level site, so the same library file requested from two sites is cached twice, which ends a tracking leak but reduces the cross-site cache hit rate of shared CDN libraries.
how: A cache decides what to do with each request in a fixed sequence. First it forms the cache key: the method (normally GET or HEAD) and the target URL, plus the values of the request headers listed in the stored response's `Vary` field. For `Vary: Accept-Encoding`, a gzip request and a Brotli request are stored as different entries. If there is no matching entry, the cache forwards the request to the origin, and when the response arrives it decides whether it may store it. `Cache-Control: no-store` forbids storing. `private` allows only a private cache (a CDN must not store it). `public` or an explicit lifetime allows shared caches. By default, status codes such as 200, 301, 308, 404 and 410 are cacheable, and responses to POST are normally not.
If an entry exists, the cache computes whether it is fresh. The age is the time since the response was generated, measured as the `Age` header (set by upstream caches, so time spent in a CDN is counted) plus the time the entry has sat in this cache. The freshness lifetime comes from `s-maxage` (shared caches only) or `max-age`, in seconds, or from the older `Expires` date minus the `Date` header. If age is less than lifetime, the response is fresh and is returned immediately with no network traffic at all. The use of a relative lifetime in seconds is the contribution of the clock layer: it needs only a local timer on each machine, not agreement between the server's wall clock and the client's. If a response has no explicit lifetime but has `Last-Modified`, a cache may use a heuristic: typically 10 percent of the time since the last change. A file last modified 10 days ago is considered fresh for about a day. That is why responses with no headers at all are cached in surprising ways.
If the entry is stale, the cache revalidates. It sends a conditional request to the origin with the validator it holds: `If-None-Match: "3f9a1c"` (and/or `If-Modified-Since: <date>`). The origin compares. If the resource is unchanged, it answers `304 Not Modified` with a few headers (including possibly new freshness information) and no body. The cache updates its stored headers, resets the age and serves the stored body. If the resource has changed, the origin answers `200` with the new body, and the cache replaces the entry. A revalidation costs a full round trip but few bytes, so it saves bandwidth and origin work (the origin may skip generating the body), not latency.
Validators come in two strengths. A strong ETag changes whenever any byte of the body changes, and is commonly a hash of the content (a truncated SHA-1 or MD5, or a faster hash, in base64 or hex). The hash layer gives the property that matters: equal content gives equal tags, and different content gives different tags with overwhelming probability. Collision resistance in the cryptographic sense is not needed, since nobody attacks the tag, but a cheap and well-mixed hash is. A weak ETag (`W/"3f9a1c"`) promises only that the two versions are semantically equivalent. Some servers do not hash: NGINX builds its ETag from the file's modification time and size in hex, which is cheap but can differ between servers that hold identical files. That breaks conditional requests when a load balancer sends one client to different servers. Amazon S3 uses an MD5-based ETag for simple unencrypted uploads and a "hash of hashes" with a part count for multipart uploads, so an ETag there is not always the MD5 of the content.
Control directives refine the rules. `no-cache` is badly named: it permits storing but requires revalidation before every reuse. `no-store` means do not keep it at all. `must-revalidate` forbids serving stale copies, even when the origin is unreachable. `stale-while-revalidate=60` allows serving a stale copy for up to 60 more seconds while a background request refreshes it, which hides revalidation latency. `immutable` says the response will never change during its lifetime. User actions interact too: a normal reload makes the browser revalidate the main resource; a hard reload skips the cache.
The standard recipe follows from these rules. Static files whose names contain a content hash get `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`: they are fetched once and never revalidated, and when the content changes, so does the URL. The HTML document that refers to these files gets `Cache-Control: no-cache` plus an ETag (or a short `max-age` with `stale-while-revalidate`), so it is always checked and always points at current asset names. API responses depend on the data: personalised ones get `private` or `no-store`, shared ones get a short `max-age` or `s-maxage`.
diagram:
  browser                 CDN edge                    origin
     |   GET /app.js          |                          |
     |----------------------->|  miss                    |
     |                        |------------------------->|
     |                        |  200  ETag:"a1"          |
     |                        |  Cache-Control: max-age=3600
     |<-----------------------|<-------------------------|
     |  200 (stored)          | (stored, Age counts up)  |
     |                                                   |
   t=1000 s:  age 1000 < 3600  => fresh, served from cache, no traffic
   t=4000 s:  age 4000 > 3600  => stale, revalidate
     |   GET /app.js          |                          |
     |   If-None-Match:"a1"   |                          |
     |----------------------->|   If-None-Match:"a1"     |
     |                        |------------------------->|
     |                        |  304 Not Modified        |
     |<-----------------------|<-------------------------|
     |  304 (headers only, ~300 bytes) => reuse stored body

  cache key = method + URL + values of headers named in Vary
example: Follow one file, `/static/app.js`, 500 KB, served with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600` and `ETag: "a1"`. At t = 0 a user's browser has no copy and fetches it: 500 KB over the network. At t = 1,000 s the user opens another page that uses the file. The age is 1,000 seconds, below the lifetime of 3,600, so the browser uses its copy with no request. At t = 4,000 s the age is 4,000, above 3,600, so the copy is stale. The browser sends `GET /static/app.js` with `If-None-Match: "a1"`. The server sees the file is unchanged and returns `304` with about 300 bytes of headers. The browser resets the copy's age to zero and uses the stored 500 KB. Cost: one round trip (perhaps 50 ms) and 300 bytes, against 500 KB for a full download, a saving of about 99.9 percent of the bytes.
Now the heuristic rule. A response has `Date: 10:00:00` and `Last-Modified: 08:00:00`, and no `Cache-Control`. The file last changed two hours (7,200 s) before the response. With the 10 percent heuristic, the freshness lifetime is 720 seconds, so a cache may reuse it for 12 minutes without asking. If the author edits the file one minute later, users will see the old version for up to 12 minutes. This is a common source of "I deployed, but users still see the old page".
Next, a cache-busting design. A site's `index.html` links to `/assets/app.3f9a1c.js` and `/assets/app.css.7b20de`. The assets are served with `max-age=31536000, immutable` (one year), and `index.html` with `no-cache` and an ETag. A deploy changes the content of app.js, so the build produces `app.91be07.js` and a new HTML file that refers to it. Returning users send a conditional request for `index.html`, get a 200 with the new HTML (about 5 KB), then fetch the new JavaScript (500 KB), and still use the cached CSS because its name did not change. No purge, no stale content, and no user ever runs a mix of old HTML and new JS.
Finally, the effect at scale. An origin sees 10,000 requests per second for cacheable content. At a CDN hit ratio of 90 percent, 1,000 requests per second reach it. At 99 percent it is 100 per second, a tenfold difference caused by the last nine percent. With `stale-while-revalidate`, even the misses on popular objects are hidden from users, and request collapsing (the CDN sends one request upstream for many simultaneous misses of the same object) prevents a stampede when a popular object expires.
```bash
# illustrative headers for a fingerprinted asset
curl -sI https://example.com/assets/app.3f9a1c.js
# HTTP/2 200
# cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# etag: "3f9a1c"
# age: 8123            (seconds already spent in the CDN)

# revalidation of an HTML page
curl -sI -H 'If-None-Match: "d41d8c"' https://example.com/
# HTTP/2 304
# cache-control: no-cache
```
intuition: A library book with a due date and an edition number: while the date has not passed you keep the book at home, and after it you ask the librarian "is edition 3 still the current one?" instead of carrying the whole book back.
Caching is natural because most of the web changes slowly compared with how often it is read. The cheap trick is to let the author state how long a copy can be trusted (a promise) and to give the cache a fingerprint so that it can check cheaply (a verification). The author knows the shelf life; the cache knows how old its copy is. Splitting the work between them lets millions of copies stay correct without the origin tracking who holds which. The cost is that, within the lifetime, a cache can serve data the origin has already replaced.
mistakes: People often think `no-cache` means "do not cache". In fact it means "you may store it, but check with the origin before each use". The directive that forbids storing is `no-store`. Using `no-cache` for sensitive pages leaves copies on disk; using `no-store` for assets throws away all the benefit.
People often think a 304 response makes the page load faster. In fact a revalidation still costs a round trip to the origin. It saves bytes and origin work, not latency. To avoid the round trip, the response must be fresh (a long `max-age`) or served with `stale-while-revalidate`.
People often think you can purge a browser cache. In fact once a response with `max-age=31536000` is in users' browsers, you cannot recall it; it stays until it expires or the user clears it. You can purge a CDN, but not a browser. This is why long lifetimes belong only to URLs that change when the content changes.
People often think the ETag is a secure hash of the content. In fact it is an opaque string chosen by the server; it may be a hash, a version number, or file time plus size. Do not use it as a checksum or for integrity, and do not assume equal ETags on two servers mean equal files, unless both servers compute them the same way.
People often think a `Cache-Control` header on a request forces every cache to obey it. In fact request directives such as `no-cache` or `max-age=0` only ask caches along the way to revalidate, and a CDN may honour or ignore them; the response headers set by the origin decide what is stored.
friction: Invalidation is the hard part of caching. The only reliable ways to remove stale content are to wait for the lifetime to pass or to change the URL. CDN purges exist but take seconds to minutes to propagate across hundreds of locations, and they cannot reach browsers or intermediate proxies. Teams that cache HTML for hours discover that a bug fix cannot be rolled out quickly.
Mistakes with `Vary` and cache keys cause both misses and leaks. A missing `Vary: Accept-Encoding` or `Vary: Accept-Language` can serve a gzipped body to a client that cannot decode it, or the wrong language. Too many `Vary` values (such as `Vary: Cookie` or `Vary: User-Agent`) fragment the cache into near-unique entries and destroy the hit ratio. Worse, caching a personalised response in a shared cache without `private` shows one user's data to another, a classic and serious security incident, which also appears as "web cache deception" and "web cache poisoning" attacks, where an unkeyed header or path trick causes a harmful response to be stored under a popular URL.
Clocks and heuristics produce surprises. Servers behind a load balancer with different times produce inconsistent `Last-Modified` and `ETag` values, so revalidation fails more often than it should. Heuristic freshness from a missing `Cache-Control` is unpredictable between browsers. Cache behaviour also differs across clients, and developer tools and reload buttons change it further, so testing caching needs care.
Finally, caching does not help the first request, personalised content, or write-heavy workloads, and cold caches after a deploy or a CDN change cause a surge on the origin ("thundering herd") unless the origin is protected by request collapsing, tiered caching and rate limits.
tradeoffs: Choose long lifetimes with content-hashed URLs (a year, `immutable`) for static assets: you get zero requests on repeat visits, and you give up nothing as long as the build changes the URL on every change. Choose `no-cache` with ETag for HTML and other entry documents: users always see the current version, at the cost of a conditional request (one round trip, 300 bytes) per page.
Choose short lifetimes (seconds to a minute) with `stale-while-revalidate` for semi-dynamic content such as listings, feeds and public API responses that can be a little old: you absorb traffic spikes and hide latency, and you accept bounded staleness. Choose `private, no-store` for personalised or sensitive responses (account pages, tokens): you give up all sharing and benefit.
Choose a strong content-hash ETag when exactness matters and the server can afford to compute (or store) the hash; choose cheap validators (mtime and size, or a version number from the database) when hashing large bodies per request is too expensive. You give up the guarantee that equal tags mean equal bytes across servers.
Choose CDN-level caching (`s-maxage`, tiered caches, purge-by-tag) when you need to invalidate quickly without waiting for browser lifetimes; keep browser lifetimes short for those responses and let the CDN hold them longer. You pay for purge infrastructure and the risk of inconsistent copies between edges during propagation.
practice: Set an explicit policy for every response class, and never rely on heuristics. A typical policy: fingerprinted assets `public, max-age=31536000, immutable`; HTML `no-cache` (or `max-age=0, must-revalidate`) with an ETag; public API data `public, max-age=30, stale-while-revalidate=60` where acceptable; anything with a session cookie or personal data `private` or `no-store`. Check the result with browser developer tools (the Network tab shows "disk cache", "memory cache", 304), and with `curl -I`, and audit it with Lighthouse.
Make ETags consistent across servers: compute them from content (a hash) or from a stored version, not from file times. Set `Vary: Accept-Encoding` when you compress, and keep `Vary` lists short. Do not vary on `Cookie` or `User-Agent`; normalise at the CDN instead (a device class or language header). In CDN configuration, define the cache key explicitly, strip tracking query parameters from it, and ignore irrelevant cookies for static paths.
Protect the origin: enable request collapsing, a second cache tier (origin shield), and `stale-if-error` so an origin outage does not become a site outage. Watch the cache hit ratio per path class and the bytes saved, and alert on a sudden drop (often caused by a new cookie or query parameter that fragments the key).
For object stores and APIs, support conditional requests: return ETags, honour `If-None-Match` and `If-Match` (also useful for optimistic concurrency on writes: a PUT with `If-Match` fails with 412 if someone else changed the resource), and use range requests with `If-Range` for resumable downloads.
quiz: What is the difference between `no-cache` and `no-store`? || `no-cache` lets a cache store the response but forces it to revalidate with the origin before each reuse. `no-store` forbids storing it at all.
Why is a content hash in the file name combined with a one-year `max-age`? || The URL changes whenever the content changes, so old URLs can safely be cached for a very long time. Users fetch each version once, and a deploy needs no purge because the HTML points to new names.
How does a conditional request save work, and what does it not save? || The client sends the validator (`If-None-Match`); if unchanged, the server returns 304 with headers only, avoiding the body transfer and often the work to generate it. It does not avoid the round trip, so latency is unchanged.
A response has a `Date` of 12:00 and a `Last-Modified` time exactly 5 days earlier, and no `Cache-Control`. What heuristic freshness may a cache use? || About 10 percent of 5 days: 0.5 days, i.e. 12 hours. A cache may reuse the response for that long without asking the origin.
Your CDN hit ratio dropped from 95 percent to 60 percent after a release. What would you check first? || The cache key: a new cookie, query parameter or `Vary` header that makes URLs unique per user or request. Then check changed `Cache-Control` headers (a new `private` or `no-store`), changed TTLs, and a flushed cache or many new URLs after the deploy.
interview: How do you roll out a new frontend version so that users never see a mix of old HTML and new JavaScript, with no purge? || Build assets with content-hashed names and serve them with a one-year immutable lifetime; serve the HTML with `no-cache` and an ETag so it is always revalidated and refers to the new names. Keep the previous version's assets available for a while for users with open pages. No purge is needed because names change.
Why can a shared cache leak private data, and how do you prevent it? || If a personalised response is cacheable by a shared cache (no `private`, or default cacheability of a 200) and the cache key lacks the user identity, the next user gets the stored copy. Prevent it with `Cache-Control: private` or `no-store` on such responses, `Vary: Cookie` only where unavoidable, and CDN rules that never cache requests with session cookies on those paths.
Explain stale-while-revalidate and stale-if-error and when they are safe. || The first lets a cache serve an expired copy for a bounded extra window while it refreshes in the background, hiding revalidation latency. The second serves a stale copy when the origin fails or errors. They are safe when bounded staleness is acceptable (public content, feeds) and unsafe for data where correctness is critical, such as balances or permissions.
How do ETags enable safe concurrent updates, not only caching? || A client reads a resource with its ETag and sends the update with `If-Match: <etag>`. If the resource has changed in the meantime, the tag differs and the server returns 412 Precondition Failed, so lost updates are prevented. It is optimistic concurrency control in HTTP.
How would you debug a user who still sees the old version after a deploy? || Check the response headers they received (`Cache-Control`, `Age`, `Date`, `Last-Modified`, `ETag`, `Via`), whether a heuristic lifetime applied, whether a CDN or proxy served it (cache status header), what the cache key and `Vary` are, and whether a service worker or a long-lived tab is involved. Then fix the policy so the entry document revalidates and assets are fingerprinted.
ref: spec | RFC 9111: HTTP Caching | Fielding, Nottingham & Reschke (eds.), IETF, 2022 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111
ref: spec | RFC 5861: HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale Content | M. Nottingham, IETF, 2010 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861
ref: spec | RFC 8246: HTTP Immutable Responses | P. McManus, IETF, 2017 | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8246
ref: docs | HTTP caching | MDN Web Docs | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching
ref: blog | Prevent unnecessary network requests with the HTTP Cache | Ilya Grigorik & Jeff Posnick, web.dev | https://web.dev/articles/http-cache
ref: blog | Caching Tutorial for Web Authors and Webmasters | Mark Nottingham, 2002 (updated) | https://www.mnot.net/cache_docs/
ref: paper | Practical Web Cache Poisoning | James Kettle, PortSwigger Research, 2018 |
ref: book | High Performance Browser Networking, ch. 13 (HTTP caching section) | Ilya Grigorik, 2013 | https://hpbn.co
@@ overlaynet
value: A tunnel puts one packet inside another. The inner packet, with its own source and destination addresses, becomes the payload of an outer packet that the ordinary network can carry. At the far end, the outer wrapper is removed and the inner packet continues as if it had never left. This simple trick, called encapsulation, lets hosts that are far apart behave as if they were on one private network, and lets the addresses and rules of that private network differ from those of the network that carries it.
Three needs drive almost all uses. Privacy: a VPN (virtual private network) encrypts the inner packet, so that a laptop in a cafe or an office in another city can reach company systems over the public internet without anyone on the path reading or altering the traffic. Isolation: an overlay network gives every tenant, VM or container its own address space and its own segment, independent of the physical network, so that thousands of customers can each use 10.0.0.0/8 on shared hardware. Reach: a tunnel can carry traffic through a network that does not know the protocol or the addresses, such as IPv6 across an IPv4-only link, or a private cluster across the internet.
For the layer above, this is how cloud virtual networks (VPCs) are implemented, how Kubernetes pods get cluster-wide addresses (Flannel, Calico and Cilium overlay modes), and how companies connect offices and developers (IPsec, WireGuard, and products built on them). The layers below supply the parts. IP and routing (L3) carry the outer packet like any other, and routing tables decide which destinations go into a tunnel interface. The layered network model (L3) is what makes wrapping legal: each layer treats the layer above as opaque payload, so an IP packet can be the payload of a UDP datagram. Symmetric encryption (AES) and authenticated encryption (AEAD) protect each packet with a key and detect tampering; public-key cryptography (L3) lets two endpoints that have never met agree on those keys safely.
terms: Encapsulation — placing a complete packet inside the payload of another packet with a new header.
Tunnel — a logical point-to-point path created by encapsulation between two endpoints.
Overlay and underlay — the overlay is the virtual network that users see; the underlay is the physical or lower-level network that carries the encapsulated packets.
VTEP (VXLAN tunnel endpoint) — the device or host software that adds and removes the VXLAN header.
VNI (VXLAN network identifier) — a 24-bit number naming one virtual network segment, so there can be about 16 million.
IPsec — a suite of protocols for authenticating and encrypting IP packets; ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload) carries the encrypted data and IKE negotiates the keys.
WireGuard — a modern VPN protocol using fixed, modern cryptography and a very small code base, where each peer is identified by a public key.
MTU (maximum transmission unit) — the largest packet a link can carry, commonly 1500 bytes on Ethernet; encapsulation headers reduce the room left for the inner packet.
Anti-replay window — a record of recently seen sequence numbers that lets a receiver drop copies of old packets an attacker may re-send.
history: The first tunnels were practical fixes. IP-in-IP (RFC 1853 in 1995, RFC 2003 in 1996) carried IP packets for mobile hosts. GRE (Generic Routing Encapsulation, RFC 1701 in 1994, updated as RFC 2784 in 2000) wrapped many protocols in IP and became the usual building block for router-to-router links. In the mid-1990s, companies wanted to replace leased lines with the internet, which needed encryption. IPsec was specified in the IETF from 1995 (RFC 1825) and revised in 1998 (RFC 2401); the IKE key exchange followed, and IKEv2 (RFC 7296) arrived in 2014. SSL/TLS-based VPNs, among them OpenVPN (2001), took a different path by running over TCP or UDP and being easier to deploy through firewalls.
Data centres produced the second wave. Virtual machines needed to move between hosts keeping their addresses, and VLANs allow only 4,094 segments. VXLAN (RFC 7348, 2014, from VMware, Cisco and others) wrapped Ethernet frames in UDP with a 24-bit segment identifier, so a layer-2 network could be stretched over a layer-3 fabric. NVGRE (Microsoft) and STT were alternatives, and Geneve (RFC 8926, 2020) unified them with extensible options. Cloud providers built their virtual networks the same way. Container platforms copied the idea: Flannel's VXLAN mode (2014) and later Calico's IP-in-IP and Cilium's VXLAN or Geneve modes give every pod a routable address.
IPsec's complexity (hundreds of thousands of lines in some implementations, many options) led Jason Donenfeld to design WireGuard (paper at NDSS 2017). It uses a fixed set of modern primitives (Curve25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, BLAKE2s), a handshake based on the Noise framework, and roughly 4,000 lines of kernel code. It was merged into Linux 5.6 in March 2020. Tailscale (2019) and others built mesh networks on it, adding NAT traversal so that two laptops behind home routers can connect directly.
how: Encapsulation first. A packet leaves host A addressed to host B's private address, say 10.2.0.9. A routing table entry such as "10.2.0.0/16 via tunnel interface" sends it to the tunnel software instead of the network card. The tunnel code prepends an outer header addressed to the far tunnel endpoint's real, reachable address, and hands the result to the normal IP stack, which routes it like any other packet. In the middle, routers see only the outer header and forward by the outer destination. At the far end, the endpoint recognises the outer packet (a protocol number, UDP port or key), strips the header, and injects the inner packet into its own network, where ordinary routing delivers it. This works because of the layered model: each layer's payload is opaque bytes to the layer below.
VXLAN is the standard data-centre overlay. A virtual machine or container sends an Ethernet frame. The host's VTEP looks up which remote VTEP holds the destination MAC address in this segment, then builds: outer Ethernet, outer IPv4 (source and destination are the host addresses), outer UDP (destination port 4789; source port derived from a hash of the inner flow), an 8-byte VXLAN header containing the 24-bit VNI, and then the original inner frame. The hashed source port is a deliberate design: switches in the underlay use ECMP over the UDP/IP 5-tuple, so different inner flows take different physical paths even though all look like host-to-host UDP. The overhead is 50 bytes (14 + 20 + 8 + 8). The part that needs a control plane is the mapping of inner addresses to remote VTEPs. Early VXLAN flooded unknown destinations using multicast and learned from replies. Modern deployments use BGP EVPN (RFC 7432), or in Kubernetes the cluster's API server holds the mapping: each node is told which pod subnet lives on which node.
IPsec in tunnel mode wraps the whole inner IP packet. Two gateways first run IKE: they authenticate each other (pre-shared key or certificates), run an authenticated Diffie–Hellman exchange (public-key cryptography) to create shared secrets that were never sent, and agree on a security association, a set of keys and algorithms. Then each packet becomes an ESP packet: the original packet is padded, encrypted and authenticated with an AEAD cipher such as AES-GCM, and preceded by a security parameter index (which SA) and a sequence number (for anti-replay). Overhead is around 50 to 70 bytes. If a NAT is in the path, ESP is wrapped again in UDP port 4500. Authenticated encryption matters because encryption alone does not stop an attacker who flips bits or injects packets; the tag lets the receiver drop any modified packet.
WireGuard does the same job with less. Every peer has a Curve25519 key pair. The interface configuration lists peers by public key, with `AllowedIPs`: the inner addresses that may come from this peer and also the routes for sending to it. This "cryptokey routing" merges routing table and firewall rule into one list. Handshake: the initiator sends one message with an ephemeral public key and its encrypted static key; the responder replies; after one round trip both hold fresh session keys. Data packets are UDP: a 16-byte header (type, receiver index, 64-bit counter), the inner packet encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305, and a 16-byte tag. With an IPv4 outer header (20) and UDP (8), overhead is 60 bytes (80 over IPv6), which is why the default tunnel MTU is 1420. Keys are replaced after about two minutes, so a stolen key exposes little. A peer that does not know the right key gets no reply at all, so the port looks closed to scanners.
The MTU problem appears in all tunnels. The outer packet must fit the underlay MTU (1500), so the inner packet may be at most 1500 minus the overhead: 1450 for VXLAN, about 1420 for WireGuard. A larger inner packet is either fragmented (slow, and often dropped) or, if its DF bit is set, rejected with an ICMP message that firewalls sometimes block, which makes connections hang after the handshake works. The common fixes are to set the interface MTU to the right value, to clamp TCP's MSS (the largest segment a TCP endpoint will send) on the tunnel to MTU minus 40, or to use jumbo frames (9000 bytes) in the underlay.
The crypto cost is real but moderate. With AES hardware instructions (AES-NI) or ChaCha20 on vector units, one core can encrypt many gigabits per second. The bigger cost is often per-packet processing and lost offloads: NICs can segment large TCP sends and verify checksums, but only if they understand the tunnel format (VXLAN and Geneve offloads exist; many encrypted tunnels rely on software). Throughput of 10 Gbit/s and above needs care with CPU affinity and offloads.
diagram:
  ORIGINAL PACKET (inner)
  +----------+---------------------------+
  | IP hdr   | TCP segment / payload     |   10.1.0.5 -> 10.2.0.9
  +----------+---------------------------+

  VXLAN (over IPv4 underlay)         overhead 50 bytes
  +-------+-------+-------+--------+-------+--------------------+
  | outer | outer | outer | VXLAN  | inner | inner IP + payload |
  | Eth   | IPv4  | UDP   | VNI=42 | Eth   |                    |
  | 14    | 20    | 8     | 8      | 14    |                    |
  +-------+-------+-------+--------+-------+--------------------+
   host A -> host B, UDP dst 4789, src port = hash(inner flow)

  WIREGUARD                          overhead 60 bytes (IPv4)
  +-------+-------+--------+---------------------------+-------+
  | outer | outer | WG hdr | encrypted inner IP packet | tag   |
  | IPv4  | UDP   | 16     | ChaCha20-Poly1305         | 16    |
  | 20    | 8     |        |                           |       |
  +-------+-------+--------+---------------------------+-------+

  node1 (pod 10.244.1.5) ==== underlay 192.168.0.0/24 ==== node2
  VTEP adds/removes outer headers   (pod 10.244.2.7)
example: A Kubernetes cluster uses VXLAN. Pod A (10.244.1.5) on node 1 (192.168.0.11) sends a TCP packet to pod B (10.244.2.7) on node 2 (192.168.0.12). Node 1's routing table says "10.244.2.0/24 via the vxlan interface to 192.168.0.12". The VTEP builds the frame. The inner frame has source 10.244.1.5 and destination 10.244.2.7. The outer headers are Ethernet (node MACs), IPv4 192.168.0.11 → 192.168.0.12, UDP to port 4789 with a source port taken from a hash of the inner flow, and a VXLAN header with VNI 1. The underlay switches see only host-to-host UDP and route it. Node 2's VTEP checks the port and VNI, removes 50 bytes of outer headers, and delivers the inner frame to pod B. The reply travels the same way in reverse.
Overhead arithmetic. The underlay MTU is 1500. The outer IP packet can carry 1500 bytes, of which 20 (outer IP) + 8 (UDP) + 8 (VXLAN) + 14 (inner Ethernet) = 50 are overhead. So the inner IP packet is at most 1450 bytes, and the pod interface MTU is set to 1450. A TCP segment inside has at most 1450 − 40 = 1410 bytes of data. On the wire a full packet takes 1500 + 14 (outer Ethernet) = 1514 bytes. Efficiency is 1410 / 1514 = 93.1 percent, against 1448 / 1514 = 95.6 percent without the tunnel: about 2.5 points of bandwidth, plus CPU work. For small packets (say 100 bytes of data) the 50 extra bytes are 33 percent more on the wire.
What happens if the MTU is wrong? If a pod interface keeps MTU 1500 and a pod sends a full-size 1500-byte packet, the encapsulated size would be 1550. The node cannot send it as one packet. If the DF bit is set (TCP normally sets it), the node should return ICMP "fragmentation needed", and TCP would shrink its segments; if that ICMP is lost to a firewall, large packets vanish silently, small requests succeed and large responses hang. This "works for ping, hangs for large downloads" pattern is the classic symptom of a tunnel MTU error.
Now a site-to-site VPN with WireGuard. Office A has the network 10.1.0.0/16 and a gateway with tunnel address 10.99.0.1; office B has 10.2.0.0/16 and gateway 10.99.0.2 at the public address 198.51.100.20. On gateway A:
```text
[Interface]
PrivateKey = <A private key>
Address = 10.99.0.1/24
ListenPort = 51820

[Peer]
PublicKey = <B public key>
Endpoint = 198.51.100.20:51820
AllowedIPs = 10.99.0.2/32, 10.2.0.0/16
PersistentKeepalive = 25
```
`AllowedIPs` does two jobs: packets to 10.2.0.0/16 are routed into the tunnel toward B, and packets that arrive from B are accepted only if their inner source is in that range, so B cannot inject traffic that claims to come from elsewhere. The keepalive every 25 seconds keeps the NAT mapping alive if A is behind a NAT. Workflow: a host in A pings 10.2.0.9; the gateway encrypts the ICMP packet with the session key, sends a 60-bytes-larger UDP packet to 198.51.100.20:51820; B decrypts, checks the tag and the allowed range, and forwards. About one round trip is spent on the first packet for the handshake; then data flows.
intuition: A sealed envelope inside a courier's package: the courier handles only the outer label, and only the person at the other end opens it, so the inner address, the sender and even the existence of other letters are invisible to the couriers along the way.
Encapsulation works because networks only look at the outermost header of a packet; whatever is inside is payload. That means you can build a new network on top of an old one, with its own addresses and rules, just by agreeing at two endpoints on how to wrap and unwrap. Adding encryption and an authentication tag to the wrapping turns the untrusted network between them into a pipe that cannot be read or altered. The design is the same idea as layering itself, used recursively: a layer's payload can contain a full stack of another network.
mistakes: People often think a VPN makes you anonymous. In fact a VPN moves trust: your traffic is hidden from the local network, but visible to the VPN operator, and the sites you visit still see you through accounts, cookies and fingerprints. A corporate VPN protects the path to the company, not your identity.
People often think a tunnel is the same as encryption. In fact encapsulation alone provides no secrecy: GRE, IP-in-IP and VXLAN carry the inner packet in clear text. Encryption must be added (IPsec, WireGuard, or an encrypted overlay mode). Many "private" overlays between data centres are unencrypted unless configured.
People often think IPsec and WireGuard are interchangeable, and that the newer one is simply better. In fact IPsec is a standard with wide interoperability (every router and cloud gateway speaks it), many options and long support in hardware; WireGuard is far simpler and faster in software but has fewer options (one fixed cipher suite, static peers) and less vendor support, and its handling of dynamic address allocation or user identity needs extra tooling.
People often think the overlay costs only a few CPU cycles. In fact encapsulation changes packet sizes (MTU trouble), hides inner flows from underlay load balancers (unless the source port is hashed, as in VXLAN), disables some NIC offloads, and makes debugging harder, because a packet capture on the wire shows only host-to-host UDP.
People often think the MTU is just a number you set once. In fact every layer of encapsulation subtracts from it, and paths that include several tunnels (VPN over VXLAN over a cloud overlay) can lose 100 bytes or more, breaking large transfers in ways that look like random application bugs.
friction: MTU and fragmentation problems are the most common operational pain, and they show up as hangs rather than clean errors. Another is that overlays hide topology: troubleshooting needs tools in both layers (a capture on the overlay interface, one on the underlay), and a flow can fail at a VTEP mapping, a security group, the underlay or the MTU. Performance varies: software encapsulation can cut throughput by tens of percent without offloads, and packet-per-second limits matter more than bits per second for small packets.
Scale is another limit. Overlays need a control plane that maps inner addresses to endpoints: the table of MAC and IP entries grows with the number of endpoints, flooding does not scale, and control-plane convergence after a failure takes time. Kubernetes clusters with thousands of nodes avoid overlays or use native routing (BGP, cloud routes) to remove the encapsulation tax and keep packets visible to the network.
Security has its own problems. Key management is the hard part of a VPN: rotating keys, revoking a lost device, and handling thousands of peers. WireGuard has no built-in user identity, so teams add a coordination layer. Long-lived pre-shared keys on IPsec gateways are a risk, and bugs in complicated IKE implementations have been exploited. Encryption hides traffic from the network but also from firewalls and intrusion detection systems on the path, which then must move to the endpoints. Quantum computers could break today's key exchanges, so WireGuard offers an optional pre-shared key layer, and IKEv2 has post-quantum extensions (RFC 9370, 2023).
NAT and firewalls make the connection itself hard: peers behind NATs need hole punching or a relay, and some networks block UDP or non-standard ports, which pushes users toward TCP-based VPNs with worse performance (TCP over TCP can collapse under packet loss).
tradeoffs: Choose WireGuard when you control both ends, want a simple configuration, high throughput in software and low latency of connection setup, and a fixed modern cipher suite is acceptable: site-to-site links, server meshes, personal and developer access (often via Tailscale or similar). You give up broad vendor interoperability and built-in user management.
Choose IPsec (IKEv2) when you must interoperate with other vendors' gateways, cloud VPN services and compliance-driven environments, or need hardware-accelerated crypto at line rate on network appliances. You accept a more complex configuration and a larger attack surface.
Choose VXLAN or Geneve when you need isolated virtual segments on a shared fabric, and want the underlay to remain a plain routed IP network: you pay 50 bytes of overhead, an MTU adjustment, and a control plane. Choose native routing (no overlay: cloud VPC routes, BGP to the pods) when the network can learn pod addresses, because you save the overhead and gain visibility; you give up independence from the underlay's address plan.
Choose an encrypted overlay (Cilium or Calico with WireGuard, IPsec) for node-to-node traffic that crosses untrusted links or must meet compliance rules; accept a few percent of throughput and extra CPU. Choose application-level encryption (mutual TLS in a service mesh) when you need identity per service rather than per node, at higher per-connection cost.
Choose a TCP- or HTTPS-based VPN (OpenVPN over TCP, MASQUE over HTTP/3) only when UDP is blocked; you accept worse behaviour under loss.
practice: Plan addressing first: overlapping private ranges are the cause of most hybrid-network trouble. Keep clusters, VPCs and offices on distinct ranges (for example from 10.0.0.0/8 slices), even if you do not connect them yet. Decide the MTU early: write down the underlay MTU, subtract the overhead of each layer, set the interface MTU explicitly, and clamp TCP MSS at tunnel edges. Test with large packets: `ping -M do -s 1400 <host>` on Linux sets DF and shows where the limit is.
For debugging, capture on both layers: on the tunnel interface (`tcpdump -i wg0`) to see the inner traffic, on the physical interface (`tcpdump -ni eth0 udp port 4789` or `port 51820`) to see the outer. Check `wg show` for the latest handshake time and transfer counters, `ip -d link show` for VXLAN settings, and `bridge fdb show` for VTEP mappings. A handshake that never completes usually means a blocked UDP port, a wrong public key, or a NAT without keepalive.
In Kubernetes, choose the networking mode deliberately: use the cloud's native routing or a BGP mode if available, and the overlay if you need to run anywhere. Turn on encryption (WireGuard mode in Cilium or Calico) when pod traffic crosses untrusted networks, and check throughput with a tool such as iperf3 between pods on different nodes, comparing with node-to-node.
For remote access, prefer short-lived device keys or certificates, centralised revocation, and least-privilege `AllowedIPs` or firewall rules so a VPN does not give access to the whole network. Rotate keys, monitor the handshake age, and keep gateway software patched, since VPN gateways are exposed to the internet and frequently targeted.
quiz: What is encapsulation, and why does it work? || It places a complete packet as the payload of another packet with a new outer header. It works because each network layer treats the payload as opaque bytes, so routers forward by the outer header and the far endpoint removes it.
Why does VXLAN derive the outer UDP source port from a hash of the inner flow? || Underlay switches spread traffic across equal-cost paths using the 5-tuple. All VXLAN traffic between two hosts would otherwise share one 5-tuple and one path; hashing the inner flow into the source port gives the underlay entropy to balance flows.
What does encryption plus an authentication tag (AEAD) add over encryption alone in a VPN? || It lets the receiver detect any modification or forged packet and drop it, and with sequence numbers it blocks replays. Encryption alone hides content but does not stop tampering.
Underlay MTU 1500, VXLAN over IPv4. What is the largest inner IP packet, and the TCP MSS inside? || The overhead is 50 bytes (outer IP 20, UDP 8, VXLAN 8, inner Ethernet 14), so the inner IP MTU is 1450 and the TCP MSS is 1450 − 40 = 1410 bytes.
When do you choose native routing over an overlay for a Kubernetes cluster? || When the underlay can route pod addresses (cloud route tables, BGP). You avoid the 50-byte overhead, lost offloads and hidden flows, and gain visibility, but you depend on the network's address plan and capacity.
interview: A tunnel works for ping and small requests but large downloads hang. What is wrong and how do you confirm? || Probably an MTU or path-MTU-discovery problem: the encapsulated large packets exceed the underlay MTU and are dropped, and the ICMP error is blocked. Confirm with `ping -M do -s <size>` to find the largest working size, compare with the interface MTU, and fix by lowering MTU, clamping MSS, or enabling jumbo frames in the underlay.
Compare IPsec, WireGuard and TLS-based VPNs on design and operations. || IPsec works at the IP layer with IKE negotiation, broad interoperability and hardware offload, but is complex. WireGuard is a minimal UDP protocol with fixed modern cryptography, static peer keys, a one-round-trip handshake and small code; it is simple and fast but has less flexibility. TLS-based VPNs run over TCP or UDP, pass firewalls easily, and integrate with existing identity systems, with more overhead.
How do pods on different Kubernetes nodes communicate in an overlay, and where does the mapping come from? || The sending node routes the pod subnet's traffic to a VXLAN or Geneve interface, which wraps the packet in UDP to the destination node's address and a VNI; the receiving node unwraps it. The mapping of pod subnet to node comes from the cluster state (the API server and CNI agents), or from BGP in routed designs.
What is "cryptokey routing" in WireGuard? || Each peer is identified by its public key and has an `AllowedIPs` list. For outgoing packets, the destination address selects the peer (and thus the key); for incoming packets, the decrypted inner source address must be inside that peer's `AllowedIPs`. It merges routing and access control in one table.
Why might you not want an overlay between all nodes of a very large cluster? || Encapsulation costs 50 bytes per packet, forces MTU changes, reduces NIC offload benefit, and needs a control plane that maps every endpoint. It hides flows from the network, which complicates debugging and load balancing. Native routing removes these costs if the fabric can learn pod prefixes.
